/*
 * profiles.en.js — English country profiles for the Capitals quiz.
 *
 * Shown under "Did you know?" after each answer, in the "Country profile"
 * section: what the country is known for, how its economy works, business and
 * trade, tourism and a short history. Keyed by the exact English country name
 * used in countries.js. Loaded lazily by the page (ensureProfiles), only for
 * the language on screen, so it never slows the first question.
 *
 * Written and then independently fact-checked in September 2026. Keep it
 * timeless: no living office-holders, no figures that go stale; prefer "about
 * a third" to a precise statistic. No HTML (the page inserts it as text).
 */
window.CAPITALS_PROFILES_EN = {
  "Albania": {
    known: "Albania is known for its rugged mountains, its sunny coast on the Adriatic and Ionian seas and a strong tradition of hospitality rooted in besa, a code of honour meaning a promise that must be kept. Albanians call their country Shqipëria, often translated as 'land of the eagles', and the famous nun Mother Teresa was of Albanian descent.",
    economy: "Albania's economy is now mostly based on services, especially tourism, trade and construction, which have grown quickly since the end of communism. Farming still employs many people, growing olives, grapes, vegetables and medicinal herbs such as sage, and the country mines chromium and pumps some oil. Almost all the electricity it produces comes from hydropower, and money sent home by Albanians working abroad remains an important source of income.",
    business: "Albania's main exports are clothing and shoes, many of them made for Italian and other European brands, along with chromium ore, oil, electricity and farm products. Italy is by far its biggest trading partner, and Greece, Germany, Turkey and China are also important. The currency is the lek, the port of Durrës is the country's busiest, and Albania belongs to the Central European Free Trade Agreement with other Balkan countries.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the beaches of the Albanian Riviera, where the Ionian Sea meets steep mountains, and for hiking in the Albanian Alps around Theth and Valbona. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the ancient city of Butrint and the Ottoman-era towns of Berat, 'the city of a thousand windows', and Gjirokastër, 'the city of stone'. Food lovers try byrek, a savoury pastry, and tavë kosi, lamb baked with yoghurt.",
    history: "In ancient times the land was home to the Illyrians, and it later came under Roman and Byzantine rule and then Ottoman rule, which lasted more than four centuries despite a famous revolt led by Skanderbeg in the 1400s. Albania declared independence from the Ottoman Empire in 1912. After decades of strict communist rule it became a multi-party democracy in the early 1990s, and it joined NATO in 2009."
  },
  "Andorra": {
    known: "Andorra is known as a small, wealthy mountain country between France and Spain, famous for low taxes and for one of the longest life expectancies in the world. It is the only country where Catalan is the sole official language, and it has no airport or railway of its own, so almost all visitors arrive by road.",
    economy: "Andorra's economy is almost entirely based on services. Tourism is by far the largest part, together with the shops and hotels that serve millions of visitors a year, and banking and finance are also important. Farming is small because so little land is flat, though tobacco has long been grown in the valleys and sheep and cattle graze the high pastures.",
    business: "Andorra is not a member of the European Union, but it uses the euro under an agreement with the EU and has a customs union with it for manufactured goods. Most of its trade is with Spain and France, as nearly everything sold in the country is imported. Low taxes and a banking sector led by local groups such as Andbank, Crèdit Andorrà and MoraBanc attract businesses and wealthy residents from abroad.",
    tourism: "In winter visitors ski at large resorts such as Grandvalira, and in summer they hike and cycle through high valleys, including the Madriu-Perafita-Claror Valley, a UNESCO World Heritage site of glacial scenery, stone huts and old mountain paths. Other draws are small Romanesque stone churches and the Caldea thermal spa. Visitors also enjoy hearty mountain food such as trinxat, made from cabbage, potato and bacon.",
    history: "Legend links Andorra's founding to Charlemagne, but its unusual system of two co-princes dates from an agreement signed in 1278 between the Bishop of Urgell and the Count of Foix, whose rights later passed to the French head of state. For centuries it was a poor, isolated land of farmers and shepherds. In 1993 Andorrans approved their first written constitution, and the country joined the United Nations the same year."
  },
  "Austria": {
    known: "Austria is known for the snowy peaks of the Alps, which cover much of the country, and for world-class skiing and hiking. It is also famous for the white Lipizzaner horses of the Spanish Riding School in Vienna, for Wiener schnitzel and Sachertorte chocolate cake, and for the film The Sound of Music, set in and around Salzburg.",
    economy: "Austria has a wealthy, mainly service-based economy in which tourism is especially important in the Alpine regions. Industry is also strong, making machinery, steel, vehicle parts, chemicals and wood products, and many medium-sized family firms lead world markets in specialised products. Farms are small, but about a quarter of farmland is organic, one of the highest shares in Europe, and hydropower supplies most of the country's electricity.",
    business: "Austria's main exports are machinery, vehicles and parts, medicines, metals, and food and drink, and Germany is by far its largest trading partner. Well-known Austrian brands include Red Bull, Swarovski crystal and KTM motorcycles, and the Mercedes-Benz G-Class off-roader is built in Graz. Austria uses the euro, has been in the European Union since 1995, and Vienna is a base for many firms doing business in Central and Eastern Europe.",
    tourism: "Visitors ski or hike in Tyrol and other Alpine regions and drive the Grossglockner High Alpine Road below Austria's highest mountain. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the old town of Salzburg, the lakeside village of Hallstatt, the vineyard-lined Wachau valley on the Danube and the Semmering Railway, one of the first mountain railways. Christmas markets, the Salzburg Festival of music and theatre, and apple strudel are other favourites.",
    history: "Celts and Romans lived here before the Habsburg family made Vienna the centre of a vast empire, which collapsed at the end of the First World War in 1918, leaving Austria a small republic. Nazi Germany annexed it in 1938. After the Second World War it was occupied by the Allies until 1955, when it regained full independence and declared permanent neutrality."
  },
  "Belarus": {
    known: "Belarus is known for its flat landscape of wetlands and thousands of lakes, and for its love of potatoes, which star in dishes such as draniki, crispy potato pancakes. The painter Marc Chagall was born near Vitebsk, and Belarusian and Russian are both official languages, though most people speak Russian at home.",
    economy: "Belarus has a heavily industrialised economy, although services make up the largest share. Large state-owned companies still play the main role, much as in Soviet times. Factories build tractors, trucks and giant mining dump trucks, refineries process crude oil bought from Russia, and huge mines produce potash for fertiliser. Farming is also important, especially potatoes, grain, flax, milk and meat.",
    business: "Belarus's main exports are potash fertiliser, fuels made from oil, machinery and vehicles, and dairy and meat products. Russia is by far its main trading partner, and trade with China has grown. Well-known makers include BelAZ, whose mining trucks are among the largest in the world, and the Minsk Tractor Works; the currency is the Belarusian rouble, and Belarus belongs to the Eurasian Economic Union.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the castles of Mir and Nesvizh, once owned by the powerful Radziwiłł family and now UNESCO World Heritage sites, and for the Brest Fortress, where a huge memorial honours its defence against the German invasion of 1941. Nature lovers explore the Braslav Lakes and the marshes of the Pripyat region, and the city of Vitebsk hosts the Slavianski Bazaar music festival every summer.",
    history: "Much of medieval Belarus belonged to the Principality of Polotsk and later the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, then passed to the Russian Empire in the late 1700s. As a Soviet republic it suffered huge losses in the Second World War and received much of the radioactive fallout from the 1986 Chernobyl disaster. It became independent when the Soviet Union broke up in 1991, and large protests followed the disputed presidential election of 2020."
  },
  "Belgium": {
    known: "Belgium is known for comic strips such as Tintin and the Smurfs, for crispy fries served with mayonnaise and for a deep passion for cycling. It is a small country with three official languages, Dutch, French and German, and the port city of Antwerp has long been one of the world's great centres of the diamond trade.",
    economy: "Belgium has a wealthy economy based mainly on services, including finance, trade and logistics, helped by its central position between large European markets. Industry remains important, especially chemicals, medicines and food processing, and the port of Antwerp is surrounded by one of the largest chemical clusters in the world. Farming is small but intensive, and Belgium is one of the world's biggest exporters of frozen fries.",
    business: "Belgium exports medicines and vaccines, chemicals and plastics, cars, diamonds, and food and drink, trading mostly with its neighbours Germany, the Netherlands and France. It is home to AB InBev, one of the world's largest brewers, and the port of Antwerp-Bruges is among Europe's busiest. Belgium uses the euro, was a founding member of what became the European Union, and forms the Benelux union with the Netherlands and Luxembourg.",
    tourism: "Visitors wander the canals and medieval streets of Bruges and Ghent, admire the Atomium in Brussels and visit the First World War battlefields and cemeteries around Ypres. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the historic centre of Bruges, the Grand-Place in Brussels and the Art Nouveau houses of the architect Victor Horta. The Carnival of Binche is famous for its dancers in wax masks.",
    history: "In the Middle Ages, Flemish cities such as Bruges and Ghent grew rich from the cloth trade, and the land was later ruled by Spain, Austria, France and the Netherlands in turn. Belgium won independence from the Netherlands in 1830, and its King Leopold II later ruled the Congo as his private colony with great brutality; the Congo then stayed under Belgian rule until 1960. Germany occupied Belgium in both World Wars."
  },
  "Bosnia and Herzegovina": {
    known: "Bosnia and Herzegovina is known for its green mountains and fast rivers, for towns that blend Ottoman and Austro-Hungarian styles, and for grilled ćevapi served in flatbread. Sarajevo hosted the 1984 Winter Olympics. The country is home to three main peoples, Bosniaks, Serbs and Croats, and touches the sea only along a short stretch of coast at Neum.",
    economy: "Bosnia and Herzegovina has a mixed economy in which services are the largest part, but industry still matters, including metals, wood and furniture, and electricity from coal and hydropower, some of which is sold abroad. Farmers grow fruit such as plums and raspberries and raise livestock. Money sent home by the many Bosnians living abroad is an important source of income for families.",
    business: "Bosnia and Herzegovina's main exports include electricity, furniture and wood products, metals, shoes and clothing, sold mainly to neighbouring Croatia and Serbia and to EU countries such as Germany, Slovenia and Austria. The currency is the convertible mark, which is tied to the euro at a fixed rate. The country belongs to the Central European Free Trade Agreement, and Sarajevo is the main centre for banking and business.",
    tourism: "Visitors explore Sarajevo's Ottoman-era bazaar, Baščaršija, raft down clear rivers such as the Una and Neretva, and admire the Kravica waterfalls. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the Old Bridge area of Mostar, where local divers leap into the river far below, the Mehmed Paša Sokolović Bridge in Višegrad and medieval carved tombstones called stećci. The Sarajevo Film Festival draws big crowds every summer.",
    history: "A medieval Bosnian kingdom fell to the Ottomans in 1463, and many people converted to Islam during four centuries of Ottoman rule, before Austria-Hungary took control in 1878. Independence from Yugoslavia in 1992 was followed by a war until 1995, including the siege of Sarajevo and the Srebrenica massacre, which international courts ruled a genocide. The Dayton Agreement ended the fighting and created a state made up of two self-governing entities."
  },
  "Bulgaria": {
    known: "Bulgaria is known for its Rose Valley, which produces much of the world's rose oil for perfumes, and for yoghurt made with a bacterium named after the country, Lactobacillus bulgaricus. Bulgarians traditionally nod their heads to mean no and shake them to mean yes, and the country's haunting folk choirs are famous far beyond its borders.",
    economy: "Bulgaria has a mainly service-based economy, with tourism on the Black Sea coast and in the mountains playing a big part, and a fast-growing IT and outsourcing sector in Sofia. Industry includes copper refining, machinery, food processing and energy, including a nuclear power plant at Kozloduy on the Danube. Farmers grow wheat, maize and sunflowers, and Bulgaria is one of the world's leading producers of lavender oil.",
    business: "Bulgaria's main exports include refined copper, fuels, machinery and electrical equipment, grain and sunflower seeds, medicines and clothing. Germany, Romania, Italy, Turkey and Greece are among its biggest trading partners. The country adopted the euro in 2026, replacing its old currency, the lev, and low taxes have drawn foreign factories to industrial zones around Plovdiv.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Black Sea beaches, skiing in Bansko and Borovets, and the old towns of Plovdiv, with its Roman theatre, and Veliko Tarnovo, the medieval capital. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the Rila Monastery, the ancient town of Nessebar and the painted Thracian Tomb of Kazanlak. Food lovers try shopska salad topped with white cheese and banitsa, a flaky cheese pastry.",
    history: "The ancient Thracians, famous for their gold treasures, lived here long before the Bulgars founded a state in 681. After nearly five centuries of Ottoman rule, Bulgaria won self-rule in 1878 after a war between Russia and the Ottoman Empire, and declared full independence from the Ottomans in 1908. It was a communist state allied to the Soviet Union until 1989, then joined NATO in 2004 and the European Union in 2007."
  },
  "Croatia": {
    known: "Croatia is known for its crystal-clear sea and for giving the world the necktie, which comes from scarves worn by Croatian soldiers in the 1600s, and the Dalmatian dog, named after its coastal region of Dalmatia. The inventor Nikola Tesla was born in what is now Croatia, and its football team reached the World Cup final in 2018.",
    economy: "Croatia has a service-based economy in which tourism is especially important, bringing in a larger share of national income than in most European countries. Industry includes food processing, medicines, electrical equipment, shipbuilding and oil refining. Farmers grow maize and wheat on the fertile plains of Slavonia in the east, while olives, wine and fishing matter along the coast.",
    business: "Croatia exports electrical machinery, medicines, fuels, wood and food, trading most with Italy, Germany, Slovenia, Hungary and neighbouring Bosnia and Herzegovina. Well-known companies include Podravka, maker of the Vegeta seasoning, the drug maker Pliva and Rimac, known for its electric supercars. Croatia joined the European Union in 2013 and in 2023 replaced its currency, the kuna, with the euro, the same year it entered the Schengen area of passport-free travel.",
    tourism: "Visitors sail between islands such as Hvar and Brač, admire the waterfalls of Krka and walk the wooden paths of Plitvice Lakes National Park, a UNESCO World Heritage site. Other UNESCO sites include Diocletian's Palace in Split, a Roman emperor's home that is now part of the old town, and the historic town of Trogir. Istria is known for truffles, olive oil and wine.",
    history: "Croats settled here in the 600s and formed a kingdom in the 900s, which later entered a union with Hungary, while Venice ruled much of the coast for centuries and Dubrovnik thrived as an independent trading republic. After the First World War Croatia became part of Yugoslavia. It declared independence from Yugoslavia in 1991, which was followed by a war that lasted until 1995, and it joined NATO in 2009."
  },
  "Czechia": {
    known: "Czechia is known for Bohemian crystal and glass, for grand spa towns such as Karlovy Vary and for its love of ice hockey. The word 'robot' comes from a Czech play written by Karel Čapek in 1920, soft contact lenses were invented by a Czech chemist, and the writer Franz Kafka was born in Prague.",
    economy: "Czechia has one of the most industrial economies in Europe, although services still make up the largest share. Car making is the biggest industry, and the country builds more cars per person than almost any other, along with machinery, electronics and glass. Farmers grow wheat, barley and rapeseed, as well as the famous Saaz hops used to brew beer around the world.",
    business: "Cars and car parts, machinery and electronics make up most of Czechia's exports, and Germany is by far its biggest trading partner, taking about a third of them. Famous Czech brands include Škoda cars, Tatra trucks and Budweiser Budvar beer, and the Bata shoe company began in the town of Zlín. Czechia joined the European Union in 2004 but still uses its own currency, the Czech koruna, or crown.",
    tourism: "Visitors cross the Charles Bridge to Prague Castle, explore the fairy-tale town of Český Krumlov and visit the old silver-mining town of Kutná Hora, famous for a nearby chapel decorated with human bones; the historic centres of all three are UNESCO World Heritage sites. Hikers head for the sandstone arches of Bohemian Switzerland National Park, and hungry travellers try svíčková, beef in a creamy sauce with dumplings.",
    history: "In the 1300s Prague was the capital of the Holy Roman Empire under Charles IV, who founded the oldest university in Central Europe there. The Czech lands later came under Habsburg rule, and Czechoslovakia was founded in 1918 as Austria-Hungary collapsed. After Nazi occupation and four decades of communist rule, including the crushing of the Prague Spring in 1968, the Velvet Revolution of 1989 brought democracy, and Czechia and Slovakia split peacefully in 1993."
  },
  "Denmark": {
    known: "Denmark is known for hygge, the Danish idea of cosy, relaxed time with friends, for clean modern design in furniture and lamps, and for its Viking past. It is a world pioneer in wind power, and the Kingdom of Denmark also includes Greenland and the Faroe Islands, which govern most of their own affairs.",
    economy: "Denmark has a wealthy, service-based economy with high taxes that pay for free healthcare and education. Its leading industries include medicines, wind turbines, shipping and food. Well over half of the land is farmed, and Danish farms are known for efficient, high-tech methods and for producing large amounts of pork and dairy products.",
    business: "Denmark's biggest export is medicines, led by Novo Nordisk, known for its diabetes and weight-loss drugs, and it also sells machinery, pork and dairy products, especially to Germany, Sweden, the United States and the United Kingdom. Other famous Danish firms include the shipping giant Maersk, the brewer Carlsberg, the wind-turbine maker Vestas and the jewellery brand Pandora. Denmark joined the European Community, today's EU, in 1973 but keeps its own currency, the krone.",
    tourism: "Visitors enjoy Copenhagen's Tivoli Gardens, one of the world's oldest amusement parks, and the colourful harbour of Nyhavn. UNESCO World Heritage sites include Kronborg Castle, the setting of Shakespeare's Hamlet, the Viking-age rune stones at Jelling and the tidal flats of the Wadden Sea. Other highlights are the northern tip at Skagen, where two seas meet, the Roskilde music festival and smørrebrød, open sandwiches on dark rye bread.",
    history: "Danish Vikings raided and settled across northern Europe, and in the 1000s King Cnut ruled Denmark, England and Norway; in 1397 the Kalmar Union joined Denmark, Norway and Sweden under one crown, but Denmark later lost Sweden and, in 1814, Norway. Germany occupied Denmark in the Second World War, and in 1943 Danes helped most of the country's Jews escape by boat to Sweden. Denmark was a founding member of NATO in 1949."
  },
  "Estonia": {
    known: "Estonia is known for its huge song festivals, where tens of thousands of singers perform together, and for a landscape of forests, bogs and islands, with forest covering about half the country. It is also known for 'e-residency', which lets people from other countries set up and run an Estonian company online.",
    economy: "Estonia has a modern, mainly service-based economy, with information technology, logistics and finance among its strongest sectors. Its forests support a large timber, wood and furniture industry, and factories make electronics and machinery. For decades most of its electricity came from burning oil shale, a rock mined in the north-east, although the country is now shifting towards wind and solar power.",
    business: "Estonia exports electronics, wood and furniture, machinery and fuels, and its main trading partners include Finland, Sweden, Germany and its Baltic neighbours Latvia and Lithuania. Tallinn has a lively start-up scene: the ride-hailing company Bolt is based there, and the money-transfer firm Wise was founded by Estonians. In 2011 Estonia became the first former Soviet republic to adopt the euro.",
    tourism: "Beyond Tallinn, visitors walk boardwalks across peat bogs in Lahemaa and Soomaa national parks, explore the windmills and medieval castle of Saaremaa island, and relax in the beach resort of Pärnu. The university city of Tartu is lively with students. The smoke sauna tradition of south-east Estonia is on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage, and dark rye bread is served with almost every meal.",
    history: "In the 1200s German and Danish crusading knights conquered the land, which was later ruled by Sweden and then the Russian Empire. Estonia declared independence from Russia in 1918, but the Soviet Union occupied it in 1940 and, after a German occupation, again in 1944. The peaceful 'Singing Revolution', in which huge crowds sang banned national songs, helped it regain independence in 1991, and it joined the EU and NATO in 2004."
  },
  "Finland": {
    known: "Finland is known as the land of a thousand lakes, although it really has nearly 190,000, and for forests that cover about three quarters of the country. It is also famous for its highly regarded schools, for the Moomins created by the writer Tove Jansson, and for Lapland, where visitors seek the northern lights and Santa Claus Village in Rovaniemi.",
    economy: "Finland has a high-tech, service-based economy with a strong industrial base. Its forests supply a large paper, pulp and timber industry, and other key industries include metals, machinery, telecommunications equipment and shipbuilding, including some of the world's largest cruise ships. Farming is limited by the cold climate but produces oats, barley and dairy products, and nuclear power stations supply a large part of the country's electricity.",
    business: "Finland exports paper, pulp and wood products, machinery, metals and electronics, trading most with Germany, Sweden, the United States and the Netherlands. Famous Finnish companies include Nokia, the lift maker Kone, the ship-engine maker Wärtsilä, the design brands Marimekko and Fiskars, and games studios such as Supercell and Rovio, maker of Angry Birds. Finland joined the EU in 1995 and is the only Nordic country that uses the euro.",
    tourism: "In winter visitors ride husky or reindeer sleds in Lapland, and in summer they rent lakeside cottages in the Lakeland region around Lake Saimaa, home to a rare freshwater seal. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the Suomenlinna sea fortress off Helsinki, the wooden old town of Rauma and the Kvarken archipelago. Many visitors join in Midsummer bonfires and try Karelian pies, rye pastries filled with rice porridge.",
    history: "For roughly six hundred years Finland was part of Sweden, and in 1809 it became a self-governing grand duchy within the Russian Empire. It declared independence from Russia in 1917, and in the Winter War of 1939 to 1940 it held off a much larger Soviet invasion, though it had to give up part of Karelia. Finland stayed neutral during the Cold War and joined NATO in 2023."
  },
  "France": {
    known: "France is famous for fashion and perfume, for hundreds of different cheeses and for the Tour de France, the world's best-known cycling race. The motto of its revolution, 'Liberty, Equality, Fraternity', inspired democratic movements around the world, and for centuries French was the main language of diplomacy.",
    economy: "France has one of the world's largest economies, based mainly on services such as tourism, retail, banking and insurance. Its industries include aircraft and space technology, cars, medicines, chemicals and luxury goods, and nuclear power stations produce most of its electricity. France is also the biggest farm producer in the European Union, growing wheat, wine grapes and sugar beet and raising dairy and beef cattle.",
    business: "France's main exports include aircraft, machinery, cars, medicines, chemicals, cosmetics, and wine and spirits, and Germany is its largest trading partner. Famous names include Airbus, which assembles many planes in Toulouse, the luxury groups LVMH and Hermès, L'Oréal, Michelin tyres and the car brands Renault and Peugeot. France uses the euro, and La Défense, next to Paris, is one of Europe's largest business districts.",
    tourism: "Visitors come to see the Louvre and its Mona Lisa, as well as UNESCO World Heritage sites such as the Palace of Versailles, the abbey of Mont-Saint-Michel on its rocky tidal island and the banks of the Seine in Paris. Others head for Riviera beaches, skiing in the Alps below Mont Blanc and the lavender fields of Provence. Crowds gather for the Cannes Film Festival and Bastille Day on 14 July.",
    history: "Julius Caesar conquered Celtic Gaul in the first century BC, and later the Franks, who gave France its name, built a kingdom that Charlemagne expanded into an empire around 800. The Revolution of 1789 ended the absolute monarchy, and Napoleon then conquered much of Europe. France also built a large colonial empire in the Americas, Africa and Asia, was occupied by Germany from 1940 to 1944, and helped found what became the European Union."
  },
  "Germany": {
    known: "Germany is known for its engineering and carmakers, for motorways where some stretches have no general speed limit, and for great composers such as Bach and Beethoven. It is also famous for its Christmas markets, hundreds of kinds of bread, Munich's Oktoberfest beer festival and the Brothers Grimm, who collected fairy tales such as Snow White and Hansel and Gretel.",
    economy: "Germany has a large, export-driven economy in which services are the biggest part, but manufacturing is unusually strong for a rich country, led by cars, machinery, chemicals and electrical equipment. Much of it rests on thousands of medium-sized family firms, the Mittelstand. Farming is a small part of the economy but produces wheat, barley, potatoes, pork and dairy, and renewable energy supplies over half of its electricity; the last nuclear plants closed in 2023.",
    business: "Germany is one of the world's biggest exporters, selling cars, machinery, chemicals, medicines and electrical goods, and its largest trading partners include the United States, China, France, the Netherlands and Poland. World-famous German companies include Volkswagen, Mercedes-Benz, BMW, Siemens, Bosch, BASF, SAP and Adidas. A founding member of what became the EU, Germany uses the euro, which replaced the Deutsche Mark, and Frankfurt, home of the European Central Bank, is a major financial centre.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for fairy-tale castles such as Neuschwanstein, at the foot of the Bavarian Alps, medieval towns such as Rothenburg ob der Tauber, and hiking in the Black Forest. UNESCO World Heritage sites include Cologne Cathedral, the castle-lined Upper Middle Rhine Valley, Berlin's Museum Island and the old Hanseatic town of Lübeck. Food lovers try bratwurst sausages, soft pretzels and Black Forest gateau.",
    history: "For centuries the German lands were a patchwork of states within the Holy Roman Empire, and in 1517 Martin Luther started the Protestant Reformation there. Germany was first united in 1871, and in 1933, after defeat in the First World War, the Nazis took power; their dictatorship started the Second World War and murdered six million Jews in the Holocaust. Divided into West and East Germany after 1945, the country was reunited in 1990."
  },
  "Greece": {
    known: "Greece is known for its thousands of islands and islets, its sunny beaches and the whitewashed villages of the Cyclades, such as those on Santorini. It is also famous for the myths of gods such as Zeus and Athena, for the Greek alphabet, from which the Latin and Cyrillic alphabets developed, and for foods such as olive oil, feta cheese and yoghurt.",
    economy: "Greece has a service-based economy in which tourism is the biggest earner, bringing in tens of millions of visitors a year. Shipping is another pillar, as Greek shipowners control one of the largest merchant fleets in the world. Farming remains important in many regions, producing olives, cotton, fruit such as peaches and kiwis, and cheese, while industry includes food processing, oil refining, aluminium and medicines.",
    business: "Refined oil products are Greece's biggest export; others include food such as olive oil, olives, feta, fruit and farmed fish, as well as aluminium and medicines. Its main trading partners include Italy, Germany, Cyprus and neighbouring Bulgaria and Turkey. Greece joined what is now the European Union in 1981 and replaced the drachma with the euro in 2001, and the port of Piraeus, near Athens, is one of the busiest in the Mediterranean.",
    tourism: "Most visitors come for the sun and sea, hopping between islands such as Santorini, Mykonos, Crete, Rhodes and Corfu. UNESCO World Heritage sites include ancient Delphi and Olympia, the monasteries of Meteora perched on towering rock pillars and the medieval old town of Rhodes. Food is a highlight, from grilled souvlaki and moussaka to fresh fish by the sea, and Orthodox Easter is the most important festival of the year.",
    history: "The Minoans of Crete built one of Europe's first civilisations, and Alexander the Great spread Greek culture as far as India. Greece later became part of the Roman and Byzantine empires, then the Ottoman Empire, until a war of independence begun in 1821 led to a free Greek state in 1830. Modern Greece lived through occupation in the Second World War, a military dictatorship from 1967 to 1974 and a debt crisis after 2009."
  },
  "Hungary": {
    known: "Hungary is known for its paprika-spiced cooking, such as goulash, and for the Rubik's Cube, invented in Budapest in the 1970s. It has produced great composers such as Franz Liszt and Béla Bartók, brilliant scientists such as John von Neumann, and has long been one of the strongest nations in Olympic water polo and fencing.",
    economy: "Hungary has a mainly services-based economy, but industry plays a bigger role than in many European countries, above all car making. Foreign carmakers build vehicles and engines there, alongside factories for electronics, medicines and, more recently, batteries for electric cars. The fertile Great Plain grows wheat, maize and sunflowers, and farming and food processing remain important.",
    business: "Hungary sells cars, car parts, electronics and medicines abroad, and Germany is by far its biggest trading partner. Well-known Hungarian names include the oil company MOL, OTP Bank, the drug maker Gedeon Richter and Herend porcelain. The currency is the forint, and Hungary is a member of the European Union but not of the euro area.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Budapest's riverside Parliament and Castle Hill, and for Lake Balaton, the largest lake in Central Europe and a favourite summer escape. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the Tokaj wine region, the traditional village of Hollókő and the Hortobágy grasslands, home to horse herders and grey cattle. Food lovers try fried lángos bread and layered Dobos cake.",
    history: "Magyar tribes settled the Carpathian Basin in the late 800s, and Stephen I was crowned the first king around 1000. After about 150 years of partial Ottoman rule, Hungary passed to the Habsburgs and in 1867 became an equal partner in Austria-Hungary. It lost about two-thirds of its territory after the First World War, rose against Soviet control in 1956 and became a democracy in 1989."
  },
  "Iceland": {
    known: "Iceland is known as the land of fire and ice, where active volcanoes sit beside huge glaciers and steaming hot springs. The English word geyser comes from Geysir, a hot spring in Iceland, and the country is also famous for its medieval sagas, among the great works of early European literature.",
    economy: "Iceland has a services-based economy that rests on three pillars: fishing, aluminium and tourism. Fish and seafood have long been vital exports, while large smelters turn imported raw material into aluminium using cheap electricity. Tourism has grown into one of the biggest earners, and only a small share of the land can be farmed, mostly for hay and grazing sheep.",
    business: "Most of Iceland's trade is with Europe and North America, and its best-known companies include the airline Icelandair and makers of equipment for fishing and fish processing. The currency is the Icelandic króna. Iceland belongs to the European Economic Area and EFTA, so it takes part in the EU's single market without being a member; staying outside the EU lets it keep control of its rich fishing waters.",
    tourism: "Many visitors drive the Golden Circle, which links the Gullfoss waterfall, the hot springs of Geysir, where Strokkur shoots water skywards every few minutes, and Thingvellir, a UNESCO World Heritage site in a rift between two tectonic plates. Others hike on glaciers in Vatnajökull National Park, go whale watching or bathe in geothermal lagoons.",
    history: "Norse settlers, many from Norway, arrived from about 874 and in 930 founded the Althing, one of the world's oldest parliaments. Iceland later came under Norwegian and then Danish rule, gained self-rule in stages and became a fully independent republic in 1944. It clashed with Britain over fishing limits in the Cod Wars and suffered a severe banking collapse in 2008."
  },
  "Ireland": {
    known: "Ireland is known around the world for St Patrick's Day, celebrated on 17 March in cities far beyond its shores, and for its traditional music, dance and pubs. It has its own sports, hurling and Gaelic football, and tens of millions of people abroad, especially in the United States, have Irish ancestry.",
    economy: "Ireland has a modern economy led by services and high-tech industry. Many global technology and pharmaceutical companies have their European headquarters or large factories there, attracted partly by low company taxes and an English-speaking workforce. Farming is smaller but still important: rich grassland feeds large herds of cattle, so beef and dairy products are major exports.",
    business: "Ireland's biggest exports are medicines, medical devices and computer services, sold mainly to the United States, EU countries and the United Kingdom. Famous Irish brands include Kerrygold butter, Jameson whiskey and the airline Ryanair. Ireland joined what is now the European Union in 1973 and uses the euro, and Dublin is a major hub for international finance and technology.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the dramatic west coast, from the Cliffs of Moher to the Ring of Kerry, linked by the Wild Atlantic Way coastal route. UNESCO World Heritage sites include Brú na Bóinne, home to the Newgrange passage tomb, older than Stonehenge, and Skellig Michael, a rocky island with an early Christian monastery. Traditional music sessions in pubs, Irish stew and soda bread complete a trip.",
    history: "Christianity arrived in the 400s, traditionally with St Patrick, and Irish monasteries became centres of learning. English and later British control grew from the Norman invasion of 1169. The Great Famine of the 1840s killed about a million people and forced another million to emigrate. After a war of independence, most of the island became self-governing in 1922 and a republic in 1949, while Northern Ireland stayed in the United Kingdom."
  },
  "Italy": {
    known: "Italy is famous for its food, from pizza and pasta to gelato and espresso, and for opera, which began there around 1600. It is also known for style and speed: Milan is a world fashion capital, and Italian sports cars such as Ferrari and Lamborghini are admired everywhere. Football is a national passion.",
    economy: "Italy has one of Europe's largest economies, based mainly on services but with a strong manufacturing sector, especially machinery, vehicles, clothing, furniture and food. Many firms are small family businesses. The industrial north is much richer than the south, where growing olives, grapes, citrus fruit and tomatoes matters more, and tourism is a major earner across the country.",
    business: "Italy exports machinery, cars, medicines, fashion and food, trading most with Germany, France and the United States. World-famous brands include Ferrari, Fiat, Gucci, Prada and Ferrero, maker of Nutella. Italy uses the euro, was a founding member of the European Economic Community in 1957 and belongs to the G7 group of major economies, and Milan is its financial and business centre.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Colosseum in Rome, the canals of Venice, the art of Florence and the leaning tower of Pisa. The buried Roman city of Pompeii, the cliffside villages of the Amalfi Coast and the jagged Dolomite mountains are all UNESCO World Heritage sites. Venice's masked carnival and Siena's horse race, the Palio, draw big crowds.",
    history: "Before Rome rose to power, the Etruscans and Greek colonists shaped the peninsula. After the Western Roman Empire fell in 476, Italy was divided for centuries among city-states, kingdoms and the Papal States. It was united as a kingdom in 1861, with help from Giuseppe Garibaldi, lived under Mussolini's Fascist dictatorship from 1922, and voted to become a republic in 1946."
  },
  "Kosovo": {
    known: "Kosovo is best known abroad for the debate over its status: its independence is recognised by many countries, but not by Serbia or by several others, including some EU members. Most of its people are ethnic Albanians, with a Serb minority. It is also known for its judo champions, who won its first Olympic gold medal in 2016, and for international pop stars with Kosovar roots.",
    economy: "Kosovo has a small, mainly services-based economy that relies heavily on remittances, money sent home by the many Kosovars working abroad, especially in Germany and Switzerland. It has large reserves of lignite, a soft brown coal that generates most of its electricity, and mines for lead and zinc. Small family farms grow wheat, maize, peppers and raspberries.",
    business: "Kosovo buys far more from abroad than it sells. Its exports include metals, plastics, furniture and food, going mainly to nearby Albania and North Macedonia and to EU countries such as Germany. Although it is not in the European Union or the euro area, Kosovo uses the euro as its currency, and it takes part in the CEFTA free-trade agreement with other Balkan economies.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Prizren, a riverside old town with an Ottoman stone bridge, mosques and a hilltop fortress, which hosts the DokuFest documentary film festival each summer. Medieval Serbian Orthodox monasteries such as Visoki Dečani and Gračanica, famous for their frescoes, are UNESCO World Heritage sites. Hikers head for the Rugova Canyon and the Accursed Mountains near the borders with Albania and Montenegro.",
    history: "Kosovo was a heartland of medieval Serbia, and the Battle of Kosovo in 1389 opened the way to over four centuries of Ottoman rule. Taken mostly by Serbia in 1912, it later became part of Yugoslavia. After a war in 1998 and 1999 between Yugoslav and Serbian forces and ethnic Albanian fighters, ended by NATO air strikes, it was run by the United Nations until its 2008 declaration of independence."
  },
  "Latvia": {
    known: "Latvia is known for its love of singing: its Song and Dance Festival, held about every five years, brings together tens of thousands of performers and is part of a Baltic tradition recognised by UNESCO. It is also famous for Jāņi, its midsummer festival, for Riga Black Balsam, a bitter herbal liqueur, and for its passion for ice hockey.",
    economy: "Latvia has a mainly services-based economy in which transport and logistics are important, thanks to its Baltic Sea ports and rail links. Its forests supply a large wood-processing industry, and timber products are among its biggest exports. Farms grow wheat and other grains and raise dairy cattle, while other industries include food processing, medicines and electronics.",
    business: "Latvia exports wood products, electrical equipment, machinery, grain and drinks, trading mainly with its Baltic neighbours Lithuania and Estonia and with other EU countries. Well-known names include the airline airBaltic and Laima chocolates. Latvia joined the European Union and NATO in 2004 and adopted the euro in 2014, and Riga is the largest city and a leading business hub of the Baltic states.",
    tourism: "Besides Riga's medieval old town, visitors enjoy the long sandy beaches of Jūrmala, the castles and forest trails of Gauja National Park, and Rundāle Palace, a baroque masterpiece by the architect of St Petersburg's Winter Palace. The small town of Kuldīga, a UNESCO World Heritage site since 2023, sits beside Europe's widest waterfall. Hearty rye bread and smoked fish are local favourites.",
    history: "German crusaders conquered the lands of the Baltic tribes in the 1200s, and Riga, founded in 1201, became a rich Hanseatic trading city. After periods of Polish, Swedish and Russian rule, Latvia declared independence in 1918. It was occupied by the Soviet Union in 1940 and by Nazi Germany during the Second World War, then ruled by Moscow until it regained independence in 1991."
  },
  "Liechtenstein": {
    known: "Liechtenstein is known as one of the richest countries in the world per person and as a financial centre for private banking and wealth management. It has had no army since 1868, and its prince keeps more political power than most European monarchs. Its colourful postage stamps have long been prized by collectors.",
    economy: "Although famous for banking, Liechtenstein is also strongly industrial, and manufacturing provides a much larger share of its income than in most European countries. Its factories make precision goods such as construction tools, machinery and car parts, as well as processed food. There are so many jobs that more than half of the people working there commute daily from Switzerland and Austria.",
    business: "Liechtenstein's best-known companies include Hilti, a global maker of construction tools, and LGT, a private bank owned by the princely family. It uses the Swiss franc and has shared a customs union with Switzerland since 1924, but it also belongs to the European Economic Area, which opens the EU single market to its firms. Most of its goods go to Switzerland and EU countries.",
    tourism: "Visitors explore Vaduz, with its modern art museum and the prince's own winery, then hike the Liechtenstein Trail, a route of about 75 kilometres that passes through all eleven municipalities. In winter families ski at the small resort of Malbun. Many travellers also enjoy collecting a souvenir Liechtenstein stamp in their passport, offered at the tourist centre in Vaduz for a small fee.",
    history: "The princely Liechtenstein family, powerful nobles in Austria, bought the lands of Schellenberg and Vaduz, which were united as an imperial principality in 1719. It became fully sovereign in 1806 and stayed neutral in both world wars. After the First World War it turned from Austria towards Switzerland, and in 1938 the prince made Vaduz his permanent home. Women gained the vote only in 1984."
  },
  "Lithuania": {
    known: "Lithuania is known for its passion for basketball, often called its second religion, and for a language that keeps many ancient features of the Indo-European family, which fascinates linguists. It was also the last country in Europe to adopt Christianity, in 1387, and the Hill of Crosses, covered with many thousands of crosses, is a famous symbol of faith.",
    economy: "Lithuania has a mostly services-based economy in which transport and logistics are especially important: its haulage firms run lorries across Europe, and Klaipėda is an ice-free Baltic port. Industry includes oil refining, fertilisers, furniture and food processing, and the country is a world leader in scientific lasers. Its farms grow wheat and other grains, much of it for export.",
    business: "Lithuania exports refined oil products, furniture, food, fertilisers and plastics, trading mostly with its neighbours Latvia and Poland and with Germany and other EU countries. Vilnius has become a busy hub for financial technology start-ups, and a well-known Lithuanian business is the Maxima supermarket chain, found across the Baltic states. Lithuania joined the European Union and NATO in 2004 and adopted the euro in 2015.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Trakai, a red-brick island castle on a lake, and the Curonian Spit, a strip of towering sand dunes and pine forest shared with Russia and listed by UNESCO. The modernist buildings of Kaunas also became a World Heritage site in 2023. Food lovers try cepelinai, large potato dumplings, and šaltibarščiai, a bright pink cold beetroot soup.",
    history: "In the 1200s Mindaugas united the Lithuanian tribes and was crowned king, and the Grand Duchy of Lithuania later reached from the Baltic to the Black Sea. Russia ruled most of the country from 1795 until independence in 1918. After Soviet and Nazi occupations, and the Holocaust, which destroyed its large Jewish community, it was ruled from Moscow until 1990. In January 1991 Soviet troops killed 14 unarmed civilians in Vilnius."
  },
  "Luxembourg": {
    known: "Luxembourg is the world's only grand duchy, a small monarchy headed by a grand duke. It is known as a major financial centre, especially for investment funds, and as one of the three main seats of European Union institutions, home to the EU's Court of Justice and the European Investment Bank.",
    economy: "Luxembourg's economy is dominated by services, above all banking, investment funds and insurance. The country first grew rich from steel, made from iron ore mined in the south, and steel is still produced there. Satellite communications, air cargo and logistics are also important, and nearly half of all workers commute in every day from France, Belgium and Germany.",
    business: "Luxembourg is home to the headquarters of ArcelorMittal, a global steel giant, the satellite operator SES, the broadcaster RTL Group and the cargo airline Cargolux. Its main trading partners are Germany, France and Belgium. It uses the euro and was a founding member of both the Benelux union with Belgium and the Netherlands and the European Economic Community.",
    tourism: "Visitors explore the old fortifications of Luxembourg City, a UNESCO World Heritage site, including the Bock Casemates, kilometres of tunnels cut into the rock. Beyond the capital lie fairy-tale Vianden Castle, the rocky trails of the Mullerthal, nicknamed Little Switzerland, the vineyards of the Moselle valley and the village of Schengen, where the agreement on border-free travel in Europe was signed in 1985.",
    history: "Luxembourg began in 963, when Count Siegfried acquired a rocky outcrop and built a castle there. In the Middle Ages the House of Luxembourg produced several Holy Roman Emperors, and the city grew into a mighty fortress. The Congress of Vienna made it a grand duchy in 1815; it was declared neutral in 1867, yet Germany occupied it in both world wars."
  },
  "Malta": {
    known: "Malta is the smallest country in the European Union and one of the most densely populated in the world. Its people speak both Maltese and English, which makes it a popular place to learn English, and its sunny, historic streets have appeared in many films and TV series. Its flag carries the George Cross, awarded by Britain for the islanders' courage during the Second World War.",
    economy: "Malta has a services-based economy with few natural resources. Tourism, financial services and online gaming, where many international betting companies are licensed, are major earners, and Malta runs one of the world's largest ship registers. Industry includes a large microchip plant, medicines and aircraft maintenance, while scarce water and land keep farming small.",
    business: "Malta's leading exports are microchips, medicines and fuel supplied to ships, and its busy Freeport is a hub where containers are moved between ocean-going ships. Local favourites include Cisk beer and Kinnie, a bitter-orange soft drink. Malta joined the European Union in 2004 and adopted the euro in 2008, and it is also a member of the Commonwealth.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for sunshine, clear water and diving among wrecks and sea caves, and for Mdina, a walled hilltop town known as the Silent City. The Hypogeum of Ħal Saflieni, an underground burial complex carved more than 5,000 years ago, is a UNESCO World Heritage site. In Valletta, St John's Co-Cathedral holds a masterpiece by Caravaggio. Summer village festas bring fireworks, and flaky pastizzi pastries are a favourite snack.",
    history: "Phoenicians, Romans and Arabs all ruled Malta, and tradition says St Paul was shipwrecked there around AD 60. In 1530 the islands passed to the Knights of St John, who drove off a huge Ottoman attack in the Great Siege of 1565. After a brief French occupation, Malta became a British colony, won independence from Britain in 1964 and became a republic in 1974."
  },
  "Moldova": {
    known: "Besides wine, Moldova is known for Transnistria, a strip of land along the Dniester river that broke away in the early 1990s and runs its own affairs without international recognition. Moldova also gave the world the 2000s pop hit Dragostea din tei by the band O-Zone, and it has a rich tradition of folk music, dance and embroidered costumes.",
    economy: "Moldova has a mainly services-based economy in which farming and food processing play a big part, and money sent home by the many Moldovans working abroad is an important source of income. Its rich black soil grows grapes, apples, plums, walnuts, sunflowers, maize and wheat. Factories make clothing and electrical wiring for European carmakers, and a small but growing IT sector employs young programmers.",
    business: "Moldova sells wiring for cars, sunflower seeds and oil, wine, walnuts, fruit and grain, and Romania, with which it shares a language and much history, is its biggest trading partner. Its best-known brands are wineries such as Cricova and Purcari. The currency is the Moldovan leu, and a free-trade agreement with the European Union, signed in 2014, helps send most exports to EU countries.",
    tourism: "Visitors tour wineries and come for Orheiul Vechi, a cave monastery cut into a limestone cliff above a winding river, and for the round medieval fortress of Soroca on the Dniester. Moldova's only UNESCO World Heritage site is a point of the Struve Geodetic Arc, a chain of survey stations used to measure the shape and size of the Earth. National Wine Day each October fills Chisinau with tastings and folk music.",
    history: "Moldova was once the eastern part of the medieval Principality of Moldavia, whose ruler Stephen the Great fought the Ottomans in the late 1400s. Russia annexed the region, then named Bessarabia, in 1812; it joined Romania in 1918 and was taken by the Soviet Union in 1940. Moldova declared independence in 1991, and a short war in 1992 left Transnistria outside the government's control."
  },
  "Monaco": {
    known: "Monaco is known for luxury and glamour: superyachts in its harbour, grand hotels and the Monte Carlo Rally. Most of its residents pay no personal income tax, which attracts wealthy people from around the world. Many people also remember the American film star Grace Kelly, who married Monaco's prince in 1956 and became Princess Grace.",
    economy: "Monaco's economy is almost entirely services: banking and wealth management, tourism, luxury shopping, property and business conferences. The famous casino, once the state's main source of money, now brings in only a small part of its income, which comes largely from taxes on spending and on businesses. Most of the people who work there commute from nearby France and Italy.",
    business: "Monaco is not in the European Union, but it uses the euro under an agreement with the EU and forms a customs union with France, so goods cross its border freely. The Société des Bains de Mer, founded in 1863, runs the casino and several grand hotels, and the yearly Monaco Yacht Show is one of the biggest events in the superyacht business.",
    tourism: "Visitors climb to the old town on the Rock to watch the changing of the guard at the Prince's Palace and to visit the Oceanographic Museum, perched on a cliff above the sea and once directed by the explorer Jacques Cousteau. They admire the Belle Époque casino and relax on Larvotto beach, and each January the Monte Carlo circus festival draws performers from around the world.",
    history: "In 1297 François Grimaldi, disguised as a monk, captured Monaco's fortress, which is why monks appear on the national coat of arms. The principality later came under Spanish, French and Sardinian protection. In 1861 it gave up the towns of Menton and Roquebrune, most of its territory, to France in return for recognised independence, and developed the Monte Carlo casino to replace the lost income."
  },
  "Montenegro": {
    known: "Montenegro is known for packing dramatic scenery into a small space: sandy Adriatic beaches, walled coastal towns and, just inland, rugged mountains and deep canyons. The tiny island village of Sveti Stefan, joined to the shore by a narrow strip of land, is one of its most photographed sights. Montenegro also uses the euro, even though it is not a member of the European Union.",
    economy: "Montenegro has a small, services-based economy in which tourism is by far the most important industry, earning much of the country's income each summer. The country also generates electricity from its rivers and from coal, and produces wine from the local Vranac grape, olive oil and smoked Njeguši ham. Construction and sales of property to foreign buyers are other big earners.",
    business: "Montenegro imports much more than it exports; its main exports include electricity, ores and metals, wine and food, sold mostly to neighbouring countries and the EU. Local favourites include Nikšićko beer and Plantaže wines. It uses the euro, belongs to the CEFTA free-trade area, joined NATO in 2017 and opened talks on joining the European Union in 2012.",
    tourism: "Beyond the walled old towns of Kotor and Budva, visitors raft through the Tara River Canyon, one of the deepest in Europe, in Durmitor National Park, a UNESCO World Heritage site. Others take boats among the water lilies of Lake Skadar, the largest lake in the Balkans, or visit Ostrog Monastery, built into a sheer cliff face and a major Orthodox pilgrimage site.",
    history: "For centuries prince-bishops ruling from Cetinje kept the mountain heartland largely free of Ottoman control, and the great powers recognised Montenegro's independence at the Congress of Berlin in 1878. It was proclaimed a kingdom in 1910, and in 1918 it became part of the new Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, later Yugoslavia. After Yugoslavia broke up, it stayed linked with Serbia until a referendum in 2006 restored its independence."
  },
  "Netherlands": {
    known: "The Netherlands is famous for tulips, windmills, cheeses such as Gouda and Edam, and for being a nation of cyclists, with more bicycles than people. It gave the world great painters, including Rembrandt, Vermeer and Van Gogh, and on King's Day the whole country dresses in orange, the colour of the royal House of Orange.",
    economy: "The Netherlands has a rich, mainly services-based economy built on trade: Rotterdam is Europe's largest port, and goods flow through it to the rest of the continent. Despite its small size, it is one of the world's biggest exporters of food and flowers, thanks to highly efficient farms and vast greenhouses. Chemicals, oil refining and high-tech machinery are also important.",
    business: "Famous Dutch companies include Heineken, Philips, the bank ING, the airline KLM and ASML, which makes the machines used to produce the most advanced computer chips. Germany is by far its biggest trading partner. The Netherlands uses the euro and was a founding member of the European Economic Community and of the Benelux union with Belgium and Luxembourg.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Amsterdam's museums, from the Rijksmuseum with Rembrandt's Night Watch to the Van Gogh Museum and the Anne Frank House, and for the spring tulip fields around the Keukenhof gardens. The 19 windmills of Kinderdijk and the tidal flats of the Wadden Sea are UNESCO World Heritage sites, and cheese markets such as Alkmaar's keep old trading traditions alive.",
    history: "In the 1500s the Dutch provinces rose against Spanish Habsburg rule in a long revolt led by William of Orange, and their independence was confirmed in 1648. In their Golden Age, the 1600s, Dutch merchants built a trading empire stretching from Indonesia to the Caribbean and New Amsterdam, today's New York. The Netherlands became a kingdom in 1815 and was occupied by Nazi Germany from 1940 to 1945."
  },
  "North Macedonia": {
    known: "North Macedonia is known for the lakeside town of Ohrid, filled with medieval churches and precious Byzantine icons, for its rugged mountains and vineyards, and for settling a long dispute with Greece over its name. Under the 2018 Prespa Agreement it adopted its current name in 2019.",
    economy: "North Macedonia has a mainly services-based economy with a sizeable industrial sector. Foreign-owned factories in special economic zones make car parts such as exhaust catalysts and electrical wiring, while mines produce lead, zinc and copper. Farming remains important, especially tobacco, grapes, fruit, vegetables and lamb, and money sent home by emigrants supports many families.",
    business: "Germany is by far its largest export market, and neighbours such as Serbia, Bulgaria and Greece are other key partners. Main exports include vehicle catalysts, electrical wiring, iron and steel, clothing, tobacco and wine. The currency is the denar. The country belongs to CEFTA, a regional free-trade area, joined NATO in 2020 and is a candidate for European Union membership. Skopje is the main business centre.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Ohrid, whose old town and lake form a UNESCO World Heritage site shared with Albania, and for Lake Prespa, Matka Canyon near Skopje and monasteries such as Saint Naum on the lake shore and Saint John Bigorski in the mountains. Wine lovers visit the Tikveš region, known for the red Vranec grape, and a favourite dish is tavče gravče, baked beans.",
    history: "In ancient times much of the area was home to the Paeonians, before coming under Macedonian and then Roman rule. In the ninth century Saint Clement founded a school at Ohrid that helped spread Slavic writing, and the Ottomans ruled the region for about five centuries until 1912. After decades within Yugoslavia it declared independence peacefully in 1991, and a brief conflict in 2001 ended with an agreement widening the rights of its Albanian community."
  },
  "Norway": {
    known: "Norway is known for the midnight sun of its Arctic north and for its success in winter sports: it has won more Winter Olympic medals than any other country. It is also the homeland of the playwright Henrik Ibsen and the painter Edvard Munch, whose painting The Scream is famous worldwide.",
    economy: "Norway's economy is built on oil and natural gas from beneath the North Sea and other offshore waters, which make up the largest share of its exports, although most people work in services. Other key industries are fishing and salmon farming, shipping, and hydropower, which supplies about nine tenths of its electricity. Oil income is saved in a state fund, one of the largest investment funds in the world.",
    business: "Its main exports are oil, gas, fish and seafood, and aluminium, sold mostly to European countries such as Germany, the UK and the Netherlands. Well-known companies include the energy group Equinor, the aluminium maker Norsk Hydro and the fertiliser firm Yara, and Stavanger is the hub of the oil industry. The currency is the Norwegian krone. Norway has twice voted against joining the European Union but trades freely with it through the European Economic Area.",
    tourism: "Visitors hike to viewpoints such as Pulpit Rock and Trolltunga, explore the Lofoten Islands and travel to Tromsø to watch the northern lights in winter. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the Geirangerfjord and Nærøyfjord, Bergen's colourful Bryggen wharf and the wooden stave church at Urnes. Travellers also try brown cheese, dried cod and fresh seafood, especially salmon.",
    history: "Vikings from Norway raided and traded across Europe from around 800 and settled Iceland, while their descendants reached Greenland and North America. Norway later spent about four centuries in union with Denmark and almost a century with Sweden, becoming fully independent in 1905. Nazi Germany occupied it from 1940 to 1945, and the discovery of oil in the North Sea in 1969 transformed it into one of the world's wealthiest countries."
  },
  "Poland": {
    known: "Poland is known for Pope John Paul II, the first Polish pope, for amber from its Baltic coast and pierogi dumplings, and for The Witcher, a fantasy saga that began as Polish novels and grew into hit video games and a television series.",
    economy: "Poland has a large, mixed economy based mainly on services and manufacturing, and it has been one of Europe's fastest-growing economies since communism ended. Factories make cars and car parts, batteries for electric cars, furniture, household appliances and food products, often for other European firms. Farms produce apples, grains, potatoes, pork and poultry, and coal, though in decline, still generates much of its electricity.",
    business: "Germany is by far its biggest trading partner, and most of its trade is with fellow members of the European Union, which Poland joined in 2004. Well-known companies include the video game studio CD Projekt, the fuel group Orlen, the online marketplace Allegro and the parcel locker firm InPost. The currency is the złoty, as Poland has not adopted the euro, and Warsaw is a major financial centre for Central Europe.",
    tourism: "Visitors explore Kraków's old town and royal Wawel Castle, the Wieliczka salt mine with chapels carved from salt, and the former Nazi German camp of Auschwitz-Birkenau, now a memorial and museum, all UNESCO World Heritage sites. Others hike in the Tatra Mountains, see wild bison in the ancient Białowieża Forest or visit Gdańsk on the Baltic. Favourite foods include bigos, a hunter's stew, and pączki doughnuts.",
    history: "Poland became Christian in 966, and in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, united with Lithuania, it was one of Europe's largest states. In the late eighteenth century Russia, Prussia and Austria divided it up, and it vanished from the map until regaining independence in 1918. The Second World War began with Germany's invasion of Poland in 1939, and after decades of communist rule the Solidarity movement helped bring democracy in 1989."
  },
  "Portugal": {
    known: "Portugal is known for port wine from the Douro Valley, for its painted blue and white tiles called azulejos, for custard tarts called pastéis de nata and for its passion for football. Its language, carried abroad by its explorers, is now spoken by more than 250 million people, most of them in Brazil.",
    economy: "Portugal has a mainly services-based economy in which tourism plays a large part, alongside a solid industrial base. Factories make cars and car parts, clothing and shoes, paper and plastics. Farming and forestry produce wine, olive oil, fruit, tomatoes and cork, of which Portugal supplies about half of the world's total, and much of its electricity comes from wind, water and sunshine.",
    business: "Spain is its biggest trading partner, followed by France and Germany, and exports include cars and car parts, clothing, shoes, cork, paper, wine and refined fuels. Well-known names include the energy company EDP, the oil group Galp and the cork maker Corticeira Amorim. Portugal joined the European Union in 1986 and uses the euro, and Lisbon and Porto have grown into hubs for technology start-ups.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the beaches and golden cliffs of the Algarve, the cities of Lisbon and Porto and the hilltop palaces of Sintra. UNESCO World Heritage sites include Lisbon's Jerónimos Monastery and Belém Tower, Porto's historic centre, Sintra and the terraced vineyards of the Douro. The islands of Madeira and the Azores offer volcanic scenery and hiking, while food lovers try grilled sardines and salted cod, known as bacalhau.",
    history: "The land was ruled by the Romans and later by Muslim rulers from North Africa, before Portugal emerged as an independent kingdom in the twelfth century. Its sea voyages built an empire from Brazil to Africa and Asia. In 1755 an earthquake and tsunami destroyed much of Lisbon. Nearly half a century of dictatorship ended with the largely peaceful Carnation Revolution of 1974, after which its African colonies became independent and Portugal became a democracy."
  },
  "Romania": {
    known: "Romania is known for its language, which comes from Latin, a legacy of Roman times, for the modern sculptor Constantin Brâncuși and for the gymnast Nadia Comăneci, who scored the first perfect 10 in Olympic history in 1976. Its Carpathian forests are home to one of Europe's largest populations of brown bears.",
    economy: "Romania has a mainly services-based economy with a strong industrial sector and one of the largest farming areas in the European Union. Its factories make cars and car parts, machinery and electrical goods, and it produces oil and natural gas, including gas from under the Black Sea. Farmers grow wheat, maize and sunflowers, making Romania a big grain exporter, and money sent home by Romanians working abroad also matters.",
    business: "Germany is its largest trading partner, followed by other European Union members such as Italy, Hungary and France. Exports include cars, especially from the Dacia brand, car parts, electrical equipment, grain and wood. The currency is the leu. Romania joined the EU in 2007 and became a full member of the Schengen free-travel area in 2025, and Bucharest, where the software company UiPath began, and Cluj-Napoca are hubs for IT services.",
    tourism: "Visitors explore Transylvania's medieval towns such as Sibiu, Brașov and Sighișoara, whose old centre is a UNESCO World Heritage site, as are the painted churches of Bucovina, the wooden churches of Maramureș and the Danube Delta, a vast wetland full of pelicans. Hikers head for the Carpathian Mountains, and favourite dishes include sarmale, which are cabbage rolls, and a maize porridge called mămăligă.",
    history: "In ancient times the land was home to the Dacians, whom the Romans conquered in 106 AD. The principalities of Wallachia and Moldavia united in 1859 to form Romania, which became fully independent of the Ottoman Empire in 1878 and was joined by Transylvania in 1918. After the Second World War a communist regime took power, and in December 1989 a violent uprising overthrew the dictator Nicolae Ceaușescu. Romania joined NATO in 2004."
  },
  "Russia": {
    known: "Russia is known for great writers such as Tolstoy and Dostoevsky, for the music of Tchaikovsky and the Bolshoi Ballet, and for matryoshka dolls that nest inside one another. The Soviet Union, governed from Moscow, launched the first satellite, Sputnik, in 1957 and sent the first person into space, Yuri Gagarin, in 1961.",
    economy: "Russia's economy rests on its vast natural resources, above all oil and natural gas, which provide a large share of export earnings and government revenue, though services make up the biggest part of output. It also mines coal, diamonds, nickel and palladium, makes aluminium and steel, and has large arms, nuclear power and space industries. Its farms make it one of the world's biggest wheat exporters.",
    business: "Its main exports are oil, gas, coal, metals, fertilisers, wheat and weapons. Sweeping Western sanctions imposed from 2022 over the war in Ukraine shifted much of its trade towards China, India and Turkey. Big companies include the energy groups Gazprom, Rosneft and Lukoil. The currency is the rouble, and Russia is the largest member of the Eurasian Economic Union and a founding member of the BRICS group of large emerging economies.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Saint Petersburg, whose historic centre, with the Hermitage Museum in the former Winter Palace and canals lined with palaces, is a UNESCO World Heritage site, and for the old towns of the Golden Ring near Moscow. Others ride the Trans-Siberian Railway to Lake Baikal, the world's deepest lake, or see the volcanoes of Kamchatka. Russian food includes blini pancakes, pelmeni dumplings and a cabbage soup called shchi.",
    history: "Like Ukraine and Belarus, Russia traces its roots to medieval Kievan Rus, and Moscow's tsars later built an empire reaching the Pacific. The 1917 revolution brought communists to power, and the Soviet Union lost over twenty million people in the Second World War. After the Soviet Union broke up in 1991, Russia became independent; it annexed Crimea from Ukraine in 2014, which most countries do not recognise, and invaded Ukraine in 2022."
  },
  "San Marino": {
    known: "San Marino is one of the world's smallest countries and is known for its unusual government: two Captains Regent, chosen every six months, share the role of head of state, a tradition going back to the Middle Ages. Its postage stamps and euro coins are prized by collectors.",
    economy: "San Marino has a mainly services-based economy in which tourism, banking and shopping play large roles, but manufacturing also matters, with small firms making textiles, electronics, ceramics and cement. Farming is limited to crops such as wheat, grapes and olives, along with livestock and cheese. The sale of stamps and collector coins also adds to government income.",
    business: "Italy is by far its main trading partner. San Marino is not a member of the European Union, but it has a customs union with the EU and uses the euro under an agreement that lets it issue its own coins. It once attracted foreign money with strict banking secrecy, which it later gave up to meet international rules on sharing tax information.",
    tourism: "Many visitors come on day trips from the Italian seaside resort of Rimini to walk the walls and narrow streets of the old town on Monte Titano, where on clear days the view reaches the Adriatic Sea. The historic centre and Monte Titano are a UNESCO World Heritage site. Visitors also watch the changing of the guard at the Public Palace, shop, and try a layered wafer cake called Torta Tre Monti.",
    history: "Tradition says San Marino was founded by Marinus, a Christian stonemason from the island of Rab in today's Croatia, who was fleeing persecution. Its hilltop position helped it stay independent through centuries of wars, and its statutes of 1600 are among the oldest written constitutions still in force. When Italy was unified in the nineteenth century, San Marino kept its freedom, partly in thanks for having sheltered the revolutionary Garibaldi in 1849."
  },
  "Serbia": {
    known: "Serbia is proud of the inventor Nikola Tesla, who was born to Serbian parents in today's Croatia, and is known for its successful tennis and basketball players, for the lively Guča trumpet festival and for šljivovica, a strong plum brandy. Its grilled meats, busy cafés and warm hospitality are famous across the Balkans.",
    economy: "Serbia has a mainly services-based economy with a growing industrial sector. Foreign-owned factories make car parts, tyres, electrical goods and cars, including electric models at Kragujevac, and a large copper and gold mining industry is based around Bor. It is also a major farming country, growing maize, wheat, sunflowers and fruit, and is one of the world's leading producers of raspberries.",
    business: "Most of its trade is with European Union countries, above all Germany and Italy, and China has become a major investor in its mines, steelworks and factories. Exports include electrical equipment, copper, tyres, cars, fruit and IT services. The currency is the Serbian dinar. Serbia is a candidate for European Union membership and belongs to CEFTA, a regional free-trade area, and Belgrade is its business hub, with a strong software industry.",
    tourism: "Visitors enjoy Belgrade's bohemian Skadarlija quarter, the Petrovaradin Fortress in Novi Sad and mountain resorts such as Zlatibor and Kopaonik. Medieval Orthodox monasteries such as Studenica, a UNESCO World Heritage site, draw pilgrims and art lovers, and Đerdap National Park holds the Danube's dramatic Iron Gates gorge. Food lovers try ćevapi, pljeskavica burgers and ajvar, a red pepper relish.",
    history: "Slavs settled the area in the sixth and seventh centuries, and a medieval Serbian state reached its height in the fourteenth century before the Ottoman Empire conquered it, after battles such as Kosovo in 1389. Serbia regained full independence from the Ottomans in 1878 and after the First World War became part of Yugoslavia. Wars followed Yugoslavia's breakup in the 1990s, and in 2008 Kosovo declared independence, recognised by many countries but not by Serbia."
  },
  "Slovakia": {
    known: "Slovakia is known for its folk culture, from the fujara, a long shepherd's flute, to the painted log houses of Čičmany, for its passion for ice hockey and for its national dish, bryndzové halušky, small potato dumplings with sheep's cheese. It also makes more cars per person than any other country.",
    economy: "Slovakia has one of the most industrial economies in Europe, although services still make up the largest share of output. Car making is its backbone: Volkswagen, Kia, Stellantis and Jaguar Land Rover all assemble cars there, supported by many parts suppliers. It also produces electronics, steel, machinery and chemicals, while farming, mainly wheat, maize, barley, sugar beet and rapeseed, plays a small role.",
    business: "Cars, car parts and electronics make up a large share of exports, which go mostly to Germany, Czechia and other European Union countries. Slovakia joined the EU and NATO in 2004 and adopted the euro in 2009. The cybersecurity company ESET was founded in Bratislava, the country's main business hub, and Košice in the east is a centre for steel and IT.",
    tourism: "Visitors hike and ski in the High Tatras, the highest range of the Carpathians, explore Spiš Castle, a vast ruin on the UNESCO World Heritage list, and stroll through the medieval mining town of Banská Štiavnica and the old town of Bardejov, both also listed by UNESCO. The Slovak Paradise national park has gorges climbed by ladders, and the Dobšiná Ice Cave and the spa town of Piešťany draw many visitors.",
    history: "Slavs settled the area in the fifth and sixth centuries, and in the ninth century it belonged to Great Moravia, where the missionaries Cyril and Methodius, brothers from Thessaloniki, spread Christianity and Slavic writing. For about a thousand years it was part of the Kingdom of Hungary, and after 1918 it joined the Czechs in Czechoslovakia. Communist rule ended with the Velvet Revolution of 1989, and Slovakia became independent in 1993 when Czechoslovakia split peacefully."
  },
  "Slovenia": {
    known: "Slovenia is known for its Lipizzaner horses, a breed named after the Lipica stud farm founded in 1580, for the Postojna Cave and its blind cave salamander, the olm, and for remarkable sporting success for a small country, especially in cycling, skiing, ski jumping and basketball.",
    economy: "Slovenia has a developed, mainly services-based economy with a strong export industry. Its factories make medicines, cars and car parts, household appliances and electrical machinery, and its forests supply a busy wood industry. Farming is small, with dairy, livestock, wine, fruit and hops, and tourism is an important source of income.",
    business: "Most of its trade is with European Union countries, especially Germany, Italy, Austria and Croatia, and it also ships large amounts of medicines to Switzerland. Well-known companies include the drug maker Krka, the appliance brand Gorenje and the ski maker Elan. Slovenia joined the EU and NATO in 2004 and in 2007 became the first former communist country to adopt the euro. The port of Koper serves much of Central Europe.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Julian Alps and Triglav National Park, the emerald Soča River, popular for rafting and kayaking, and the Škocjan Caves, a UNESCO World Heritage site with a huge underground canyon. The Venetian-style coastal town of Piran, vineyards and spa towns also draw travellers, who try potica, a rolled nut cake, and kranjska klobasa, a smoked sausage.",
    history: "The ancestors of the Slovenes settled the eastern Alps in the sixth century, and Carantania became one of their early states. For centuries most Slovene lands were ruled by the Habsburgs of Austria. After 1918 they joined what became Yugoslavia, where Slovenia grew into the most prosperous republic. It declared independence on 25 June 1991 and secured it after a brief Ten-Day War with the Yugoslav army."
  },
  "Spain": {
    known: "Spain is known for its sunny beaches and lively street life, for football clubs such as Real Madrid and FC Barcelona, for tapas and paella, for great painters including Velázquez, Goya, Picasso and Dalí, and for the writer Cervantes, author of Don Quixote.",
    economy: "Spain has a large, mainly services-based economy in which tourism is a major earner, since it is one of the most visited countries in the world. It also has one of Europe's largest car industries and makes food products, chemicals and medicines. Farms and greenhouses supply fruit and vegetables across Europe, and Spain is the world's largest producer of olive oil and a big wine producer.",
    business: "France, Germany, Portugal and Italy are the biggest buyers of its exports, which include cars, food, medicines and machinery. Famous companies include Inditex, owner of the Zara fashion chain, the banks Santander and BBVA, the telecom firm Telefónica and the energy company Iberdrola. Spain joined the European Union in 1986 and uses the euro, and Madrid and Barcelona are major centres for business and trade fairs.",
    tourism: "Visitors flock to the Costa del Sol and the Balearic and Canary Islands, and to cities such as Barcelona, with Gaudí's Sagrada Família, and Granada, with the Moorish Alhambra palace. Spain has one of the largest numbers of UNESCO World Heritage sites of any country. Festivals include Valencia's Fallas and the Tomatina tomato fight, and many pilgrims walk the Camino de Santiago.",
    history: "After Roman and Visigothic rule, much of Spain was governed from 711 by Muslim rulers, who left great mosques and palaces. Christian kingdoms gradually took the land back, and in 1492 Granada fell and Columbus reached the Americas, beginning a vast overseas empire. A civil war from 1936 to 1939 was followed by General Franco's dictatorship until his death in 1975, after which Spain became a democracy with a king as head of state."
  },
  "Sweden": {
    known: "Sweden is known for inventions such as dynamite, the Celsius temperature scale and the three-point seat belt, for Astrid Lindgren's children's books, including Pippi Longstocking, for fika, its coffee-and-cake break, and for Midsummer, when people dance around a flower-decked pole.",
    economy: "Sweden has a wealthy, mainly services-based economy with a strong industrial side built on timber, iron ore and low-carbon power from hydroelectric and nuclear plants. It makes trucks, cars, machinery, telecom equipment and medicines, and forests covering about two thirds of the land feed big pulp and paper industries. Its mines produce most of the European Union's iron ore, while farming is small.",
    business: "Its closest trading partners are its Nordic neighbours and Germany, and exports include vehicles, machinery, medicines, paper, iron and steel. Famous Swedish companies include Volvo, Scania, Ericsson, the fashion retailer H&M and the music streaming service Spotify. Sweden joined the European Union in 1995 but kept its own currency, the krona, after voters rejected the euro in 2003. Stockholm is known for its many tech start-ups.",
    tourism: "Visitors explore Stockholm's old town, Gamla Stan, and the Vasa Museum, home to a warship that sank on its first voyage in 1628, then head north to Lapland for the northern lights, a night in an ice hotel and the culture of the Sámi people and their reindeer. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the Viking-age trading town of Birka and the royal palace of Drottningholm.",
    history: "Swedish Vikings travelled east along the rivers of Eastern Europe to trade as far as Constantinople. Sweden left its union with Denmark in 1523 under King Gustav Vasa, and in the seventeenth century it became a great power ruling much of the Baltic region. It lost Finland to Russia in 1809, has not fought a war since 1814 and stayed neutral in both world wars, before joining NATO in 2024."
  },
  "Switzerland": {
    known: "Switzerland is known for its direct democracy, in which citizens vote several times a year on national questions, for its four national languages, German, French, Italian and Romansh, and for cheeses such as Emmental and Gruyère. Geneva hosts many international organisations, including the Red Cross, which was founded there in 1863.",
    economy: "Switzerland has a very wealthy, mainly services-based economy, with banking, insurance and trading in raw materials among its biggest activities, and a strong high-tech industry. Medicines and chemicals are its leading export industry, followed by machinery, precision instruments and watches, and it refines much of the world's gold. Farming is small but valued, centred on dairy cattle, cheese and wine.",
    business: "Its main trading partners are the European Union, above all Germany, and the United States. Famous companies include Nestlé, the drug makers Novartis and Roche, the bank UBS and watch brands such as Rolex and Swatch. The currency is the Swiss franc. Switzerland is not in the European Union but belongs to the European Free Trade Association and is tied to the EU by bilateral agreements, and Zurich and Geneva are global financial centres.",
    tourism: "Visitors ride scenic trains and cable cars into the Alps, gaze at peaks such as the Matterhorn, reach the Jungfraujoch, Europe's highest railway station, and ski in resorts such as Zermatt and St Moritz. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the Jungfrau-Aletsch glacier area, the Rhaetian Railway over the Albula and Bernina passes and the Lavaux vineyard terraces above Lake Geneva. Food favourites include fondue, raclette and rösti.",
    history: "The Swiss Confederation began, by tradition, in 1291, when three Alpine communities swore to defend one another, and it slowly grew as more cantons joined. Its independence from the Holy Roman Empire was recognised in 1648 and its neutrality in 1815. A brief civil war in 1847 led to a modern federal state in 1848. Swiss women won the right to vote nationally only in 1971, and the country joined the United Nations in 2002."
  },
  "Ukraine": {
    known: "Ukraine is known for its painted Easter eggs called pysanky, its embroidered shirts called vyshyvanka and borscht, a beetroot soup whose cooking tradition UNESCO lists as Ukrainian cultural heritage. It is also known for the bandura, a folk instrument with many strings, and for the 1986 Chernobyl nuclear disaster, one of the worst in history.",
    economy: "Services make up the largest part of Ukraine's economy, but farming and heavy industry have long been its backbone. Its farms make it one of the world's biggest exporters of sunflower oil, maize and wheat, and it has large deposits of iron ore that feed its steel industry. The full-scale war that began in 2022 caused heavy damage to factories, ports and power plants and made foreign financial support vital.",
    business: "Farm products, above all grain and sunflower oil, bring in the largest share of export earnings, followed by iron ore and steel, and much of this cargo is shipped from Black Sea ports around Odesa. The European Union is its main trading partner, with a free-trade agreement, and Ukraine became a candidate for EU membership in 2022. The currency is the hryvnia, and a large IT sector is centred on Kyiv, Lviv and Kharkiv.",
    tourism: "Visitors have long come for the cobbled old town and coffee houses of Lviv, the seaside city of Odesa with its Potemkin Stairs, the fortress of Kamianets-Podilskyi and the Carpathian Mountains. The historic centres of Lviv and Odesa and Kyiv's Saint Sophia Cathedral are UNESCO World Heritage sites. Food favourites include varenyky dumplings and salo, cured pork fat. Travel became difficult after the full-scale war began in 2022.",
    history: "After Kievan Rus, Ukrainian lands were divided among Lithuania, Poland and later Russia and Austria, and in the seventeenth century Cossacks founded their own state, the Hetmanate. A famine caused by Soviet policies killed millions of Ukrainians in 1932 and 1933. Ukraine declared independence from the Soviet Union in 1991. In 2014 Russia annexed Crimea, which most countries regard as part of Ukraine, and in 2022 it launched a full-scale invasion."
  },
  "United Kingdom": {
    known: "The United Kingdom is known worldwide for its royal family and centuries-old ceremonies, and for the English language, which spread from it across the globe. It is the home of Shakespeare, the Beatles, Harry Potter and James Bond, and it gave the world the modern rules of football, rugby and cricket.",
    economy: "The UK has a services-based economy: services, from finance, insurance and law to education, tourism and creative industries such as film, music and video games, produce about four fifths of its wealth, and London is one of the world's leading financial centres. Manufacturing is smaller than it once was but still makes cars, aircraft engines, medicines and defence equipment. North Sea oil and gas are declining, while offshore wind power has grown fast.",
    business: "The pound sterling is one of the oldest currencies still in use. The UK's main trading partners are the European Union and the United States, and it sells financial services, cars, machinery, medicines and Scotch whisky abroad. Well-known British companies include HSBC, BP, Shell, Unilever, AstraZeneca and Rolls-Royce. The UK belongs to the G7 and, since 2024, to the CPTPP, a trade pact that links it with countries around the Pacific.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for London's free museums, West End theatres and royal sights such as the Tower of London and Buckingham Palace, and for Stonehenge, Edinburgh Castle and the Scottish Highlands. The UK has more than 30 UNESCO World Heritage Sites, from the elegant 18th-century city of Bath to the Giant's Causeway in Northern Ireland. The Edinburgh Festival Fringe, Notting Hill Carnival, fish and chips and afternoon tea are other favourites.",
    history: "After the Romans left, Anglo-Saxons, Vikings and then the Normans, who conquered England in 1066, shaped the land. England and Scotland united as Great Britain in 1707 and joined with Ireland in 1801, but most of Ireland left the union in 1922. Britain led the Industrial Revolution, stood against Nazi Germany in the Second World War and, after a referendum in 2016, left the European Union in 2020."
  },
  "Vatican City": {
    known: "Vatican City is best known as the home of the Pope, the head of the Catholic Church, whose blessings to crowds in St Peter's Square are broadcast around the world. It is also famous for the Swiss Guard, the Pope's bodyguards in striped Renaissance-style uniforms, and for the conclave, the secret vote in which cardinals choose a new pope while the world watches for white smoke.",
    economy: "Vatican City is too small for farms or factories; its economy rests on visitors and donations. Its income comes mainly from ticket sales at the Vatican Museums, gifts from Catholics around the world, investments and property, and sales of stamps, coins and souvenirs. Almost everything it needs, from food to fuel, is brought in from Italy.",
    business: "Vatican City uses the euro through an agreement with the European Union and issues its own euro coins, which are prized by collectors, though it is not an EU member. The Institute for the Works of Religion, often called the Vatican Bank, manages money for Church bodies, and the state runs its own post office, pharmacy, radio station and newspaper, L'Osservatore Romano.",
    tourism: "Millions of people a year visit the Vatican Museums, whose treasures include ancient Greek and Roman statues and the rooms painted by Raphael, and climb to the top of the great dome of St Peter's for views over Rome. The whole country is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Crowds fill St Peter's Square for papal audiences and Christmas and Easter celebrations, and Holy Years, or Jubilees, bring pilgrims from all over the world.",
    history: "St Peter, one of Jesus' apostles, is traditionally believed to be buried beneath the basilica named after him. For over a thousand years popes ruled the Papal States across central Italy, until the newly united Kingdom of Italy took Rome in 1870. The Lateran Treaty of 1929 with Italy created Vatican City as an independent state, and the Second Vatican Council of 1962 to 1965 brought reforms such as Mass in local languages."
  },
  "Afghanistan": {
    known: "Afghanistan is known for its hand-knotted carpets, for lapis lazuli, a deep blue stone mined in its mountains for thousands of years, and for buzkashi, a rough sport in which riders on horseback fight to carry a goat carcass to a goal. Sadly, it is also known for decades of war that began in the late 1970s.",
    economy: "Afghanistan is a poor, mainly agricultural country: most families depend on farming and herding, growing wheat and fruit such as grapes, pomegranates, apricots and melons, as well as nuts and saffron. For many years it was the world's largest grower of opium poppies, until a ban in 2022 sharply cut cultivation. Before 2021 foreign aid paid for most government spending, and large deposits of copper, iron and other minerals remain mostly untapped.",
    business: "Afghanistan's currency is the afghani. Its main exports include coal, dried fruit and nuts, fresh fruit, cotton, carpets and saffron, and its biggest buyers have long been Pakistan and India, while fuel, food and manufactured goods come in from neighbours such as Iran, China, Pakistan and the Central Asian states. Being landlocked, it depends on its neighbours' roads and ports, and it is a member of the South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation (SAARC).",
    tourism: "Afghanistan's highlights include the blue lakes of Band-e Amir, its first national park, and the Bamiyan valley, whose giant Buddha statues carved into a cliff were destroyed in 2001. The valley and the soaring 12th-century Minaret of Jam are UNESCO World Heritage Sites, and Herat's Great Mosque glitters with blue tiles. Kabuli pulao, rice with lamb, carrots and raisins, is the national dish, but security worries have kept visitor numbers low for decades.",
    history: "The region was part of the Persian Empire, was conquered by Alexander the Great and later became a centre of Buddhist and then Islamic culture. The modern state began in 1747 under Ahmad Shah Durrani. After three wars with Britain, Afghanistan won full control of its foreign affairs in 1919. A Soviet invasion in 1979 was followed by years of war, Taliban rule, a US-led intervention in 2001 and the Taliban's return in 2021."
  },
  "Armenia": {
    known: "Armenia is known for its ancient stone churches and monasteries, for its own alphabet, created around the year 405, and for khachkars, stone slabs carved with intricate crosses. More Armenians live abroad than in the country itself, and Armenia is famous for apricots, brandy and chess, which children study as a school subject.",
    economy: "Armenia is a small, landlocked country with a mostly services-based economy. Mining is a key industry, above all copper and molybdenum, along with food processing, brandy and wine making, and a fast-growing technology sector. Farmers grow fruit, vegetables and grapes, and money sent home by Armenians working abroad, especially in Russia, is an important income for many families.",
    business: "The currency is the dram. Armenia exports copper and other metal ores, gold, brandy and cigarettes, and its main trading partners include Russia, the European Union and China. It belongs to the Eurasian Economic Union, Ararat brandy is its best-known brand, and Yerevan has become a hub for software firms and start-ups; the photo-editing app Picsart was founded there.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for monasteries such as Geghard, partly carved out of a cliff and a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and Tatev, reached by one of the world's longest cable cars, and for Garni, a Greco-Roman temple from the 1st century AD. Lake Sevan, one of the largest high-altitude freshwater lakes in Eurasia, is a favourite summer escape, and lavash flatbread baked in clay ovens and barbecued khorovats are highlights of the food.",
    history: "Under Tigranes the Great, in the 1st century BC, Armenia's kingdom stretched from the Caspian Sea to the Mediterranean; later, Romans, Persians, Arabs, Ottomans and Russians fought over it. From 1915 Armenians in the Ottoman Empire suffered mass killings and deportations, which Armenia and many countries recognise as genocide. Independent from the Soviet Union since 1991, Armenia fought wars with Azerbaijan over Nagorno-Karabakh, which came fully under Azerbaijani control in 2023."
  },
  "Azerbaijan": {
    known: "Azerbaijan is known for its oil and gas, for richly patterned carpets and for mugham, a traditional style of improvised music that UNESCO recognises as part of humanity's cultural heritage. Baku has hosted big international events, including the Eurovision Song Contest in 2012, a Formula One street race and the United Nations climate summit COP29 in 2024.",
    economy: "Azerbaijan's economy is built on oil and gas, which bring in most of its export earnings and a large share of state income. The oil and gas fields lie mainly under the Caspian Sea, and a state oil fund saves part of the profits for the future. Farming employs many people, growing cotton, hazelnuts, fruit, vegetables and tea, and the government is working to expand tourism and other industries.",
    business: "The currency is the manat. Pipelines carry Azerbaijani oil and gas west through Georgia to Turkey, avoiding Russia, and gas continues through Greece and Albania to Italy, a leading buyer of its oil. The state energy company SOCAR works at home and abroad, while BP runs the biggest offshore oil and gas projects. Azerbaijan belongs to the Organization of Turkic States, and its main trading partners include the European Union, Turkey, Russia and China.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Maiden Tower and the Palace of the Shirvanshahs in Baku's UNESCO-listed old city, and for the rock carvings of Gobustan, some of them many thousands of years old, near bubbling mud volcanoes. In the Caucasus foothills, the old silk town of Sheki, with its richly painted Khan's Palace, is a favourite, and plov, kebabs and tea served with jam are highlights of the food.",
    history: "Once home to Caucasian Albania, an ancient kingdom unrelated to the Balkan country, the land later came under Arab, Mongol and Persian rule until Russia took it in the early 1800s. Around 1900 Baku produced about half of the world's oil. After a short-lived democratic republic from 1918 and Soviet rule, Azerbaijan became independent in 1991; it fought Armenia over Nagorno-Karabakh, which it brought fully under its control in 2023."
  },
  "Bahrain": {
    known: "Bahrain is known for its Formula One Grand Prix, which in 2004 became the first held in the Middle East, and for the thousands of ancient burial mounds left by the Dilmun civilisation. It was also the first place on the Arab side of the Gulf where oil was discovered, in 1932.",
    economy: "Oil and gas still provide most of Bahrain's government income, but its reserves are small compared with its neighbours', so it was one of the first Gulf states to build other industries. Banking and finance, aluminium, oil refining, logistics and tourism are now major sectors, and the Alba aluminium smelter is one of the largest in the world. The islands are mostly desert, so there is little farming and most food is imported.",
    business: "The currency is the Bahraini dinar, one of the most valuable currency units in the world. Bahrain exports refined oil products and aluminium and its main trading partners include Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, China and the United States, with which it has a free trade agreement. It is a member of the Gulf Cooperation Council, Manama is a centre for Islamic banking, and Gulf Air is the national airline.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Qal'at al-Bahrain, an ancient harbour and Dilmun capital that is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, the Bahrain National Museum and the Pearling Path in Muharraq, which tells the story of the pearl trade. Others come for the Grand Prix, beach resorts and shopping malls, the lone Tree of Life growing in the desert, and seafood and machboos, spiced rice cooked with meat or fish.",
    history: "Some 4,000 years ago Bahrain was the heart of Dilmun, a trading civilisation linking Mesopotamia with the Indus Valley. Later ruled by Persian and Arab powers and, in the 16th century, by the Portuguese, it came under the Al Khalifa family in 1783 and under British protection in the 19th century, becoming independent from Britain in 1971. It became a kingdom in 2002, and protests in 2011 were ended by a security crackdown."
  },
  "Bangladesh": {
    known: "Bangladesh is known for its huge garment industry, which makes clothes for many of the world's best-known fashion brands, and as the home of the Grameen Bank, a pioneer of microcredit, small loans that help poor people start businesses. A 1952 movement there to have Bengali recognised as a state language inspired International Mother Language Day, marked worldwide on 21 February.",
    economy: "Bangladesh's economy has grown fast since the 1990s, driven by the garment industry, which earns most of its export income. Services make up about half of the economy, while farming still employs a large share of workers, growing rice, jute, tea and vegetables, and fish farming is important too. Money sent home by millions of Bangladeshis working abroad, especially in the Gulf states, is another major source of income.",
    business: "The currency is the taka. Bangladesh's main exports are clothing and knitwear, along with jute goods, leather, footwear and frozen fish and shrimp; its biggest buyers are the European Union, the United States and the United Kingdom, while its largest suppliers are China and India. It belongs to the South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation (SAARC), and Chittagong, officially Chattogram, is its main seaport.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Sundarbans, the world's largest mangrove forest, which Bangladesh shares with India and where Bengal tigers roam, and for Cox's Bazar, one of the longest natural sea beaches in the world. Other highlights include the ruined Buddhist monastery of Paharpur and the Mosque City of Bagerhat, both UNESCO World Heritage Sites, the tea gardens of Sylhet, and hilsa fish cooked in mustard.",
    history: "The Bengal region was home to Buddhist and Hindu kingdoms before Islam spread there from the 13th century, and under the Mughals it was one of the world's richest regions, famous for its fine muslin cloth. After British rule, it became the eastern part of Pakistan in 1947 and, after a bloody war, won independence from Pakistan in 1971 with help from India. In 2024 student-led mass protests forced the prime minister to resign."
  },
  "Bhutan": {
    known: "Bhutan is known as the Land of the Thunder Dragon, a small Buddhist kingdom in the eastern Himalayas that carefully guards its traditions. Many people wear national dress, the gho for men and the kira for women, archery is the national sport, and the constitution requires that at least 60 per cent of the land stay covered by forest.",
    economy: "Bhutan has a small economy based on hydropower, farming and tourism. Rivers rushing down from the Himalayas drive hydroelectric plants, and much of their electricity is sold to India, making power one of its biggest exports. Many Bhutanese still farm, growing rice, maize, potatoes and fruit on terraced hillsides, and cardamom, oranges and apples are sold abroad.",
    business: "The currency is the ngultrum, which is fixed at the same value as the Indian rupee, and rupees are also accepted. India is by far Bhutan's main trading partner, buying its electricity, ferrosilicon for steelmaking, cement and farm produce and supplying most of its imports. Bhutan belongs to the South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation (SAARC) and BIMSTEC, a group of countries around the Bay of Bengal, and Drukair is the national airline.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Paro Taktsang, the Tiger's Nest, a monastery clinging to a cliff about 900 metres above the Paro valley, and for huge fortress-monasteries called dzongs, such as Punakha Dzong. Colourful masked dance festivals called tshechus fill monastery courtyards each year, trekking routes cross high Himalayan passes, and the national dish, ema datshi, is chillies cooked in cheese. A daily fee paid by visitors helps keep tourism small.",
    history: "Buddhism reached Bhutan in the 7th and 8th centuries, and in the 17th century the Tibetan lama Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyal united the country and built many dzongs. In 1907 the Wangchuck family became hereditary kings, and Bhutan kept its independence, taking guidance on foreign affairs first from Britain and later from India. Long closed to outsiders, it let in its first tourists in 1974 and became a constitutional monarchy with elections in 2008."
  },
  "Brunei": {
    known: "Brunei is known for its golden-domed mosques and for the Istana Nurul Iman, the royal palace, one of the largest residential palaces in the world. Islam is the official religion and shapes daily life and the law; the sale of alcohol has been banned since 1991. Citizens pay no personal income tax and receive free schooling and almost free health care.",
    economy: "Brunei's economy depends on oil and natural gas, which provide the great majority of its exports and government income. Much of the gas is shipped abroad as liquefied natural gas, and newer plants make petrochemicals and fertiliser. The government employs many citizens, and it is working to build up fishing, farming, halal food and tourism, since the country imports most of its food.",
    business: "The Brunei dollar is fixed at equal value to the Singapore dollar, and each is accepted in both countries. Brunei sells its oil, gas and fuels mainly to buyers in Asia and Australia, such as Japan, Singapore and China. Brunei Shell Petroleum, owned jointly by the government and Shell, produces most of its oil and gas, and the country belongs to ASEAN and the CPTPP, a trade pact linking countries around the Pacific.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddien Mosque, whose golden dome rises above a lagoon in the capital, and for the Royal Regalia Museum. Beyond the city, Ulu Temburong National Park protects untouched rainforest crossed by a canopy walkway high in the trees, and boat trips through the mangroves reveal long-nosed proboscis monkeys. Food stalls serve ambuyat, a sticky dish made from sago starch.",
    history: "From the 15th to the 17th centuries, the Sultanate of Brunei was a powerful sea-trading state whose influence reached across much of Borneo and into parts of the Philippines. It later lost most of its land to Sarawak and North Borneo, and became a British protectorate in 1888. Oil was found at Seria in 1929. Brunei chose not to join Malaysia in 1963 and became fully independent from Britain in 1984."
  },
  "Cambodia": {
    known: "Cambodia is known for the graceful Royal Ballet, whose dancers mirror the apsaras, heavenly dancers carved on its temple walls, and for the Tonle Sap, Southeast Asia's largest freshwater lake, whose river reverses direction each year when the monsoon floods arrive. Its Kampot pepper and hand-woven silk are also prized around the world.",
    economy: "Cambodia's economy stands on a few main pillars: factories making clothes, shoes and travel goods, tourism, construction and farming. Rice is the main crop, alongside cassava, rubber, cashew nuts and pepper, and farming still employs a large share of the workforce. Many factories are foreign-owned, and the economy has grown quickly since the 1990s, though many rural families remain poor.",
    business: "The currency is the riel, but US dollars are widely used for everyday shopping. Cambodia's main exports are clothing, shoes, bags and other travel goods, bicycles, rice, rubber and cashews; its biggest markets include the United States, the European Union and Japan, while China is a leading source of imports and investment. Cambodia is a member of ASEAN.",
    tourism: "Beyond Angkor Wat itself, visitors explore the Bayon, with its giant smiling stone faces, Ta Prohm, wrapped in the roots of huge trees, and the 7th-century temple city of Sambor Prei Kuk, which, like Angkor, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Others come for the islands off the southern coast, the riverside town of Kampot, Phnom Penh's Royal Palace and dishes such as fish amok, a curry steamed in banana leaves.",
    history: "The Khmer Empire, which built Angkor, ruled much of mainland Southeast Asia from the 9th to the 15th century. Cambodia became a French protectorate in 1863 and won independence from France in 1953. From 1975 to 1979 the Khmer Rouge emptied the cities and ruled with terror; up to a quarter of the population died before Vietnamese forces drove them out. Peace agreements in 1991 and UN-run elections in 1993 followed."
  },
  "China": {
    known: "China is known for one of the world's oldest continuous civilisations, which gave the world paper, printing, gunpowder and the compass, and for its tea, silk, porcelain, kung fu and the giant panda. Today it is also called the 'world's factory', because it makes a huge share of the planet's electronics, clothes and toys.",
    economy: "China has one of the largest economies in the world, built on manufacturing and exports, from steel and ships to phones, electric cars and solar panels. Since market reforms began in 1978, hundreds of millions of people have escaped poverty. Services now make up more than half the economy, big state-owned companies work alongside private firms, and farmers produce rice, wheat, vegetables and pork for its huge population.",
    business: "The currency is the renminbi, counted in yuan. China is a trading giant, selling electronics, machinery, clothing, steel and cars worldwide; its biggest partners include the ASEAN countries of Southeast Asia, the European Union, the United States, Japan and South Korea. Famous Chinese companies include Huawei, Alibaba, Tencent, BYD, Lenovo and Xiaomi, and Shanghai, Shenzhen and Hong Kong are major business hubs. China joined the World Trade Organization in 2001.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Terracotta Army in Xi'an, thousands of life-size clay soldiers buried with China's first emperor, the towering rock pillars of Zhangjiajie, the river scenery of Guilin and the giant pandas of Chengdu. China has about 60 UNESCO World Heritage Sites, among the most of any country. The Spring Festival, or Chinese New Year, and food from dumplings and Peking duck to spicy Sichuan dishes are big attractions.",
    history: "Chinese civilisation grew up along the Yellow River more than 3,000 years ago, and in 221 BC the Qin dynasty united the country under its first emperor. Dynasties such as the Han, Tang, Song, Ming and Qing followed for over two thousand years. The last emperor gave up the throne in 1912, and after years of war the Communist Party founded the People's Republic in 1949, while the defeated Nationalist government moved to Taiwan."
  },
  "Cyprus": {
    known: "Cyprus is known as one of the sunniest holiday islands in the Mediterranean, for halloumi, a squeaky cheese that is perfect for grilling, and for Commandaria, a sweet dessert wine with a history going back many centuries. Its position between Europe, Asia and Africa has made it a meeting point of many cultures for thousands of years.",
    economy: "Cyprus has a services-based economy, with tourism, shipping, banking, legal and accounting services and property among its biggest earners, and Limassol is one of Europe's leading centres for managing ships. Farming is small but produces potatoes, citrus fruit, grapes and olives, as well as the milk for halloumi, while natural gas found off the coast since 2011 may one day become a new industry.",
    business: "Cyprus joined the European Union in 2004 and adopted the euro in 2008, replacing the Cyprus pound. It exports medicines, halloumi and potatoes, many ships fly its flag, and its main trading partners are fellow EU countries, especially Greece. Relatively low company taxes and a legal system based on English law attract international firms, and Limassol has become a hub for shipping and investment companies.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for beaches such as those at Ayia Napa and Protaras, and for Paphos, a UNESCO World Heritage Site with beautifully preserved Roman floor mosaics. The painted churches of the Troodos mountains and the Stone Age village of Choirokoitia are also UNESCO-listed, the ancient theatre of Kourion looks out over the sea, and meze, a feast of many small dishes, is a highlight.",
    history: "Greeks settled on Cyprus over 3,000 years ago, and the island was later ruled by Assyrians, Egyptians, Persians, Romans, Byzantines, Crusaders, Venetians and Ottomans. Britain took control in 1878, and Cyprus became independent from Britain in 1960. In 1974 a coup backed by the military government of Greece was followed by the landing of Turkish troops, and the island was divided; the north declared itself a separate state in 1983, recognised only by Turkey."
  },
  "Georgia": {
    known: "Georgia is known for its own alphabet with curling letters, for polyphonic singing, in which several voices weave different melodies together, and for the supra, a generous feast led by a toastmaster called the tamada. It is also famous for khachapuri, a bread filled with melted cheese, and for dramatic folk dances performed around the world.",
    economy: "Georgia has a mostly services-based economy, with tourism, transport, trade and banking leading the way. Many people farm, growing grapes, hazelnuts, citrus fruit, tea and vegetables, and mines produce manganese and copper. Lying on the route between the Caspian and Black Seas, Georgia earns money from pipelines, railways and ports that carry oil, gas and goods between Asia and Europe, and money sent home by Georgians abroad also matters.",
    business: "The currency is the lari. Georgia exports copper ore, ferroalloys used in steelmaking, wine, mineral water such as the well-known Borjomi, hazelnuts and re-exported cars, and its main trading partners include Turkey, Russia, China, Azerbaijan and the European Union. It has free trade agreements with both the EU and China, and the Black Sea ports of Poti and Batumi are key gateways.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the mountain region of Svaneti, where medieval stone towers rise above villages, and for Gelati Monastery and the old capital Mtskheta, both UNESCO World Heritage Sites. The hilltop Gergeti Trinity Church below Mount Kazbek, the seaside city of Batumi and the wine region of Kakheti are favourites, and cafés serve khinkali dumplings and churchkhela, a sweet of nuts dipped in thickened grape juice.",
    history: "Western Georgia was once the kingdom of Colchis, home of the Golden Fleece in Greek myth, and Georgians adopted Christianity in the 4th century. Their golden age came around the 12th century, under King David the Builder and Queen Tamar, before Mongol, Persian and Ottoman invasions. Russia annexed Georgia in the 1800s; after Soviet rule it became independent in 1991, and in 2008 it fought a short war with Russia over South Ossetia."
  },
  "India": {
    known: "India is known for Bollywood and its other film industries, which release well over a thousand films a year, for spicy and varied food, and for yoga, which began there in ancient times. It is also famous for cricket, followed by hundreds of millions of fans, for festivals such as Diwali and Holi, and for the zero, whose use as a number was developed by Indian mathematicians.",
    economy: "India has one of the world's largest economies, and it has grown fast in recent decades. Services such as information technology, software and outsourcing produce the biggest share of wealth, but farming still employs almost half of all workers, growing rice, wheat, cotton, sugar cane, tea and spices. Manufacturing is growing, from cars and phones to generic medicines, which have earned India the nickname 'pharmacy of the world'.",
    business: "The currency is the rupee. India's main exports include refined fuels, software and IT services, medicines, gems and jewellery, machinery, textiles, rice and smartphones, and its main trading partners include the United States, China, the European Union and the United Arab Emirates. Well-known companies include Reliance, Infosys, Mahindra and Tata, which owns Jaguar Land Rover; Mumbai and Bengaluru are major business hubs, and India is a founding member of the BRICS group.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Rajasthan's forts and palaces in Jaipur and Jodhpur, the holy city of Varanasi on the Ganges, the beaches of Goa, the backwaters of Kerala and the Himalayas. India has more than 40 UNESCO World Heritage Sites, including the cave temples of Ajanta and Ellora, and the Kumbh Mela, a Hindu pilgrimage, draws tens of millions of people. Regional food, from Punjabi curries to South Indian dosas, is a highlight.",
    history: "More than 4,500 years ago the Indus Valley civilisation built planned cities in the north-west of the subcontinent. Great empires followed, including the Maurya under Ashoka, the Gupta and the Mughals, before Britain took control. After a long struggle led by Mahatma Gandhi, India won independence from Britain in 1947 and was partitioned, creating Pakistan amid mass migration and violence. In 2023 India landed a spacecraft near the Moon's south pole."
  },
  "Indonesia": {
    known: "Indonesia has more active volcanoes than any other country, and it is known for its rainforests and coral reefs and for rare animals such as orangutans and Komodo dragons, the world's largest lizards. Its batik cloth and gamelan music, played by orchestras of bronze gongs and drums, are famous far beyond its borders.",
    economy: "Indonesia has the largest economy in Southeast Asia. Services and manufacturing produce most of its wealth, but farming still employs millions of people, who grow rice, the staple food, as well as rubber, coffee and cocoa. The country is the world's biggest producer of palm oil and of nickel, a key metal for electric car batteries, and a major coal exporter.",
    business: "Its main exports are coal, palm oil, stainless steel and other nickel products, natural gas, electronics, clothing and footwear. China is its biggest trading partner, with the United States, Japan and its Southeast Asian neighbours also important. The currency is the rupiah. Indonesia is a founding member of ASEAN and a member of the G20, and well-known names include the energy company Pertamina, the airline Garuda Indonesia and Indomie instant noodles.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Bali's beaches, Hindu temples and terraced rice fields, and for Borobudur, the world's largest Buddhist temple, and the Hindu temples of Prambanan, both UNESCO World Heritage sites on Java. Divers explore the reefs of Raja Ampat and Komodo National Park, hikers climb volcanoes such as Bromo, and dishes such as nasi goreng, satay and rendang are a big draw.",
    history: "Hindu and Buddhist kingdoms such as Srivijaya and Majapahit once controlled trade across the islands, and Islam spread there from around the 13th century. The Dutch gradually took control from the 1600s. After Japanese occupation in the Second World War, Indonesia declared independence in 1945, which the Netherlands accepted in 1949 after four years of fighting. It moved to democracy after decades of authoritarian rule ended in 1998."
  },
  "Iran": {
    known: "Iran is known for having some of the world's largest reserves of oil and natural gas, and for producing most of the world's saffron along with large crops of pistachios. Nowruz, the Persian New Year celebrated at the spring equinox, is its biggest festival, and Iranian films have won major international awards, including Oscars.",
    economy: "Iran has a large, mixed economy in which oil, natural gas and petrochemicals bring in most of the export income and much of the government's revenue. It also has big industries making cars, steel and cement, and farmers grow wheat, rice, fruit, pistachios and saffron. Much of the economy is controlled by the state or by powerful state-linked foundations, and international sanctions have limited its trade for many years.",
    business: "Its main exports are crude oil, petrochemicals, steel, copper and farm goods such as pistachios and fruit. China is its largest trading partner, with the United Arab Emirates, Turkey and Iraq also important. The currency is the rial. Iran was a founding member of OPEC and has joined the BRICS group and the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation, and Tehran's historic Grand Bazaar is still a busy centre of trade.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the ruins of Persepolis, the ceremonial capital of the ancient Persian kings, and for Isfahan's vast Naqsh-e Jahan Square with its blue-tiled mosques, both UNESCO World Heritage sites. The desert city of Yazd, with its mud-brick lanes and wind towers, and the tombs of the poets Hafez and Saadi in Shiraz are also favourites, and visitors feast on saffron rice, kebabs and slow-cooked herb stews.",
    history: "Cyrus the Great founded the Persian Empire in the 6th century BC, and later the Parthians and Sasanians rivalled Rome. After the Arab conquest in the 7th century, Islam gradually replaced the old Zoroastrian faith, and from 1501 the Safavids made Shia Islam the state religion. Iran was never a colony. In 1979 a revolution ended the monarchy and created the Islamic Republic, which fought a war with Iraq from 1980 to 1988."
  },
  "Iraq": {
    known: "Iraq is known for its huge oil reserves and for the ruins of legendary ancient cities such as Babylon, Ur and Nineveh. It is also famous for its date palms, which have grown along its rivers since ancient times, and for the Iraqi maqam, a classical style of Arabic music recognised by UNESCO. In recent decades, wars have often brought it into the news.",
    economy: "Iraq's economy is built on oil, which provides almost all of its export earnings and most of the government's income. The state is the biggest employer, paying millions of public salaries from oil money, while the private sector remains small. Farms along the Tigris and Euphrates produce wheat, barley, rice, dates and vegetables, but droughts and shrinking river flows have made farming harder.",
    business: "Most of its oil is shipped from terminals near Basra on the Gulf to buyers in Asia, especially China and India, while many everyday goods are imported, above all from China, Turkey and Iran. The currency is the Iraqi dinar. Iraq helped to found OPEC at a meeting in Baghdad in 1960, and Erbil, capital of the self-governing Kurdistan Region in the north, is another important business centre.",
    tourism: "Most foreign visitors are religious pilgrims heading for the golden-domed shrines of Najaf and Karbala, which draw millions each year. Others come for UNESCO World Heritage sites such as the spiral minaret of Samarra, the ancient city of Hatra, the Erbil Citadel and the southern marshes, while the mountains of the Kurdish north are popular in summer. Masgouf, river fish grilled beside an open fire, is the national dish.",
    history: "After the Sumerians came the Babylonians, whose king Hammurabi issued a famous law code, and the Assyrians. Mongol armies sacked Baghdad in 1258, and the region later spent centuries under Ottoman rule. Britain controlled Iraq after the First World War until independence in 1932. Under Saddam Hussein, Iraq fought Iran and invaded Kuwait. A US-led invasion removed him in 2003, and the Islamic State group lost its last Iraqi strongholds in 2017."
  },
  "Israel": {
    known: "Israel is known for the Dead Sea, the lowest point on land, where the salty water lets bathers float with ease, and for the beaches and nightlife of Tel Aviv. Hebrew, an ancient language revived for everyday speech from the late 1800s, is its main language, and Israeli engineers helped pioneer modern drip irrigation. It is also at the centre of the long-running Israeli-Palestinian conflict.",
    economy: "Israel has a high-income economy based mainly on services and advanced industry. Technology, from software and cybersecurity to medical devices and computer chips, brings in about half of its export income, and the country also has large pharmaceutical, chemical and defence industries. Natural gas from fields found off its coast since 2009 now generates most of its electricity, and its small farming sector grows crops efficiently in dry conditions.",
    business: "Its main exports are technology products and services, cut diamonds, medicines, chemicals and military equipment, and its biggest trading partners are the United States, the European Union and China. The currency is the new shekel. Well-known companies from Israel include the generic medicine maker Teva, the cybersecurity firm Check Point and the navigation app Waze, and Tel Aviv, with its stock exchange, is the main business hub.",
    tourism: "Christian pilgrims visit Nazareth and the shores of the Sea of Galilee, while the mountaintop fortress of Masada, the white Bauhaus buildings of Tel Aviv and the terraced Bahá'í Gardens in Haifa are UNESCO World Heritage sites. Others dive on coral reefs off Eilat on the Red Sea or hike in the Negev desert, and falafel, hummus and shakshuka are everyday favourites.",
    history: "Ancient Israelite kingdoms flourished here, and after Rome destroyed Jerusalem's Temple in AD 70 many Jews were scattered abroad. Israel declared independence in 1948 as British rule ended, and in the war that followed hundreds of thousands of Palestinians fled or were driven out. It captured the West Bank and Gaza in 1967 and later made peace with Egypt and Jordan. A Hamas-led attack in 2023 set off a war that devastated Gaza."
  },
  "Japan": {
    known: "Japan is known around the world for anime, manga and video games, for sushi and ramen, and for cars and electronics sold in almost every country. Its spring cherry blossom season, its samurai history and martial arts such as judo and karate are famous too, and Japanese people have some of the longest lifespans on Earth.",
    economy: "Japan has one of the world's largest economies, based mostly on services and backed by a powerful manufacturing sector that makes cars, machinery, electronic parts, robots and steel. It has few natural resources, so it imports nearly all of its oil and gas and much of its food. Farming is small and centred on rice, and a shrinking, ageing population is one of its biggest challenges.",
    business: "Its main exports are cars and car parts, machinery, electronic components and steel, and its biggest trading partners are China and the United States. The currency is the yen. Famous Japanese companies include Toyota, Honda, Sony, Nintendo, Panasonic and Uniqlo, and Japan belongs to the G7 group of major industrial democracies and to large Asia-Pacific free trade agreements.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the temples and gardens of Kyoto, the white Himeji Castle, the Hiroshima Peace Memorial and the shrine gate of Itsukushima, which seems to float at high tide, all UNESCO World Heritage sites. They also soak in hot springs, ski the deep powder snow of Hokkaido, admire autumn leaves and explore the neon-lit streets of Tokyo and Osaka, and many travel simply for the food.",
    history: "For centuries real power lay with military rulers called shoguns, and under the Tokugawa shoguns Japan largely shut itself off from the world. After American warships arrived in 1853, the Meiji Restoration of 1868 brought rapid modernisation and an overseas empire. Defeat in the Second World War, after the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki, was followed by an economic boom, and in 2011 an earthquake and tsunami caused the Fukushima nuclear disaster."
  },
  "Jordan": {
    known: "Jordan is known for the red sands and cliffs of Wadi Rum, where films from 'Lawrence of Arabia' to space adventures set on Mars have been shot, and for its warm hospitality. It has also taken in large numbers of refugees from neighbouring conflicts over many decades, and its national dish, mansaf, is lamb cooked in a sauce made from dried yoghurt and served on rice.",
    economy: "Jordan has few natural resources and very little water, so its economy relies mainly on services such as tourism, banking, trade and transport. Money sent home by Jordanians working in the Gulf states, and foreign aid, are important sources of income. Phosphate and potash mining, factories making clothing and medicines, and vegetable farming in the Jordan Valley add to the mix, but most energy has to be imported.",
    business: "Its main exports are clothing, fertilisers, potash, phosphates and medicines, with much of the clothing sold to the United States under a free trade agreement. The currency is the Jordanian dinar, which is tied to the US dollar. Aqaba, on the Red Sea, is the country's only seaport and a special economic zone, and well-known firms founded in Jordan include the drug maker Hikma and the courier company Aramex.",
    tourism: "Beyond Petra, visitors explore the Roman city of Jerash with its columned streets, float in the salty Dead Sea and dive in the Red Sea off Aqaba. UNESCO World Heritage sites include Quseir Amra, an early Islamic desert castle with painted walls, and the Baptism Site on the Jordan River, where many Christians believe Jesus was baptised. Many also stop at Mount Nebo, where Moses is said to have seen the Promised Land.",
    history: "In ancient times the land held biblical kingdoms such as Moab and Edom, then Roman cities and Umayyad desert palaces. After four centuries of Ottoman rule, Britain created the Emirate of Transjordan in 1921 under Abdullah I, whose Hashemite family had led the Arab Revolt against the Ottomans. Jordan became independent from Britain in 1946, lost control of the West Bank in the 1967 war and signed a peace treaty with Israel in 1994."
  },
  "Kazakhstan": {
    known: "Kazakhstan is known as the home of the Baikonur Cosmodrome, from which Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space in 1961, and as the place where the wild ancestors of today's apples still grow in mountain forests. It is also famous for its horse culture, its eagle hunters and the nomadic traditions of the Kazakh people.",
    economy: "Kazakhstan has the largest economy in Central Asia, driven by oil, which brings in about half of its export income, along with natural gas and metals such as copper, zinc, iron and chromium. The northern steppe is a major wheat-growing region, making the country a big exporter of grain and flour, and herders raise sheep, cattle and horses.",
    business: "Crude oil, much of it piped across Russia to the Black Sea, is the main export, with metals, uranium and wheat also important, and its biggest trading partners are the European Union, China and Russia. The currency is the tenge. Kazakhstan belongs to the Eurasian Economic Union, a trade bloc with Russia and other former Soviet states, and Almaty, its largest city and former capital, is the main business and banking centre.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the mountains around Almaty, with alpine lakes, ski slopes and the high-altitude Medeu skating rink, and for Charyn Canyon, often compared to a smaller Grand Canyon. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the turquoise-domed Mausoleum of Khoja Ahmed Yasawi in Turkistan and the rock carvings of Tamgaly. Guests are often served beshbarmak, boiled meat on flat noodles, and kumis, fermented mare's milk.",
    history: "The steppe was home to Saka nomads and later to Turkic peoples and the Mongol Golden Horde, before the Kazakh Khanate formed in the 15th century. Russia took control in the 19th century. Under Soviet rule a famine in the 1930s killed a huge share of all Kazakhs, and hundreds of nuclear tests took place near Semey. Kazakhstan became independent from the Soviet Union in 1991 and gave up its Soviet nuclear weapons."
  },
  "Kuwait": {
    known: "Kuwait is known for its great oil wealth, which pays for free healthcare, free education and generous benefits for its citizens, who make up only about a third of the people living there. In 1953 it created the world's oldest sovereign wealth fund, a state fund that invests oil money abroad for future generations, and its dinar is one of the most valuable currency units in the world.",
    economy: "Oil dominates Kuwait's economy, providing the great majority of its export earnings and government revenue, and much of it is refined into fuels or turned into petrochemicals. Most Kuwaiti citizens work for the state, while foreign workers fill most private sector jobs, from building sites to shops and homes. Very little can be farmed in the desert, so nearly all food is imported.",
    business: "Crude oil and refined fuels make up almost all exports, sold mainly to Asian buyers such as China, India, South Korea and Japan. The currency is the Kuwaiti dinar. Kuwait was a founding member of OPEC and of the Gulf Cooperation Council, and well-known Kuwaiti companies include the mobile operator Zain, the logistics firm Agility and the National Bank of Kuwait.",
    tourism: "Visitors wander the old Souq Al-Mubarakiya market, see the Grand Mosque and the Liberation Tower, and shop in some of the largest malls in the region. A short boat ride away, Failaka Island, which the ancient Greeks called Ikaros, holds the ruins of a Greek fortress and temple. In the cooler winter months many families camp in the desert, and the national dish, machboos, is spiced rice with meat or fish.",
    history: "Arab clans settled Kuwait in the early 18th century, and the Al Sabah family has ruled it since the 1750s. People lived by pearl diving, boat building and sea trade, and in 1899 Kuwait came under British protection. Oil was found in 1938, and independence from Britain followed in 1961. Iraq invaded in 1990, and a US-led coalition drove its forces out in 1991, after they set hundreds of oil wells on fire."
  },
  "Kyrgyzstan": {
    known: "Kyrgyzstan is known for Issyk-Kul, a huge mountain lake that never freezes, even in the harsh winter, and for the Epic of Manas, one of the longest poems in the world, which storytellers recite from memory. Its flag shows the wooden crown at the top of a yurt, the felt tent of the nomads, and kok-boru, a rough horseback game in which riders fight over a goat carcass, is hugely popular.",
    economy: "Kyrgyzstan is one of the poorer countries of the former Soviet Union, and money sent home by hundreds of thousands of migrants working in Russia makes up a large share of the economy. Gold mining, especially at the huge Kumtor mine, earns much of its export income, while farmers raise sheep, cattle and horses and grow cotton, potatoes and fruit. Rivers from the mountains power hydroelectric dams.",
    business: "Gold is the main export, while many goods arrive from China, its biggest source of imports, and are traded on across the region through huge markets such as Bishkek's Dordoi Bazaar. Russia and Kazakhstan are also key partners. The currency is the som, and Kyrgyzstan is a member of the Eurasian Economic Union and was the first former Soviet republic to join the World Trade Organization, in 1998.",
    tourism: "Visitors come to trek, ride horses and sleep in yurt camps on high summer pastures, such as those around Song-Kul lake. UNESCO World Heritage sites include Sulaiman-Too, a sacred mountain in the city of Osh, and the ruins of Silk Road towns such as Balasagun, marked by the Burana Tower. Travellers also visit Tash Rabat, a stone inn used by Silk Road merchants, and the walnut forests of Arslanbob.",
    history: "The Kyrgyz are thought to have migrated from southern Siberia, and the land was ruled by Turkic khanates and the Mongols. The Russian Empire took over in the 19th century, and a revolt against Russian rule in 1916 ended in massacres and a desperate flight to China. After decades as a Soviet republic, Kyrgyzstan became independent in 1991, and popular protests brought down presidents in 2005, 2010 and 2020."
  },
  "Laos": {
    known: "Laos is known for its slow, relaxed pace of life and for sticky rice, eaten with the hands at almost every meal. It also carries a painful legacy: it is the most heavily bombed country per person in history, hit by millions of bombs during the Vietnam War, and unexploded ones still injure people today.",
    economy: "Farming, mostly of rice, still employs a large part of the population, but the main sources of export income are hydropower and mining. Laos sells electricity from dams on the Mekong and its tributaries to its neighbours, which has earned it the nickname 'the battery of Southeast Asia', and it mines copper, gold and potash. Heavy borrowing for dams and a railway has left a large debt, much of it owed to China.",
    business: "Its main exports are electricity, gold, copper, potash, rubber, bananas and coffee from the Bolaven Plateau, and most of its trade is with its neighbours Thailand, China and Vietnam. The currency is the kip. Laos joined ASEAN in 1997, and a Chinese-built railway, opened in 2021, links Vientiane with southern China. Beerlao, the national beer, is a well-known local brand.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Luang Prabang, a UNESCO-listed former royal capital where saffron-robed monks collect offerings of food at dawn among temples and French colonial houses, and for Vat Phou, an ancient Khmer temple also on the UNESCO list. Others kayak among the limestone peaks of Vang Vieng, swim at the turquoise Kuang Si Falls or relax on the Mekong's Four Thousand Islands, and try larb, a spicy minced meat salad.",
    history: "The kingdom of Lan Xang, the 'Land of a Million Elephants', was founded in 1353, but it later split apart and fell under the influence of Siam, today's Thailand. Laos became part of French Indochina in 1893 and won independence from France in 1953. A long civil war followed, drawn into the Vietnam War, and in 1975 the communist Pathet Lao abolished the monarchy and set up today's one-party state."
  },
  "Lebanon": {
    known: "Lebanon is known for the cedar tree on its flag, for a cuisine of mezze, hummus and tabbouleh loved around the world, and for its mix of religious communities, with 18 officially recognised sects. More people of Lebanese descent live abroad, from Brazil to Australia, than in Lebanon itself.",
    economy: "Lebanon's economy is based mainly on services such as banking, trade and tourism, and money sent home by Lebanese living abroad is a lifeline for many families. Farmers, especially in the fertile Bekaa Valley, grow fruit, vegetables, grapes for wine and tobacco. In 2019 the country plunged into one of the worst financial crises in modern history, as banks froze savings and the Lebanese pound lost almost all its value.",
    business: "Its main exports include gold and jewellery, fruit, vegetables and wine from wineries such as Ksara, founded in 1857, and much of its trade is with the Gulf states, China, Turkey and Europe. The currency is the Lebanese pound. Beirut has long been a regional centre for banking, media and fashion, home to designers such as Elie Saab, and Lebanon was a founding member of the Arab League.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Roman temples of Baalbek, the ancient ports of Byblos and Tyre, the Umayyad town of Anjar, and the Qadisha Valley and nearby Cedars of God forest, all UNESCO World Heritage sites. The underground caves of Jeita Grotto, summer music festivals held among Baalbek's ruins and ski slopes within easy reach of the coast are further draws, as is a lively restaurant scene.",
    history: "The Phoenician cities later fell to a long line of conquerors, from the Assyrians and Persians to the Greeks, Romans, Arabs and Crusaders, and for four centuries the Ottomans. France governed Lebanon after the First World War until independence in 1943. A civil war from 1975 to 1990 killed well over 100,000 people, and in 2020 a huge explosion at Beirut's port, caused by badly stored chemicals, devastated the surrounding neighbourhoods."
  },
  "Malaysia": {
    known: "Malaysia is known for its mix of Malay, Chinese, Indian and indigenous cultures, which shows in its many festivals and in dishes such as nasi lemak, satay and laksa. It is also famous for the orangutans of its Borneo rainforests and for being one of the world's biggest producers of palm oil.",
    economy: "Services such as trade, finance and tourism make up more than half of Malaysia's economy, and manufacturing is its other pillar. Factories, especially around Penang, assemble and test computer chips for companies worldwide, while the state oil company, Petronas, produces oil and gas, and large plantations grow oil palms. Rubber, cocoa and pepper are traditional crops.",
    business: "Electronics are by far its largest export, with fuels, palm oil, chemicals and rubber gloves also important, and its biggest trading partners are China, Singapore and the United States. The currency is the ringgit. Well-known Malaysian companies include the budget airline AirAsia and the carmaker Proton, and the country is a founding member of ASEAN and a world leader in Islamic banking.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for George Town in Penang and the old port of Melaka, which together form a UNESCO World Heritage site known for colonial streets and street food, and for Kinabalu Park and Gunung Mulu National Park on Borneo, also UNESCO sites, with a towering mountain and giant caves. Others relax on islands such as Langkawi, dive at Sipadan or walk among the tea plantations of the Cameron Highlands.",
    history: "The Melaka Sultanate, founded around 1400, grew rich on the spice trade and helped spread Islam across the region. Melaka fell to the Portuguese in 1511, then to the Dutch and later the British, under whose rule Chinese and Indian workers came to work the tin mines and rubber estates. Malaya became independent from Britain in 1957, and in 1963 it joined Sabah, Sarawak and Singapore to form Malaysia; Singapore left in 1965."
  },
  "Maldives": {
    known: "The Maldives is known for luxury island resorts, where a single hotel often has a whole island to itself and villas stand on stilts over the lagoon. Its clear waters are famous for whale sharks, turtles and coral reefs, and it is Asia's smallest country by land area. In 2009 its government held a cabinet meeting underwater to warn the world about rising seas.",
    economy: "Tourism drives the Maldives' economy, earning most of its foreign currency and supporting many jobs, directly and indirectly. Fishing, especially for tuna caught one fish at a time with pole and line, is the traditional industry and provides the main export. Little can be grown on the sandy coral islands apart from coconuts and some fruit, so almost all food and fuel is imported.",
    business: "Its main exports are tuna and other fish, sold to buyers such as Thailand and European countries, while fuel, food and building materials come from countries such as the United Arab Emirates, China and India. The currency is the rufiyaa, though US dollars are widely used at resorts. The Maldives was a founding member of the South Asian regional group SAARC, and many resorts are run by international hotel chains.",
    tourism: "Most visitors come to relax on white sand beaches, snorkel and dive, staying in resorts or in guesthouses on inhabited local islands. Baa Atoll, a UNESCO biosphere reserve, is famous for gatherings of feeding manta rays at Hanifaru Bay. In Male, visitors see the Old Friday Mosque, built from carved coral stone in the 17th century, and try mas huni, a breakfast of tuna, coconut and chilli.",
    history: "The first settlers probably came from South Asia, and the islands were Buddhist until they converted to Islam in the 12th century, after which sultans ruled for centuries. The Portuguese held Male for about 15 years in the 16th century before being driven out. The Maldives came under British protection in 1887, won independence from Britain in 1965 and became a republic in 1968. The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami caused heavy damage."
  },
  "Mongolia": {
    known: "Mongolia is known as the homeland of Genghis Khan, whose Mongol Empire became the largest connected land empire in history, and for the dinosaur fossils of the Gobi Desert, including some of the first dinosaur eggs ever recognised by scientists. It is also famous for throat singing, in which one singer produces two notes at once, and for the round felt tents called gers.",
    economy: "Mining drives Mongolia's economy: coal, copper, gold and iron ore make up the great majority of its exports, most of them sold to China. Herding remains a way of life for many families, and the country has many times more sheep, goats, cattle, horses and camels than people, which makes it a major producer of cashmere. Severe winters called dzud can kill millions of animals.",
    business: "China buys most of Mongolia's exports, mainly coal and copper, while Russia supplies most of its fuel, so the country depends heavily on its two giant neighbours. The currency is the tögrög, also written tugrik. Oyu Tolgoi, one of the world's largest copper and gold mines, is run by the mining company Rio Tinto together with the government, and Mongolian cashmere brands such as Gobi sell knitwear abroad.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Naadam festival each July, with wrestling, horse racing and archery, and to stay in gers with herding families. The Orkhon Valley, with the ruins of the imperial capital Karakorum and the Erdene Zuu monastery, is a UNESCO World Heritage site, and travellers also head for the Gobi's dunes, Lake Khövsgöl and the eagle festivals of the Altai. Buuz dumplings and airag, fermented mare's milk, are traditional favourites.",
    history: "Nomadic peoples such as the Xiongnu ruled the steppe long before Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes in 1206 and launched conquests stretching from Korea to Eastern Europe. Later the Manchu Qing dynasty, which ruled China, controlled Mongolia for about two centuries, until Mongolia declared independence in 1911. With Soviet backing it became a communist state in 1924, and a peaceful protest movement brought democracy in 1990."
  },
  "Myanmar": {
    known: "Myanmar is known for its deeply Buddhist way of life, with monks in maroon robes a common sight, and for Bagan, a plain dotted with more than 2,000 ancient temples. It is also famous for its rubies and jade, and for thanaka, a pale paste made from ground bark that many people wear on their faces.",
    economy: "Myanmar's economy relies heavily on farming, which employs a large share of the population. Rice is the main crop, grown especially in the Irrawaddy delta, along with beans, peas and sesame. The country also has natural gas, jade, gemstones and teak forests, but the conflict that followed the 2021 military takeover has badly damaged the economy.",
    business: "Myanmar sells natural gas to Thailand and China through pipelines, and also exports clothing, rice, beans and minerals; China and Thailand are its biggest trading partners. The currency is the kyat, and Myanmar has been a member of ASEAN, the Association of Southeast Asian Nations, since 1997. Yangon, the former capital, remains the country's commercial centre and main port.",
    tourism: "Visitors watch the sunrise over Bagan's temples and explore Inle Lake, where fishermen row with one leg and farmers grow vegetables on floating gardens. Other sights include Mandalay, the last royal capital, and the Golden Rock, a gilded boulder balanced on a cliff edge. Bagan is a UNESCO World Heritage site, and a favourite dish is mohinga, a fish noodle soup.",
    history: "The Pyu people built some of the region's first cities more than 2,000 years ago, and the kingdom of Bagan united much of the land in the 11th century. Britain conquered the country in three wars during the 1800s and ruled it until independence in 1948. Military governments ruled for most of the decades after that; the army renamed the country Myanmar in 1989 and seized power again in 2021."
  },
  "Nepal": {
    known: "Nepal is known as the birthplace of the Buddha, who was born at Lumbini in the south of the country around 2,500 years ago. It is also famous for trekking and mountaineering, for the Sherpa people, many of whom work as mountain guides, and for its Gurkha soldiers, who have served in British forces for more than 200 years.",
    economy: "Nepal's economy rests on farming and on remittances, the money sent home by Nepalis working abroad. Rice, maize and wheat are the main crops, and farming supports a large share of the population, while millions of Nepalis work in India, the Gulf states and Malaysia and send home money equal to about a quarter of the economy. Fast Himalayan rivers give Nepal great potential for hydropower.",
    business: "India is by far Nepal's biggest trading partner, and Nepal buys much more from abroad than it sells. Its best-known exports include hand-knotted woollen carpets, pashmina shawls, tea and cardamom, and it sells surplus hydroelectric power to India. The currency is the Nepalese rupee, which is tied to the Indian rupee, and Kathmandu is home to the headquarters of SAARC, the South Asian regional group.",
    tourism: "Trekkers come for the trail to Everest Base Camp and the paths of the Annapurna region, reached from the lakeside city of Pokhara, and many try white-water rafting. Chitwan National Park, a UNESCO World Heritage site, shelters one-horned rhinos and Bengal tigers. Dashain and Tihar, the festival of lights, are the biggest festivals, and favourite foods are dal bhat, lentils with rice, and momo dumplings.",
    history: "In the 1760s Prithvi Narayan Shah, king of the small hill state of Gorkha, conquered the Kathmandu Valley and united Nepal. The country was never colonised, although it lost land after a war with the British East India Company from 1814 to 1816. A ten-year Maoist civil war ended in 2006, the monarchy was abolished in 2008, and an earthquake in 2015 killed nearly 9,000 people."
  },
  "North Korea": {
    known: "North Korea is known for its nuclear weapons and missile programmes, which have brought heavy United Nations sanctions, and for its huge military parades and mass gymnastics displays involving thousands of performers. It shares one of the most heavily guarded borders in the world with South Korea, and most of its people have no access to the global internet.",
    economy: "North Korea's economy is run by the state, which controls almost all farms, mines and factories. Mining and heavy industry are important, based on large deposits of coal, iron ore and other minerals, while farms grow rice, maize and potatoes; mountains, a short growing season and a lack of fuel and fertiliser often cause food shortages. Since a famine in the 1990s, small private markets have become part of daily life.",
    business: "Most of North Korea's recorded trade is with China. United Nations sanctions, imposed because of its nuclear and missile tests, ban many of its exports, including coal, iron ore, textiles and seafood, and a joint industrial park run with South Korea at Kaesong was closed in 2016. The currency is the North Korean won, and the country belongs to no major trade bloc.",
    tourism: "Tourism is tightly controlled: the few foreign visitors allowed in travel only on guided tours with official guides. Tours often include the truce village of Panmunjom on the border with South Korea and mountain areas such as Mount Kumgang and Mount Paektu, a volcano on the Chinese border. The tombs of the Goguryeo kingdom and the historic monuments of Kaesong are UNESCO World Heritage sites, and Pyongyang is famous for its cold noodles.",
    history: "The north of the Korean peninsula was part of the ancient kingdom of Goguryeo, which made Pyongyang its capital in AD 427. Korea was a Japanese colony from 1910 to 1945, then was divided between Soviet and American forces, and North Korea was founded in 1948. It invaded the South in 1950, starting the Korean War, which ended in 1953 with a ceasefire but no peace treaty."
  },
  "Oman": {
    known: "Oman is known for its dramatic scenery of desert dunes, rugged mountains and deep sea inlets, and for hundreds of old forts and watchtowers. It is also known for its quiet, neutral diplomacy: it keeps friendly ties with rival countries and has often hosted talks between them.",
    economy: "Oman has an oil and gas economy: oil and natural gas provide most of the government's income, although its reserves are smaller than those of its largest Gulf neighbours. Farming is small-scale and traditional, based on date palms in oases watered by ancient channels called aflaj, and fishing is important along its long coast. The government is working to expand tourism, shipping and manufacturing.",
    business: "Oil and natural gas are Oman's main exports, and most of its crude oil is sold to China; other important partners include the United Arab Emirates and India. It also exports fertilisers, chemicals and aluminium, and runs big ports at Sohar, Salalah and Duqm. The currency is the Omani rial, Oman is a founding member of the Gulf Cooperation Council, and the luxury perfume house Amouage was founded in Muscat in 1983.",
    tourism: "Visitors camp in the Wahiba Sands desert, swim in turquoise canyon pools such as Wadi Shab, hike on Jebel Shams, Oman's highest mountain, and watch green turtles nest at Ras al Jinz. Bahla Fort and the ancient beehive-shaped tombs at Bat are UNESCO World Heritage sites, and from June to September the monsoon turns the hills around Salalah green. Guests are welcomed with coffee flavoured with cardamom, served with dates.",
    history: "Thousands of years ago Oman was probably the land called Magan, which traded copper with Mesopotamia, and Islam arrived in the 7th century. The Portuguese held Muscat from 1507 until Omani forces drove them out in 1650. In 1970 Sultan Qaboos came to power, and until his death in 2020 he used oil money to build roads, schools and hospitals across a country that had very few."
  },
  "Pakistan": {
    known: "Pakistan is known for its passion for cricket, having won the Cricket World Cup in 1992, and for the towering peaks of the Karakoram range, including K2, the second-highest mountain on Earth. It is also known for Sufi qawwali music and brightly painted trucks, and it is a nuclear-armed country with one of the largest populations in the world.",
    economy: "Pakistan's economy is based mainly on services and farming, with a large textile industry. Agriculture employs a large share of workers and relies on one of the world's biggest irrigation networks, fed by the River Indus; the main crops are wheat, rice, cotton and sugar cane, and the country is famous for its mangoes. Money sent home by Pakistanis working in the Gulf states and Britain is a vital source of income.",
    business: "Textiles and clothing, such as towels and bed linen, make up most of Pakistan's exports, along with basmati rice, and the city of Sialkot is famous for hand-stitched footballs and surgical instruments. Its main trading partners include China, the United States, the United Arab Emirates and the European Union. The currency is the Pakistani rupee, Karachi is the business capital and main port, and China has invested in roads, power plants and Gwadar port.",
    tourism: "In the far north, visitors travel the Karakoram Highway past glaciers and high valleys such as Hunza. In Lahore the Mughal fort and the Shalimar Gardens are UNESCO World Heritage sites, as are the ruins of Mohenjo-daro and the ancient city of Taxila. Pakistani food is a highlight, from biryani and slow-cooked nihari to sizzling karahi, and the cities are known for their busy bazaars.",
    history: "Many empires ruled the region, and the Mughal emperors built Lahore's finest monuments in the 16th and 17th centuries. Led by Muhammad Ali Jinnah, Pakistan became independent from Britain on 14 August 1947 as a homeland for Muslims, when British India was divided amid mass migration and violence. In 1971 East Pakistan broke away as Bangladesh, and Pakistan has fought several wars with India, mostly over the disputed region of Kashmir."
  },
  "Philippines": {
    known: "The Philippines is known for its white-sand beaches and coral reefs, and for jeepneys, the colourful minibuses that crowd its streets. Filipinos love basketball and karaoke, and Filipino nurses, sailors and other workers can be found all over the world.",
    economy: "The Philippines has a services-based economy. Call centres and other outsourcing companies employ more than a million people, and money sent home by millions of Filipinos working abroad supports many families. Farms grow rice, coconuts, bananas, sugar cane and pineapples, factories assemble electronics such as computer chips, and the islands are rich in nickel and copper.",
    business: "Electronics, especially computer chips, are by far the biggest export, followed by products such as coconut oil, bananas and nickel ore; the main trading partners include the United States, China and Japan. The currency is the Philippine peso, and the country was a founding member of ASEAN in 1967. Filipino brands known abroad include the fast-food chain Jollibee and San Miguel beer.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for island beaches such as Boracay and El Nido in Palawan, for diving and for the Chocolate Hills of Bohol. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the rice terraces carved into the mountains of the Cordilleras, the Spanish colonial town of Vigan and the Puerto Princesa underground river. Festivals such as Sinulog in Cebu are highlights, and favourite dishes include adobo, roast pig called lechon and the iced dessert halo-halo.",
    history: "Spain colonised the islands from 1565 and named them after the Spanish king Philip II; its rule lasted more than 300 years. Filipino revolutionaries declared independence in 1898, but the United States took control after defeating Spain, and the country became fully independent from the United States in 1946, after Japanese occupation during the Second World War. In 1986 the peaceful People Power Revolution ended years of authoritarian rule."
  },
  "Qatar": {
    known: "Qatar is known for the Al Jazeera television network, founded in Doha in 1996, for its national airline, Qatar Airways, and for its role as a mediator that often hosts talks between opposing sides in regional conflicts. It is a small, flat desert peninsula jutting into the Gulf.",
    economy: "Qatar has an oil and gas economy: its North Field, which it shares with Iran, is the largest natural gas field in the world, and gas and oil bring in most of its export earnings and government income. Because the land is desert, most food is imported, although large dairy farms were built from 2017 so that Qatar could produce its own milk. Most people living in Qatar are foreign workers.",
    business: "Natural gas, cooled into a liquid so it can be shipped in tankers mainly to Asia and Europe, is the main export, along with oil, fertilisers and chemicals. The currency is the Qatari riyal, and Qatar is a member of the Gulf Cooperation Council. Hamad International Airport makes Doha a global travel hub, and Qatar's state investment fund owns major businesses abroad, such as the London department store Harrods.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Museum of Islamic Art, the National Museum of Qatar, shaped like a desert rose crystal, and the busy Souq Waqif market. Outside Doha, drives across the sand lead to Khor Al Adaid, the Inland Sea, where dunes meet the water, and the ruins of the walled trading town of Al Zubarah are a UNESCO World Heritage site. Popular foods include machboos, spiced rice with meat, and sweet karak tea.",
    history: "For centuries Qatar's people earned their living from pearl diving, fishing and trade, and the Al Thani family has led the country since the 19th century. From 1916 Britain handled Qatar's defence and foreign affairs, until Qatar became independent from Britain in 1971. Oil was discovered in 1939, and from 2017 to 2021 Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain and Egypt cut trade and travel links with Qatar in a diplomatic dispute."
  },
  "Saudi Arabia": {
    known: "Saudi Arabia is known for oil: it holds some of the largest oil reserves on Earth and is one of the biggest oil exporters. It is also known for the Rub' al Khali, or Empty Quarter, one of the largest sand deserts in the world, and for its tradition of welcoming guests with Arabic coffee and dates.",
    economy: "Saudi Arabia has an oil and gas economy: petroleum brings in most government income and most export earnings, and it feeds a large chemical and plastics industry. Farming is limited by the desert, but the country is one of the world's biggest producers of dates. A plan called Vision 2030, launched in 2016, aims to reduce the dependence on oil by investing in tourism, entertainment, mining and new cities.",
    business: "Crude oil and refined fuels make up most exports, sold mainly to Asian countries such as China, India, Japan and South Korea, followed by plastics and chemicals. Saudi Aramco, the state oil company, is one of the largest companies in the world. The currency is the Saudi riyal, and Saudi Arabia is a founding member of OPEC, the group of oil-exporting countries, and of the Gulf Cooperation Council, and belongs to the G20.",
    tourism: "Besides religious pilgrims, Saudi Arabia has welcomed holiday visitors since it began issuing tourist visas in 2019. Top sights include Hegra near AlUla, with its rock-cut Nabataean tombs, the old coral-stone houses of Jeddah and the mud-brick ruins of At-Turaif in Diriyah, all UNESCO World Heritage sites, as well as the coral reefs of the Red Sea. The national dish is kabsa, rice cooked with meat and spices.",
    history: "In the 18th century the first Saudi state was founded at Diriyah, where the Al Saud family allied with the religious reformer Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab. Abdulaziz Ibn Saud captured Riyadh in 1902, united most of the Arabian peninsula and proclaimed the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia in 1932; the country was never a European colony. Oil, discovered in 1938, made the kingdom rich, and a ban on women driving was lifted in 2018."
  },
  "Singapore": {
    known: "Singapore is known as a global hub for banking, shipping and air travel, and as a multicultural society where Chinese, Malay and Indian communities live side by side; English, Malay, Mandarin and Tamil are all official languages. It is also famous for the Merlion, a statue that is half lion and half fish, and for Changi Airport, with its gardens and giant indoor waterfall.",
    economy: "Singapore has almost no natural resources and very little farmland, so its economy is based mainly on services such as banking, insurance, trade, shipping and tourism. Manufacturing is also important, especially computer chips, medicines and oil refining. The country imports most of its food and some of its water, and recycles used water into ultra-clean water it calls NEWater.",
    business: "Singapore's main exports are electronics, especially computer chips, refined oil products, machinery and medicines, and its main trading partners include China, Malaysia, the United States and Indonesia. Its port is one of the busiest in the world, and many international companies base their Asian headquarters in the city. The currency is the Singapore dollar, the country was a founding member of ASEAN, and well-known brands include Singapore Airlines and Tiger Beer.",
    tourism: "Visitors come to see the Marina Bay skyline, the island resort of Sentosa and the Singapore Botanic Gardens, a UNESCO World Heritage site. Neighbourhoods such as Chinatown, Little India and Kampong Glam show its mix of cultures, and festivals include Chinese New Year and Deepavali. Food is a highlight, from chilli crab to Hainanese chicken rice, served at hawker centres, whose street food culture is recognised by UNESCO.",
    history: "In the 14th century a trading town called Temasek stood on the island, and in 1819 Stamford Raffles set up a British trading post there. Singapore became a British colony, was occupied by Japan during the Second World War and joined Malaysia in 1963. It became an independent country on 9 August 1965, when it separated from Malaysia, and its first prime minister, Lee Kuan Yew, led the government for more than 30 years."
  },
  "South Korea": {
    known: "South Korea is known for Hangul, the Korean alphabet created in the 15th century, for kimchi and Korean barbecue, and for taekwondo, the martial art that began there. It is also famous for its skincare and beauty products, its television dramas and its love of online gaming, with professional gamers followed like sports stars.",
    economy: "South Korea has an industrial and services economy built on exports. Its factories produce memory chips, cars, ships, steel, chemicals and batteries, and it is one of the world's leading shipbuilders. The country has few natural resources and imports almost all of its oil and gas, while farming, mostly of rice, now plays only a small part.",
    business: "South Korea's main exports are computer chips, cars, ships, refined oil products and machinery, and its main trading partners include China, the United States, Japan and Vietnam. Much of the economy is led by giant family-run business groups called chaebol, such as Samsung, Hyundai, LG and SK. The currency is the South Korean won, Busan is one of the world's busiest ports, and the country belongs to the OECD and the G20.",
    tourism: "Visitors explore Gyeongju, the ancient capital of the Silla kingdom, where Bulguksa Temple and the Seokguram Grotto are UNESCO World Heritage sites, and the volcanic island of Jeju with its lava tubes and beaches. Many take tours to the Demilitarised Zone on the border with North Korea, and in spring cherry blossom festivals are held across the country. Food lovers seek out bibimbap, Korean fried chicken and busy street markets.",
    history: "Korea was ruled for centuries by kingdoms such as Silla, Goryeo, which gave Korea its name, and Joseon. After Japanese colonial rule from 1910 to 1945, the peninsula was divided, and the Republic of Korea was founded in the south in 1948; the Korean War, from 1950 to 1953, left it in ruins. Rapid industrial growth followed, mass protests brought democracy in 1987, and Seoul hosted the Olympic Games in 1988."
  },
  "Sri Lanka": {
    known: "Sri Lanka is known for its love of cricket, having won the Cricket World Cup in 1996, for its elephants and leopards, and for sapphires and other precious stones. In 1960 it became the first country in the world to have a woman as prime minister.",
    economy: "Sri Lanka's economy is based mainly on services, such as trade, transport and tourism, along with a large clothing industry. Farming employs many people: rice is the main food crop, while tea, rubber, coconuts and spices are grown for export. Money sent home by Sri Lankans working abroad, especially in the Middle East, is another important source of income.",
    business: "Clothing is Sri Lanka's biggest export, made in factories that supply well-known Western fashion and sportswear brands, followed by tea, rubber products such as tyres and gloves, and coconut products. Its main buyers include the United States, the United Kingdom, India and European countries. The currency is the Sri Lankan rupee, the Port of Colombo is a major hub for ships crossing the Indian Ocean, and Dilmah is a famous tea brand.",
    tourism: "Visitors climb Sigiriya, a rock fortress with ancient wall paintings, and explore the ruined royal cities of Anuradhapura and Polonnaruwa, the Dambulla cave temples and the fortified old town of Galle, all UNESCO World Heritage sites. Kandy's Temple of the Tooth, said to hold a tooth of the Buddha, hosts the Esala Perahera festival with decorated elephants. Other draws are safaris in Yala, whale watching and rice and curry.",
    history: "Buddhism arrived in the 3rd century BC, and Sinhalese kings ruled from Anuradhapura. The Portuguese and then the Dutch controlled the coasts, and in 1815 the British took the whole island, which they called Ceylon; it became independent from Britain in 1948 and was renamed Sri Lanka in 1972. A civil war with the Tamil Tigers, who fought for a separate state, lasted from 1983 to 2009, and an economic crisis struck in 2022."
  },
  "Syria": {
    known: "Syria is known for its long history as a crossroads of civilisations, for great historic cities such as Aleppo with its massive citadel, and for crafts like damask cloth, named after Damascus, and Aleppo soap made from olive and laurel oil. It is also known for the civil war that began in 2011 and forced millions of people to flee their homes.",
    economy: "Before the war, Syria's economy relied on farming, oil and tourism. Its farms grow wheat, barley, cotton and olives, as well as the famous Aleppo pistachios, and the country produced oil and phosphates. More than a decade of war damaged or destroyed factories, farms and oil fields across the country, and many Syrians now depend on humanitarian aid and on money sent by relatives abroad.",
    business: "Before the war Syria exported oil, cotton, textiles, olive oil, fruit and vegetables, and Aleppo was a busy centre of textile making. International sanctions imposed during the conflict cut the country off from much of world trade, and many of them were lifted in 2025. The currency is the Syrian pound, and Syria belongs to the Arab League, which suspended it in 2011 and readmitted it in 2023.",
    tourism: "Visitors have long come for the Umayyad Mosque in Damascus, the Roman theatre at Bosra, the Crusader castle of Krak des Chevaliers and the ancient villages of northern Syria, all within UNESCO World Heritage sites. Fighting damaged many historic places, and in 2013 all of Syria's World Heritage sites were placed on UNESCO's list of sites in danger. Syrian food, such as kibbeh, fattoush salad and sweet pastries, is loved across the region.",
    history: "The ancient port of Ugarit produced one of the world's earliest alphabets, and Damascus was the capital of the Umayyad Caliphate, a vast Islamic empire, from 661 to 750. After four centuries of Ottoman rule and a period under French control, Syria became independent from France in 1946. Protests in 2011 turned into a long civil war, and in December 2024 the government, led by the same family since 1970, was overthrown."
  },
  "Taiwan": {
    known: "Taiwan is known as the birthplace of bubble tea, sweet tea with chewy tapioca balls, and for making bicycles and laptops sold around the world. Its status is disputed: it has its own government, chosen in elections, but the People's Republic of China claims it as part of its territory, and only a small number of countries officially recognise it.",
    economy: "Taiwan has an industrial and services economy driven by exports of electronics: its companies make computer chips, laptops, servers and electronic parts for customers around the world, as well as machinery and chemicals. Most people work in services, and farming is small but known for tropical fruit such as pineapples and mangoes and for oolong tea grown in the mountains. The island imports nearly all of its energy.",
    business: "Electronics make up most of Taiwan's exports, and its main trading partners include mainland China, the United States and Japan. Famous Taiwanese companies include the chipmaker TSMC, Foxconn, which assembles devices for many foreign brands, the computer makers Asus and Acer, and the bicycle maker Giant. The currency is the New Taiwan dollar, and Taiwan takes part in the World Trade Organization and the APEC forum under special names such as 'Chinese Taipei'.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Taroko Gorge, a deep marble canyon, Sun Moon Lake and sunrise over a sea of clouds at Alishan, as well as hot springs and the National Palace Museum in Taipei, home to one of the largest collections of Chinese imperial art. Taiwan is not a member of UNESCO, so it has no official World Heritage sites. Sky lanterns at the Lantern Festival and beef noodle soup are other highlights.",
    history: "Indigenous peoples have lived on Taiwan for thousands of years. The Dutch held part of the island in the 17th century, China's Qing dynasty ruled it from 1683, and Japan governed it as a colony from 1895 to 1945. In 1949 the Republic of China government, led by Chiang Kai-shek, moved to Taiwan after losing the Chinese Civil War; martial law ended in 1987 and the first direct presidential election was held in 1996."
  },
  "Tajikistan": {
    known: "Tajikistan is known for the Pamir Highway, one of the highest main roads in the world, and for its Persian heritage: Tajik is a form of Persian, while the other former Soviet republics of Central Asia mainly speak Turkic languages. It is also known for huge dams on its fast mountain rivers, such as the Nurek Dam, one of the tallest in the world.",
    economy: "Tajikistan's economy depends heavily on remittances, the money sent home by people working abroad: hundreds of thousands of Tajiks work in other countries, mostly Russia, and this money equals a very large share of the whole economy. At home, many people grow cotton, wheat and fruit, while industry is led by a big aluminium plant and by mining for gold, silver and antimony. Rivers supply almost all of its electricity through hydropower.",
    business: "Tajikistan's main exports are gold, aluminium and other metals and ores, cotton and electricity sold to neighbouring countries, and its main trading partners include Russia, China, Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan. The currency is the somoni, named after the medieval ruler Ismail Samani. Tajikistan belongs to the Commonwealth of Independent States, the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation and the World Trade Organization.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for adventure: driving the Pamir Highway, trekking in the Fann Mountains past the Seven Lakes and Lake Iskanderkul, and exploring the Wakhan Valley on the Afghan border. UNESCO World Heritage sites include the ancient settlement of Sarazm and Tajik National Park in the Pamirs. Nowruz, the Persian New Year in March, is a major festival, and the rice dish plov and qurutob, flatbread soaked in a yoghurt sauce, are national favourites.",
    history: "In ancient times the region belonged to Sogdiana and Bactria, conquered by Alexander the Great, and its Silk Road towns grew rich from trade. Tajiks see the Samanid dynasty of the 9th and 10th centuries as their first state. Russia took control in the 19th century and the area later became a Soviet republic; Tajikistan became independent from the Soviet Union in 1991, and a civil war followed from 1992 to 1997."
  },
  "Thailand": {
    known: "Thailand is known around the world for its golden Buddhist temples, its fragrant and spicy food, such as pad thai and green curry, and its tropical beaches and islands. It is also the home of Muay Thai, a combat sport fought with fists, elbows, knees and shins, and is often called the Land of Smiles for the warm welcome visitors receive.",
    economy: "Thailand has a mixed economy driven mainly by industry and services. Its factories turn out cars, pickup trucks, electronics and processed food, and tourism brings in a large share of national income. Farming still employs many people, and Thailand is one of the world's top exporters of rice, natural rubber and sugar, as well as a major grower of cassava and tropical fruit such as durian.",
    business: "Thailand's main exports are cars and car parts, computer parts and electronics, rubber, rice and processed food such as canned tuna. China, the United States and Japan are its biggest trading partners, and so many Japanese carmakers build vehicles there that it is called the Detroit of Asia. The energy drink Krating Daeng, which inspired Red Bull, is Thai. The currency is the baht, and Thailand helped found ASEAN in 1967.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Grand Palace and temples of Bangkok, the old royal capitals of Ayutthaya and Sukhothai, whose ruins are UNESCO World Heritage sites, and the mountains, villages and night markets around Chiang Mai. Islands such as Phuket and Koh Samui draw beach lovers, and festivals such as Songkran, the April New Year celebrated with water fights, and Loy Krathong, when candlelit floats are set on rivers, fill the calendar.",
    history: "The kingdom of Sukhothai, founded in the 13th century, is often seen as the first Thai state. It was followed by the powerful kingdom of Ayutthaya, which a Burmese army destroyed in 1767, and in 1782 the Chakri dynasty, which still reigns, made Bangkok its capital. A bloodless revolution in 1932 ended absolute monarchy, the country's name changed from Siam to Thailand in 1939, and the army has seized power many times since."
  },
  "Timor-Leste": {
    known: "Timor-Leste is known for its long struggle for independence and for being, with the Philippines, one of only two mainly Roman Catholic countries in Asia. It is also known for its coral reefs, part of the Coral Triangle, which hold some of the richest marine life on Earth, and for tais, colourful handwoven cloths made by women across the country.",
    economy: "Timor-Leste's economy depends heavily on oil and gas from fields beneath the Timor Sea. The money goes into a national Petroleum Fund, which pays for most government spending, but the main field has been running dry, so finding new sources of income is urgent. Most people live from small-scale farming, growing maize, rice and cassava mainly to feed their own families.",
    business: "Apart from oil and gas, Timor-Leste's main export is coffee, much of it grown organically in the cool mountains. Much of its food, fuel and other goods is imported, a large share from neighbouring Indonesia. The currency is the US dollar, used alongside local coins called centavos, and in 2025 the country became the eleventh member of ASEAN, the Southeast Asian regional bloc.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for diving and snorkelling, especially around Atauro Island, whose waters are among the most species-rich ever surveyed, and to watch whales and dolphins passing along the coast on their migrations. Hikers climb Mount Ramelau, the highest peak, before dawn to watch the sunrise from its summit, while museums in Dili tell the story of the resistance. Quiet beaches and cool mountain villages draw travellers too.",
    history: "Portuguese traders arrived in the 16th century, drawn by sandalwood, and Portugal ruled the eastern half of Timor for centuries while the Dutch held the west. Soon after Portugal withdrew in 1975, Indonesia invaded, and its occupation cost many tens of thousands of lives through conflict, hunger and disease. In 1999 the people voted for independence in a UN-organised referendum, and after a period of UN administration the country became independent in 2002."
  },
  "Turkey": {
    known: "Turkey is known for Istanbul, the great city once called Byzantium and Constantinople, and for the Ottoman Empire that ruled from it for centuries. It is famous for its food, from kebabs, meze and baklava to strong coffee and tea, for hand-knotted carpets and busy bazaars, and for the hot-air balloons that float over the rock landscapes of Cappadocia at dawn.",
    economy: "Turkey has a large and varied economy in which services, including a big tourism industry, make up the largest share, backed by a strong manufacturing base. Factories produce cars, buses, household appliances, textiles and clothing, steel and processed food. Farming remains important too: Turkey grows most of the world's hazelnuts and is a leading producer of apricots, figs, cherries and olives.",
    business: "Turkey's main exports include cars and vehicle parts, machinery, clothing and textiles, iron and steel, and gold jewellery, and the European Union, especially Germany, is its main export market. A customs union with the EU, in force since 1996, ties much of its trade to Europe. Well-known Turkish brands include Turkish Airlines, Beko home appliances and Ülker snacks. The currency is the Turkish lira, and Istanbul is the country's business and financial centre.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Istanbul's Hagia Sophia, Blue Mosque and Grand Bazaar, and for UNESCO World Heritage sites such as the ancient city of Ephesus, the white travertine terraces of Pamukkale and the fairy chimneys and cave churches of Cappadocia. Beaches along the Aegean and Mediterranean coasts around Bodrum and Antalya draw millions each summer, and many travellers come for Turkish baths, called hamams, and street food such as simit, a ring-shaped sesame bread.",
    history: "Turkey's Anatolian heartland holds Göbekli Tepe, one of the oldest known monumental sites, and was later home to the Hittites, Greek cities, Romans and Byzantines. Ottoman Turks captured Constantinople in 1453 and ruled a vast empire until defeat in the First World War. The republic that followed brought sweeping reforms, such as the Latin alphabet in 1928, and joined NATO in 1952. In 2023 earthquakes in the south-east killed more than 50,000 people."
  },
  "Turkmenistan": {
    known: "Turkmenistan is known for its huge reserves of natural gas, among the largest in the world, and for Akhal-Teke horses, a prized breed with a shimmering metallic coat that appears on the state emblem. Its deep red hand-knotted carpets are famous too, and their patterns, called guls, even feature on the national flag. Strict visa rules make it one of the least visited countries.",
    economy: "Turkmenistan's economy is dominated by natural gas, which provides most of its export earnings, together with oil and oil products. Farming depends on water from the Amu Darya river, carried across the desert by the Karakum Canal, one of the longest irrigation canals in the world, and the main crops are cotton and wheat. The state owns most large businesses and controls many prices.",
    business: "Most of Turkmenistan's gas goes by pipeline to China, its biggest customer, and the country also exports oil products, cotton and textiles. Turkey, Russia and China are among its main trading partners. The currency is the manat. Turkmenistan follows a policy of permanent neutrality, recognised by the United Nations in 1995, and stays out of military alliances and most regional blocs, though it is an associate member of the Commonwealth of Independent States.",
    tourism: "The few visitors who come explore the ruins of Merv, once one of the great cities of the Silk Road, the medieval minaret and tombs of Kunya-Urgench, and the Parthian fortresses of Nisa near Ashgabat, all UNESCO World Heritage sites. Other highlights include canyons and dinosaur footprints at Koytendag, shows of Akhal-Teke horses, national days honouring carpets and horses, and Ashgabat's vast bazaars.",
    history: "Ancient Parthia, a rival of Rome, had early royal centres here, and Merv grew into one of the largest cities on Earth before the Mongols destroyed it in 1221. Russia conquered the Turkmen tribes in the 1880s, and the land later became a Soviet republic. It declared independence in 1991 as the Soviet Union broke up, and its first president, Saparmurat Niyazov, built a vast personality cult before his death in 2006."
  },
  "United Arab Emirates": {
    known: "The United Arab Emirates is known for glittering skyscrapers, luxury hotels and giant shopping malls, for the busy airline hubs of Dubai and Abu Dhabi, and for artificial islands shaped like palm trees. It is also known for its desert traditions, such as falconry and camel racing, and for the oil wealth that paid for its rapid rise.",
    economy: "The United Arab Emirates grew rich on oil, and Abu Dhabi holds almost all of the country's reserves, which remain a major source of government income. The country has worked hard to diversify, so trade, logistics, tourism, finance, property and aviation now make up most of the economy, especially in Dubai, which has little oil of its own. Most workers are foreigners, since expatriates make up the large majority of residents.",
    business: "The UAE exports crude oil, oil products and gas, plus gold, aluminium and goods re-exported through Dubai's Jebel Ali port, the largest in the Middle East. India, China and Saudi Arabia are among its main trading partners, and well-known companies include the airlines Emirates and Etihad and the port operator DP World. The currency is the dirham, and the UAE is a founding member of the Gulf Cooperation Council.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Dubai's skyline and huge malls, desert safaris over red dunes, beaches and theme parks, and the old creek district with its gold and spice souks. Abu Dhabi offers the white marble Sheikh Zayed Grand Mosque, the Louvre Abu Dhabi museum and the Formula One Grand Prix on Yas Island, while Al Ain's oases and ancient tombs form a UNESCO World Heritage site. Winter is the festival season.",
    history: "In ancient times the region traded copper with Mesopotamia. In the 19th century local rulers signed truces with Britain, which then handled their defence and foreign affairs, so the area became known as the Trucial States. Oil exports began in 1962, and after Britain withdrew, six emirates formed the United Arab Emirates in 1971 under Sheikh Zayed, its first president; Ras al-Khaimah joined in 1972."
  },
  "Uzbekistan": {
    known: "Uzbekistan is known for its crafts, such as colourful ikat silks, embroidered suzani wall hangings and painted ceramics, and for plov, a hearty dish of rice, meat, carrots and onions cooked in a huge pot, which UNESCO lists as intangible cultural heritage. It is also one of only two doubly landlocked countries in the world, meaning all its neighbours are landlocked too.",
    economy: "Uzbekistan has a mixed economy of services, industry, mining and farming. It mines gold, copper and uranium, and irrigated farms grow cotton, wheat, fruit and vegetables, though the government has shifted land away from cotton towards food crops. Natural gas is produced mainly for use at home, and money sent back by Uzbeks working in Russia and elsewhere is a vital source of income for many families.",
    business: "Gold is usually Uzbekistan's most valuable export, along with cotton yarn and textiles, copper, and fruit and vegetables such as cherries and grapes. China, Russia and Kazakhstan are its main trading partners. Its car plants, run by the state-owned UzAuto, build Chevrolet-branded cars for Central Asia. The currency is the som, and since reforms began in 2016 the country has opened up to foreign investment and tourism.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Samarkand's Registan square, framed by three tiled madrasas, the historic centre of Bukhara, the walled old town of Khiva and Shakhrisabz, birthplace of the conqueror Timur, all UNESCO World Heritage sites. Travellers also enjoy lively bazaars, silk and ceramics workshops, nights in desert yurt camps, and Navruz, the spring festival when families cook sumalak, a sweet wheat dish, through the night.",
    history: "Ancient Sogdiana lay here, and the region later became a great centre of Islamic learning: al-Khwarizmi, whose name gave us the word algorithm, and Avicenna both came from the area. Timur made Samarkand the capital of a vast empire in the 14th century. Russia conquered the region in the 1860s and 1870s, and under Soviet rule irrigation for cotton drained much of the Aral Sea. Independence from the Soviet Union came in 1991."
  },
  "Vietnam": {
    known: "Vietnam is known for its food, especially pho noodle soup, fresh spring rolls and banh mi sandwiches, for the conical leaf hats called non la, and for water puppet theatre, in which puppets perform on a pool of water. Its cities are famous for streets packed with motorbikes, and the country is widely remembered for the Vietnam War, which ended in 1975.",
    economy: "Vietnam is mainly an industrial and services economy built on exports. Since the Doi Moi market reforms of 1986, foreign companies have built huge factories making smartphones, electronics, clothing, shoes and furniture, and the economy has grown quickly for decades. Farming still employs many people: besides rice and coffee, Vietnam is a top exporter of cashew nuts and black pepper, and fish and shrimp farming in the Mekong Delta is a big industry.",
    business: "Vietnam's main exports are phones and electronics, computers, textiles and clothing, footwear, furniture and seafood. The United States and China are its biggest trading partners, and South Korea's Samsung makes a large share of its phones in Vietnam. Well-known home-grown names include Vingroup, maker of VinFast cars, and Vietnam Airlines. The currency is the dong, and Vietnam belongs to ASEAN and has free-trade agreements with the European Union and many others.",
    tourism: "Visitors cruise among the limestone peaks of Ha Long Bay, explore giant caves in Phong Nha-Ke Bang, and wander the lantern-lit old town of Hoi An and the imperial citadel of Hue, all UNESCO World Heritage sites. Others take boats through the floating markets of the Mekong Delta, relax on central coast beaches, or come for the street food. Tet, the Lunar New Year, is the biggest festival of the year.",
    history: "For about a thousand years the Vietnamese lived under Chinese rule, until they won independence in 938. France colonised the country in the 19th century. Ho Chi Minh declared independence in 1945, and after Vietnamese forces defeated the French at Dien Bien Phu in 1954 the country was split into North and South. A long war involving the United States followed, ending with the North's victory in 1975 and reunification in 1976."
  },
  "Yemen": {
    known: "Yemen is known for Socotra, an island of strange plants such as the umbrella-shaped dragon's blood tree, for the jambiya, a curved dagger many men wear on their belts, and as the place where coffee drinking first took hold: its port of Mocha gave its name to a style of coffee. It is also known for a long civil war that caused a severe humanitarian crisis.",
    economy: "Yemen is one of the poorest countries in the Arab world. Before the war, oil and gas provided most export earnings and government income, but fighting and attacks on export terminals have largely halted those exports. Many people live from small farms and herding, growing sorghum, coffee and qat, a leafy plant chewed as a mild stimulant, while money sent home by Yemenis abroad and foreign aid keep many families going.",
    business: "Yemen's exports have shrunk because of the war, but have included crude oil, liquefied natural gas, fish and its prized speciality coffee. Saudi Arabia, home to many Yemeni workers, is a key partner, and much food and fuel is imported through the ports of Aden and Hodeidah. The currency is the Yemeni rial, and Yemen belongs to the Arab League but not to the Gulf Cooperation Council.",
    tourism: "The war has almost stopped tourism, but Yemen's UNESCO World Heritage sites include Shibam, a walled desert town of mud-brick tower blocks nicknamed the Manhattan of the Desert, the historic town of Zabid, the Socotra archipelago and the landmarks of the ancient kingdom of Saba at Marib. In calmer times visitors also came for terraced mountain villages, the old port of Aden and markets scented with frankincense and spices.",
    history: "Ancient kingdoms such as Saba, linked in legend to the Queen of Sheba, grew rich on the incense trade, and the Great Dam of Marib watered their fields for over a thousand years. The north became independent as the Ottoman Empire collapsed in 1918, while Britain ruled the south, around Aden, until 1967. The two Yemens united in 1990, and a civil war began in 2014, drawing in regional powers."
  },
  "Palestine": {
    known: "Palestine is known for holy places sacred to Christians, Muslims and Jews, such as Hebron and Jericho, and for its close ties to Jerusalem, whose status is disputed: Palestinians seek East Jerusalem as the capital of their state. It is also known for olive groves, embroidered dresses, the black-and-white keffiyeh scarf, dishes such as maqluba and musakhan, and for the long Israeli-Palestinian conflict.",
    economy: "Palestine's small economy rests mainly on services, public-sector jobs, trade and light industry such as stone quarrying and food processing. Farms grow olives, vegetables and dates, and many families have relied on work in Israel, money from relatives abroad and international aid. Israeli restrictions on movement and trade, which Israel says are needed for security, and repeated conflict hold back growth, and the war that began in 2023 devastated Gaza's economy.",
    business: "Palestinian exports include stone and marble, furniture, olive oil, dates and plastics, and most trade is with Israel, which controls most border crossings and collects many taxes on the Palestinian Authority's behalf. Jordan is another key partner. There is no Palestinian currency: the Israeli shekel is used most, alongside the Jordanian dinar and the US dollar. Ramallah and Nablus are the main business centres, and Nablus soap and Hebron glass are well-known crafts.",
    tourism: "Most visitors are pilgrims and history lovers. Palestine's UNESCO World Heritage sites include Bethlehem's Church of the Nativity, the old town of Hebron, ancient Jericho, one of the oldest towns in the world, and the olive and vine terraces of Battir. Others enjoy the old market of Nablus, famous for knafeh, the monasteries of the Judaean Desert and walks among olive groves. Gaza has been largely closed to visitors since 2007.",
    history: "Canaanites, Israelites and Philistines, who gave the land its name, lived here long ago, followed by Roman, Byzantine, Islamic, Crusader and Ottoman rule, then a British mandate. In 1948 Israel was founded and hundreds of thousands of Palestinians fled or were driven out, and in 1967 Israel took control of the West Bank, East Jerusalem and Gaza. Palestine declared independence in 1988, and the 1993 Oslo Accords led to limited Palestinian self-rule."
  },
  "Algeria": {
    known: "Algeria is known for its long and bitter war of independence from France, for raï, a popular music style born in the port city of Oran, and for its oil and gas. It is also known for couscous, which it shares as a heritage dish with its North African neighbours, for dates from its desert oases, and for passionate support of its national football team.",
    economy: "Algeria's economy is built on oil and natural gas, which provide the great majority of export earnings and a large share of government income. The state owns the energy giant Sonatrach and many other large companies. Farms in the fertile north grow wheat, barley, citrus fruit, olives and vegetables, and desert oases produce fine dates such as Deglet Nour, but the country still imports much of its food.",
    business: "Algeria sells gas to Europe through pipelines under the Mediterranean to Italy and Spain, and by ship as liquefied gas. Italy, Spain and France are among its main trading partners, while China is a major supplier of imported goods. Beyond energy it exports fertilisers, cement, steel and dates. The currency is the Algerian dinar, and Algeria is a member of OPEC, the African Union and the Arab League.",
    tourism: "Algeria receives relatively few tourists, but its UNESCO World Heritage sites include the Casbah of Algiers, the Roman ruins of Timgad and Djemila, the fortified desert towns of the M'zab Valley, and Tassili n'Ajjer, a plateau of stone formations and thousands of prehistoric rock paintings. Visitors also explore the Hoggar Mountains, Constantine, a city of bridges built over a deep gorge, and Mediterranean beaches, and enjoy dishes such as couscous and chorba soup.",
    history: "Berber kingdoms such as Numidia ruled here in antiquity, before Roman rule and the arrival of Islam and Arabic in the 7th century. The Ottomans controlled Algiers for about three centuries until France invaded in 1830 and later made Algeria part of France. A war of independence from 1954 cost hundreds of thousands of lives, and Algeria became independent in 1962. In the 1990s a civil war, the Black Decade, claimed many thousands more."
  },
  "Angola": {
    known: "Angola is known for semba and kizomba, music and dance styles that have spread far beyond Africa, and for kuduro, a fast, energetic dance music from the streets of Luanda. It is also known for the giant sable antelope, a rare animal found only in Angola that has become a national symbol, and for its striking landscapes, from desert coast to waterfalls and green highlands.",
    economy: "Angola's economy depends heavily on oil, which provides most of its export earnings and much of the government's income, and it is one of Africa's largest oil producers. Diamonds are the second major export. Yet many Angolans make a living from farming and small trade, growing cassava, maize, beans and bananas, and coffee, which once made Angola one of the world's biggest producers, is now grown on a much smaller scale.",
    business: "Most of Angola's oil goes to China, its largest trading partner, with India and European countries also important buyers. Diamonds and gas are other exports, and the state oil company Sonangol and diamond firm Endiama are the biggest names in business. The currency is the kwanza. Angola belongs to the Southern African Development Community and left OPEC in 2024, and the Lobito Corridor railway links its port of Lobito to mines further inland.",
    tourism: "Tourism is still small, but visitors come for the Kalandula Falls, among Africa's largest waterfalls, the eroded red cliffs of the Miradouro da Lua near Luanda, and the Namib Desert, home to the strange, long-lived welwitschia plant. Beaches along the Atlantic, the Tundavala gap in the southern highlands and Luanda's carnival also draw travellers. Mbanza Kongo, old capital of the Kingdom of Kongo, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.",
    history: "The Kingdom of Kongo and other states flourished here before Portuguese sailors arrived in 1482. Luanda, founded in 1576, became a major hub of the Atlantic slave trade, and in the 17th century Queen Nzinga of Ndongo and Matamba resisted the Portuguese for decades. Angola won independence from Portugal in 1975 after an armed struggle, but a civil war between rival movements, drawn into the Cold War, lasted until 2002."
  },
  "Benin": {
    known: "Benin is known for the Agojie, the women soldiers of the old Kingdom of Dahomey, for the colourful appliqué cloths of Abomey, and for its role in the Atlantic slave trade, remembered today in the coastal town of Ouidah. Despite its name, it has no link to the old Kingdom of Benin in what is now Nigeria: it was named in 1975 after the Bight of Benin, the bay on its coast.",
    economy: "Benin's economy relies mainly on farming and on trade through the port of Cotonou. Cotton is the leading export crop, making Benin one of Africa's largest cotton producers, and cashew nuts, soya beans, shea nuts and pineapples also matter, while maize, cassava and yams feed the country. Much of Cotonou's trade is re-exported, often informally, to its giant neighbour Nigeria, and services linked to the port are a big part of the economy.",
    business: "Benin exports cotton, cashew nuts, soya beans and shea butter, much of it to Asia, and trades heavily with Nigeria, while its port has long served as a gateway for landlocked Niger and Burkina Faso. The Dantokpa market in Cotonou is one of the largest in West Africa. The currency is the West African CFA franc, shared across the West African Economic and Monetary Union, and Benin also belongs to ECOWAS.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Royal Palaces of Abomey, a UNESCO World Heritage site, the stilt village of Ganvié, and the Slave Route in Ouidah, which ends at the Door of No Return memorial on the beach. Pendjari National Park in the north, part of a UNESCO site shared with Niger and Burkina Faso, is one of West Africa's best places to see lions and elephants. Each January Ouidah hosts a Vodun festival.",
    history: "The Kingdom of Dahomey rose in the 17th century, ruled from Abomey, and grew rich partly by selling captives to European slave traders. France conquered it in the 1890s, and the colony became independent as Dahomey in 1960. After several coups, army officers took power in 1972 and set up a Marxist-Leninist state, but in 1990 a national conference led a peaceful change to multi-party democracy that was admired across Africa."
  },
  "Botswana": {
    known: "Botswana is known for its wildlife, including the largest population of elephants in the world, and for being one of Africa's most stable democracies, holding regular elections since independence. It is also home to the San people, whose ancestors have lived in the Kalahari region for tens of thousands of years, and it is the setting of the popular No. 1 Ladies' Detective Agency novels.",
    economy: "Botswana's economy is built on diamond mining, which provides most export earnings and a large share of government revenue. Debswana, a joint venture between the government and De Beers, runs the main mines. Cattle ranching is the main type of farming, while tourism, financial services and copper mining are growing. Diamond money has paid for roads, schools and clinics, and part of it is saved in a national fund, the Pula Fund.",
    business: "Diamonds make up the great majority of Botswana's exports, and since 2013 De Beers has run its global rough-diamond sales from Gaborone. Other exports include copper, beef and soda ash. South Africa supplies most imports, while diamond hubs such as Belgium, India and the United Arab Emirates are major buyers. The currency is the pula, meaning rain, and Gaborone hosts the headquarters of SADC, the regional bloc of southern Africa.",
    tourism: "Most visitors come on safari. In the Okavango Delta they glide through reed channels in dugout canoes called mokoros, while Chobe National Park is famous for huge herds of elephants along its river. Others visit the vast white salt pans of Makgadikgadi, the Central Kalahari Game Reserve and the Tsodilo Hills, a UNESCO World Heritage site with thousands of ancient rock paintings. Botswana deliberately favours high-value, low-volume tourism.",
    history: "San hunter-gatherers have lived here for tens of thousands of years, and Tswana chiefdoms later settled much of the land. In 1885, wary of Boer and German expansion, Britain declared the Bechuanaland Protectorate, and in 1895 three chiefs travelled to London to keep it from Cecil Rhodes's company. Independence from Britain came peacefully in 1966 under Seretse Khama, and in 2024 power passed to the opposition for the first time."
  },
  "Burkina Faso": {
    known: "Burkina Faso is known for its crafts, including bronze figures cast by the lost-wax method, carved masks used in village ceremonies, and Faso Dan Fani, a handwoven cotton cloth worn with national pride. It is also known for music played on the balafon, a wooden xylophone, and for the International Arts and Crafts Fair held in Ouagadougou every two years.",
    economy: "Burkina Faso's economy rests mainly on farming and gold mining. Most people are small farmers or herders, growing sorghum, millet and maize for food and cotton for sale, and raising cattle, sheep and goats. Since the late 2000s gold has become by far the biggest export, while cotton, cashew nuts, sesame and shea are the main farm exports. Armed attacks in many rural areas have disrupted farming, trade and mining.",
    business: "Gold earns most of Burkina Faso's export income, followed by cotton, cashew nuts, sesame and shea butter, and as the country has no coast, much of its trade passes through the ports of Côte d'Ivoire, Ghana and Togo. The currency is the West African CFA franc. In 2025, together with Mali and Niger, it left ECOWAS, the West African economic bloc, after the three had formed the Alliance of Sahel States.",
    tourism: "Burkina Faso's UNESCO World Heritage sites include the Ruins of Loropéni, stone walls linked to the medieval gold trade, its ancient iron-smelting sites, and the W-Arly-Pendjari parks shared with Benin and Niger. Visitors have also come for the Sindou Peaks rock formations, the painted houses of Tiébélé, the sacred crocodiles of Bazoulé and the lively markets of Ouagadougou, though insecurity has made much of the country hard to visit.",
    history: "The Mossi kingdoms, centred on Ouagadougou, dominated the region for centuries. France conquered the area in the 1890s and made it the colony of Upper Volta, which became independent from France in 1960. In 1984 the young revolutionary leader Thomas Sankara renamed it Burkina Faso, before he was killed in a coup in 1987. Since 2015 armed groups have attacked many areas, and army officers seized power in two coups in 2022."
  },
  "Burundi": {
    known: "Burundi is known for its green hills, its coffee and tea, and its shoreline on Lake Tanganyika, one of the deepest and oldest lakes in the world. It is also one of the most densely populated countries in Africa, with most people living in the countryside rather than in towns.",
    economy: "Burundi's economy is mainly agricultural: most people are small farmers growing beans, cassava, bananas and sweet potatoes on hillside plots, largely to feed their own families. Coffee and tea are the main cash crops, and gold mining has grown in importance. There is little industry, and the country relies heavily on foreign aid.",
    business: "Burundi's main exports are coffee, tea and gold, and one of its best-known companies is the Brarudi brewery, maker of Primus beer. The currency is the Burundian franc. The country belongs to the East African Community and COMESA, and as it is landlocked, most of its trade passes through the port of Dar es Salaam in Tanzania.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the sandy beaches of Lake Tanganyika near Bujumbura, the tea-covered hills, and Kibira and Rusizi national parks, home to chimpanzees and hippos. In the south, a small stone pyramid marks a spring that is claimed to be the southernmost source of the Nile, and the Karera waterfalls are another popular sight.",
    history: "The Kingdom of Burundi was ruled by kings, called mwami, from about the 17th century. In the late 19th century it became part of German East Africa, and after the First World War Belgium ran it together with Rwanda. Burundi became independent from Belgium in 1962 and later suffered long periods of ethnic violence, including a civil war that began in 1993 and ended with peace agreements in the 2000s."
  },
  "Cabo Verde": {
    known: "Cabo Verde is known for its music, above all the singer Cesária Évora, famous as the barefoot diva, and for its sunny islands with beaches, volcanic peaks and a blend of African and Portuguese culture. It is often praised as one of the most stable democracies in Africa.",
    economy: "Cabo Verde's economy is mainly based on services, above all tourism, which brings in a large share of national income. The islands are dry and have few natural resources, so most food has to be imported. Money sent home by the large Cape Verdean community abroad, fishing and some light industry also matter.",
    business: "Its main exports are fish, especially canned and frozen tuna, along with clothing and shoes; most of its trade is with Europe, particularly Portugal and Spain. The currency is the Cabo Verde escudo, which is pegged to the euro. The country belongs to ECOWAS, the Economic Community of West African States, and Praia and Mindelo are its main business centres.",
    tourism: "Most visitors head for the white sand beaches of Sal and Boa Vista, popular for windsurfing and kitesurfing. Others climb the volcano Pico do Fogo, the country's highest peak, hike the green valleys of Santo Antão or join the Carnival in Mindelo. Cidade Velha, the first colonial town built by Europeans in the tropics, is a UNESCO World Heritage site, and the national dish is cachupa, a stew of maize and beans.",
    history: "The islands were uninhabited when Portuguese sailors reached them around 1460. They became a Portuguese colony and a key stop in the Atlantic slave trade, which shaped the population's mixed African and European roots. Cabo Verde won independence from Portugal in 1975, after a liberation struggle led by a movement it shared with Guinea-Bissau, founded by Amílcar Cabral; its first multiparty elections were held in 1991."
  },
  "Cameroon": {
    known: "Cameroon is known for its football: the Indomitable Lions were the first African team to reach a World Cup quarter-final, in 1990, and have won the Africa Cup of Nations several times. It is also one of the few countries where both French and English are official languages.",
    economy: "Cameroon has one of the most varied economies in Central Africa. Farming and forestry employ many people, producing cocoa, coffee, cotton, bananas and timber, while oil and gas from the coast bring in important export earnings. Services, including trade and transport through the port of Douala, make up the largest part of the economy.",
    business: "Its main exports include crude oil, liquefied natural gas, cocoa beans, timber, cotton and bananas, sold largely to Europe and Asia. The currency is the Central African CFA franc, shared with five neighbours and pegged to the euro. Cameroon belongs to CEMAC, the Central African economic and monetary community, and Douala is a key port for landlocked Chad and the Central African Republic.",
    tourism: "Visitors climb Mount Cameroon, one of Africa's most active volcanoes, and relax on the black sand beaches of Limbe or the beaches near Kribi, where the Lobé Falls tumble straight into the sea. The Dja Faunal Reserve and the Sangha Trinational rainforest are UNESCO World Heritage sites, and a favourite dish is ndolé, made with bitter leaves and peanuts.",
    history: "Portuguese sailors named the Wouri river Rio dos Camarões, river of prawns, which gave the country its name. It became a German colony in 1884 and was divided between France and Britain after the First World War. French-ruled Cameroon became independent in 1960; in 1961 the southern part of British Cameroons joined it, while the northern part joined Nigeria. In 2017, tensions in the English-speaking regions turned into an armed conflict."
  },
  "Central African Republic": {
    known: "The Central African Republic is known for the polyphonic singing of the Aka people, recognised by UNESCO, and for Dzanga Bai, a forest clearing where large numbers of forest elephants gather to drink and feed on minerals. It has also suffered decades of coups and armed conflict, and it is one of the poorest countries in the world.",
    economy: "The economy is mainly agricultural: most people farm cassava, groundnuts, maize and sorghum for their own food, while cotton and coffee are grown for sale. Logging in the south-western forests and digging for diamonds and gold, much of it by hand, bring in most export income, but conflict, poor roads and the lack of a coastline hold back growth, and the country depends heavily on foreign aid.",
    business: "Its main exports are timber, diamonds and gold, with smaller amounts of coffee and cotton. Being landlocked, it sends much of its trade by road through the port of Douala in Cameroon, and some goods travel by river boat along the Ubangi. The currency is the Central African CFA franc, pegged to the euro, and the country belongs to CEMAC, the Central African economic and monetary community.",
    tourism: "Few tourists visit because of insecurity, but the Dzanga-Sangha reserve in the far south-west, part of the Sangha Trinational World Heritage site shared with Cameroon and the Republic of the Congo, offers the chance to track western lowland gorillas. Manovo-Gounda St Floris National Park, another UNESCO site, once held great herds of savanna wildlife, and the Boali Falls near Bangui are a popular day trip.",
    history: "The region was long raided for slaves by neighbouring states before France colonised it from the late 19th century, ruling it as Ubangi-Shari within French Equatorial Africa. It became independent from France in 1960. In 1976 its ruler, Jean-Bédel Bokassa, declared himself emperor and held a lavish coronation the next year before being overthrown in 1979. In 2013 rebels seized power, setting off years of fighting between armed groups."
  },
  "Chad": {
    known: "Chad is known for its huge share of the Sahara in the north, including the Tibesti mountains, the highest in the Sahara. Scientists also know it as the place where a skull nicknamed Toumaï was found in 2001; about seven million years old, it belongs to one of the oldest possible ancestors of humans.",
    economy: "Chad's economy rests on two pillars: oil, pumped in the south since 2003, which brings in most export earnings and much government revenue, and farming and herding, which employ most people. Farmers grow sorghum, millet, groundnuts and cotton, and herders raise cattle, camels, goats and sheep. Chad is also one of the leading producers of gum arabic, a natural gum from acacia trees used in food and drinks.",
    business: "Its biggest export is crude oil, carried by a pipeline across Cameroon to the Atlantic coast, and it also sells live animals, sesame, cotton and gum arabic. The currency is the Central African CFA franc, pegged to the euro. Chad belongs to CEMAC, the Central African economic and monetary community, and, having no coast, relies on the port of Douala in Cameroon for much of its trade.",
    tourism: "The Ennedi Massif, a UNESCO World Heritage site, draws adventurous travellers with its sandstone arches, canyons and ancient rock art, and the Lakes of Ounianga, another UNESCO site, are a group of lakes, some fresh and some salty, in the middle of the desert. Zakouma National Park in the south-east is known for the recovery of its elephant herds after years of poaching.",
    history: "The Lake Chad region was the heart of powerful trading states, including the Kanem-Bornu Empire, which lasted about a thousand years, and later the kingdoms of Baguirmi and Wadai. France conquered the area around 1900 and ruled it as part of French Equatorial Africa until independence in 1960. Chad then went through long civil wars and a 1980s war with Libya over the Aouzou Strip, which an international court awarded to Chad in 1994."
  },
  "Comoros": {
    known: "The Comoros is often called the perfume islands for the scent of its flowers and spices, such as vanilla and cloves. Its deep waters are home to the coelacanth, an ancient fish once thought to have died out with the dinosaurs. The country also has a long record of political instability, with around twenty coups or attempted coups since independence.",
    economy: "The Comoros has a small economy based on farming, fishing and services. Most people grow food such as cassava, bananas and coconuts, while vanilla, cloves and ylang-ylang are the main cash crops. Money sent home by Comorians living abroad, especially in France, is a vital source of income, and the country imports much of its food.",
    business: "Its main exports are cloves, vanilla and ylang-ylang essence, while rice, fuel and most manufactured goods are imported. The currency is the Comorian franc, which is pegged to the euro. The Comoros belongs to COMESA and SADC, two African trade blocs, and is also a member of the Arab League, reflecting its close ties with the Arab world.",
    tourism: "Tourism is still small. Visitors come for quiet beaches, coral reefs and diving, especially around Mohéli, whose marine park protects green turtles that nest on its beaches, while humpback whales pass by in season. Others wander the old medinas of Moroni and of Mutsamudu on Anjouan, and try lobster cooked in vanilla sauce.",
    history: "The islands were settled by Bantu-speaking Africans and later by Arab, Persian and Malagasy traders and settlers, and for centuries local sultans ruled them. France took control in the 19th century. Three islands declared independence from France in 1975, while Mayotte chose to stay under French rule, though the Comoros still claims it. Tensions between the islands later led Anjouan and Mohéli to attempt to break away in 1997."
  },
  "Congo (Republic)": {
    known: "The Republic of the Congo is known for Congolese rumba, a music style it shares with its larger neighbour and which UNESCO recognised in 2021, and for the sapeurs, men who dress in colourful designer suits as a form of art and self-expression. It is also an important oil producer.",
    economy: "The economy depends heavily on oil, mostly pumped from offshore fields near Pointe-Noire, which provides the bulk of export earnings and government revenue. Forestry is another important industry, while most rural people farm cassava, plantains and other food crops. Falls in oil prices have caused serious debt problems in the past.",
    business: "Crude oil makes up most exports, and China is the largest buyer; timber is another major export. Pointe-Noire, the main port and business centre, is linked to Brazzaville by the Congo-Ocean Railway. The currency is the Central African CFA franc, pegged to the euro, and the country belongs to CEMAC, the Central African economic and monetary community.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Odzala-Kokoua National Park, one of the oldest national parks in Africa, and for Nouabalé-Ndoki, part of the Sangha Trinational World Heritage site. On the coast, Pointe-Noire has beaches and the nearby Diosso Gorge with its red cliffs, while in Brazzaville visitors see the Basilica of Saint Anne and the rapids of the Congo river.",
    history: "The Kingdom of Loango and other local kingdoms once traded along this coast, including in enslaved people shipped to the Americas. France colonised the area in the late 19th century after the explorer Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza, who gave Brazzaville its name, signed treaties with local rulers. The country became independent from France in 1960, had a Marxist-Leninist government from 1969 to 1991, and suffered civil wars in the 1990s."
  },
  "Congo (DR)": {
    known: "The Democratic Republic of the Congo is known for the mighty Congo river, the deepest river in the world, and for the vast Congo rainforest, the second largest tropical forest on Earth after the Amazon. Its music, especially Congolese rumba and the faster soukous, has shaped popular music across Africa.",
    economy: "Mining drives the economy: the country produces most of the world's cobalt, a metal used in rechargeable batteries, and is a major producer of copper, diamonds, gold and coltan. Yet most people live by farming, growing cassava, maize and plantains, and many remain very poor, as conflict and poor roads hold back development.",
    business: "Copper and cobalt make up most exports, and China is the largest buyer. The currency is the Congolese franc, though US dollars are widely used. The country belongs to SADC, COMESA and, since 2022, the East African Community, and Lubumbashi, in the copper-mining south-east, is a major business centre.",
    tourism: "Few tourists visit because of insecurity in parts of the country, but several of its national parks and reserves are UNESCO World Heritage sites. Virunga, the oldest national park in Africa, shelters mountain gorillas and active volcanoes, and the Okapi Wildlife Reserve protects the okapi, a forest relative of the giraffe found only here. Near Kinshasa, the Lola ya Bonobo sanctuary cares for bonobos, apes also unique to this country.",
    history: "The Kingdom of Kongo flourished near the Atlantic coast from the 14th century, and the Luba and Kuba kingdoms ruled the interior. In 1885 the land became the Congo Free State, the personal property of King Leopold II of Belgium, whose regime used forced labour and brutal violence; Belgium took it over as a colony in 1908. Independence from Belgium came in 1960, and wars from 1996 to 2003 drew in several neighbouring countries."
  },
  "Cote d'Ivoire": {
    known: "Côte d'Ivoire is known for its national football team, the Elephants, who won the Africa Cup of Nations on home soil in 2024, and for its lively dance music, such as zouglou and coupé-décalé, which is popular across French-speaking Africa. Its name, meaning Ivory Coast, comes from the ivory trade of early European merchants.",
    economy: "Farming employs a large share of workers and earns much of the export income: besides cocoa, the country grows coffee, rubber, palm oil, cotton, bananas and cashew nuts, ranking among the world's biggest cashew producers. Services make up the largest part of the economy, and industry around Abidjan, such as food processing and oil refining, is growing. An offshore oil and gas field discovered in 2021 began producing in 2023.",
    business: "Cocoa beans and cocoa products are the biggest exports, along with gold, oil, rubber and cashews, and much of the cocoa is shipped to Europe, especially the Netherlands. The currency is the West African CFA franc, pegged to the euro; the country is the largest economy in the West African Economic and Monetary Union and a member of ECOWAS. Abidjan is home to the African Development Bank.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the beaches of Assinie and Grand-Bassam, the old colonial capital, whose historic quarter is a UNESCO World Heritage site. Taï National Park, one of the last large areas of West African rainforest, and Comoé National Park, both also UNESCO World Heritage sites, protect chimpanzees and other wildlife. Food lovers try attiéké, a couscous made from cassava, served with grilled fish.",
    history: "Before French traders and soldiers arrived, the area held several states, such as the Kong Empire in the north and the Baoulé and Agni kingdoms. France made it a colony in 1893, and it became independent in 1960 under Félix Houphouët-Boigny, who led it for over thirty years. After his death, political tensions led to two civil conflicts, from 2002 to 2007 and after the disputed 2010 election."
  },
  "Djibouti": {
    known: "Djibouti is known as the main gateway to the sea for Ethiopia, its large landlocked neighbour, and as one of the hottest countries on Earth, with a landscape of volcanic rock, salt flats and desert.",
    economy: "Djibouti's economy is mainly based on services: its ports, railway and roads handle most of neighbouring Ethiopia's trade, and rent paid for foreign military bases brings in important income. Very little rain falls, so there is almost no farming and most food is imported. Banking, telecommunications and free trade zones also matter.",
    business: "Most trade is transit trade to and from Ethiopia, carried by road and by the electric railway to Addis Ababa, opened in 2016 and built with Chinese support. The currency is the Djiboutian franc, pegged to the US dollar since 1949. Djibouti belongs to COMESA and the Arab League and aims to be a trade and transport hub linking Africa, the Middle East and Asia.",
    tourism: "Visitors come to swim with whale sharks in the Gulf of Tadjoura, roughly from November to February, and to see Lake Abbe, where tall limestone chimneys let off steam at dawn. The coral reefs around the Seven Brothers islands attract divers, and Day Forest National Park shelters the rare Djibouti francolin, a partridge-like bird found nowhere else.",
    history: "The area was long home to Afar and Somali Issa herders and was linked to Arabia by sea trade. France took control between the 1860s and the 1890s, calling it French Somaliland and later the French Territory of the Afars and the Issas. Djibouti became independent in 1977, the last French colony on the African mainland, and later went through a civil war in the 1990s."
  },
  "Egypt": {
    known: "Egypt is known for its ancient civilisation of pharaohs, hieroglyphs, mummies and temples, and for the Suez Canal, one of the world's most important shipping routes. It is also the most populous country in the Arab world.",
    economy: "Egypt has a large, varied economy in which services, including tourism, trade and Suez Canal fees, play the biggest part. Industry makes textiles, food products, chemicals, cement and steel, and the country produces oil and natural gas. Farms in the Nile valley and delta grow cotton, wheat, rice and fruit, and money sent home by Egyptians working abroad, especially in the Gulf, is a major source of income.",
    business: "Its main exports include oil products, fertilisers, citrus fruit, vegetables and clothing, and Egyptian cotton is prized for fine sheets and shirts. The currency is the Egyptian pound. Egypt belongs to COMESA and the African Continental Free Trade Area and has a trade agreement with the European Union, while Cairo is a regional centre for banking, media and film.",
    tourism: "Beyond the pyramids, visitors take boat trips on the Nile between Aswan and Luxor, where the temples of Karnak and the tombs of the Valley of the Kings form part of a UNESCO World Heritage site. Many continue to Abu Simbel, whose giant temples were moved in the 1960s to save them from the rising waters of Lake Nasser. Red Sea resorts such as Hurghada offer diving over coral reefs.",
    history: "Ancient Egypt was united around 3100 BC and ruled by pharaohs for nearly 3,000 years, before passing to Persians, Greeks, Romans and, in the 7th century AD, Arabs. The Ottoman Empire later ruled it; the Suez Canal opened in 1869, and Britain occupied the country in 1882. Egypt became formally independent in 1922 and a republic after the 1952 revolution, and in 1956 it nationalised the Suez Canal."
  },
  "Equatorial Guinea": {
    known: "Equatorial Guinea is known for its oil and gas wealth, which since the 1990s has brought in large revenues, although much of the population still lives in poverty. The country is made up of a mainland region, Río Muni, and several islands, including Bioko and remote Annobón.",
    economy: "The economy is dominated by oil and natural gas, pumped from offshore fields since the 1990s, which provide nearly all export earnings and most government revenue. Before oil, the country relied on cocoa from Bioko and timber from the mainland. Plants on Bioko also process natural gas for export, and oil output has been falling as the fields age.",
    business: "Crude oil, liquefied natural gas and methanol make up almost all exports, sold mainly to buyers in Asia and Europe. The currency is the Central African CFA franc, pegged to the euro; the country belongs to CEMAC, the Central African economic and monetary community, and joined OPEC, the group of oil-exporting countries, in 2017. Malabo and the mainland city of Bata are the main business centres.",
    tourism: "Tourism is limited, but visitors explore Bioko's volcanic peaks and rainforests, and the beaches of Ureca in the south of the island are nesting grounds for several kinds of sea turtle. Monte Alén National Park on the mainland shelters gorillas and forest elephants, and Malabo's Spanish colonial buildings, such as its cathedral, recall the past.",
    history: "The Portuguese explorer Fernão do Pó reached Bioko in the 1470s, and in 1778 Portugal handed the islands and rights on the mainland coast to Spain. Spain built cocoa plantations on the island, then called Fernando Pó, and ruled the colony until independence in 1968. The first president, Francisco Macías Nguema, ruled harshly until he was overthrown in 1979."
  },
  "Eritrea": {
    known: "Eritrea is known for its love of cycling, a legacy of Italian rule: in 2024 an Eritrean became the first black African rider to win a stage of the Tour de France. It is also known for its traditional coffee ceremonies and its mix of nine recognised ethnic groups, each with its own language and traditions.",
    economy: "Most Eritreans live by farming and herding, growing sorghum, teff, barley and wheat and raising cattle, goats and camels, though droughts often spoil harvests. Mining has become the main source of export earnings, with copper, zinc and gold from the Bisha mine and others. The state controls much of the economy, and money sent home by Eritreans abroad is also important.",
    business: "Mineral ores, mainly copper and zinc concentrates, make up most exports, with China the main buyer. The currency is the nakfa, named after a town that was a stronghold in the independence war. The main ports are Massawa and Assab on the Red Sea; private business is small, and Eritrea is a member of COMESA.",
    tourism: "Visitors enjoy the cafés and boulevards of Asmara, then take the winding road down the escarpment to Massawa, a port of coral-stone buildings with Ottoman and Egyptian influences. The Dahlak islands offer diving and quiet beaches, and ancient sites such as Qohaito and Adulis recall the trading kingdoms of the past.",
    history: "The highlands were part of the Kingdom of Aksum, and the coast later came under Ottoman and Egyptian control. Italy made Eritrea a colony in 1890, and after British rule from 1941 it was joined in a federation with Ethiopia in 1952, then annexed in 1962. A war that began in 1961 led to independence, and a border war with Ethiopia from 1998 to 2000 left relations tense until a peace deal in 2018."
  },
  "Eswatini": {
    known: "Eswatini is known for its colourful royal ceremonies, above all the Umhlanga, or Reed Dance, held in late August or early September, when thousands of young women bring reeds to the Queen Mother, and the Incwala, the kingship ceremony held around December and January. The Swazi people have kept many of their traditions alive.",
    economy: "Eswatini's economy is closely tied to South Africa; services make up the largest share, with manufacturing and farming also important. Sugar cane, grown on large irrigated estates, is the most important crop; forestry and citrus also matter, and many families grow maize on small plots. Factories making soft-drink concentrate and sugar, and customs income shared through the Southern African Customs Union, are major sources of revenue.",
    business: "Its main exports are soft-drink concentrate, made at a large Coca-Cola plant, sugar, timber, citrus fruit and clothing, and South Africa is by far its largest trading partner. The currency is the lilangeni, pegged one to one to the South African rand, which is also accepted in the country. Eswatini belongs to the Southern African Customs Union, SADC and COMESA.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for wildlife reserves such as Hlane Royal National Park, with lions, elephants and rhinos, and Mkhaya, known for protecting rhinos, and for the hiking trails of Malolotja. Sibebe Rock near Mbabane, one of the largest granite domes in the world, and craft workshops such as Ngwenya Glass and Swazi Candles are also popular.",
    history: "The Swazi kingdom took shape in the 18th and 19th centuries under kings such as Sobhuza I and Mswati II, after whom the Swazi people are named. It became a British protectorate after the Second Anglo-Boer War and gained independence from Britain in 1968 under King Sobhuza II, who reigned for over 82 years. In 1973 he banned political parties, and in 2018 the country changed its name from Swaziland to Eswatini."
  },
  "Ethiopia": {
    known: "Ethiopia is known as the home of coffee, for long-distance runners, among them Abebe Bikila, who won the 1960 Olympic marathon running barefoot, and for fossils of early human ancestors such as the skeleton nicknamed Lucy. It is one of Africa's most populous countries, with an ancient Christian tradition and a large Muslim community.",
    economy: "Farming is the main source of jobs in Ethiopia: most people are small farmers growing teff, maize, wheat, sorghum and coffee, and the country has one of Africa's largest herds of cattle, sheep and goats. Services, now the biggest sector, and industry have grown fast, helped by new roads, industrial parks and large hydropower dams such as the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam on the Blue Nile, and Ethiopia also sells electricity to neighbouring countries.",
    business: "Main exports include coffee, gold, oilseeds, pulses and cut flowers, while China is a leading supplier of its imports. State-owned Ethiopian Airlines is one of Africa's biggest airlines and makes Addis Ababa a busy hub. The currency is the birr. Landlocked Ethiopia ships most of its trade through the port of Djibouti, and it belongs to COMESA and the African Continental Free Trade Area and joined BRICS in 2024.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the rock-hewn churches of Lalibela, the castles of Gondar, the ancient obelisks of Aksum and the walled old town of Harar, all UNESCO World Heritage sites. Trekkers head to the Simien Mountains, home to gelada monkeys, and to the scorching Danakil Depression. Travellers also enjoy the Timkat festival, traditional coffee ceremonies and injera with spicy stews.",
    history: "Ethiopia grew from the kingdom of Aksum, a trading power that adopted Christianity in the 4th century. In 1896 its army defeated Italian invaders at the Battle of Adwa, though Italy later occupied the country from 1936 to 1941. Emperor Haile Selassie was overthrown in 1974 by a military regime, the Derg, which fell in 1991, and Eritrea became independent from Ethiopia in 1993."
  },
  "Gabon": {
    known: "Gabon is known for its oil wealth and its vast rainforests, which cover most of the country and make it one of the most forested nations on Earth. With a small population and oil income, it is one of the richer countries in sub-Saharan Africa per person, although many people still live in poverty.",
    economy: "Gabon's economy is based on oil, which provides most export earnings and a large part of government income. It is also one of the world's biggest producers of manganese, a metal used to make steel, and has a large timber industry. Farming is small, so much of the food is imported, and the government is trying to build up wood processing and other industries.",
    business: "Oil, manganese and timber, including processed wood such as plywood and veneer, are the main exports, with China a leading buyer. The currency is the Central African CFA franc, shared with five other countries in CEMAC, the Economic and Monetary Community of Central Africa. Gabon rejoined OPEC in 2016, and the Nkok special economic zone near Libreville hosts timber-processing firms.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for wildlife in parks such as Loango, where humpback whales pass offshore and leatherback turtles nest on the beaches, and Lopé, a UNESCO World Heritage site with rainforest, savannah and ancient rock carvings. Ivindo National Park, also on the UNESCO list, has thundering waterfalls. Tourism is still small, so many places feel remote and uncrowded.",
    history: "Bantu-speaking peoples settled the area long before Portuguese sailors arrived in the late 15th century, and for centuries its coast was part of the Atlantic slave trade. France took control in the 19th century and made Gabon part of French Equatorial Africa. It became independent from France in 1960. One family governed for more than five decades until a military coup in 2023."
  },
  "Gambia": {
    known: "The Gambia is known for its sandy Atlantic beaches, its birdlife and the friendly welcome of its people, which has earned it the nickname the Smiling Coast of Africa. Apart from its short coastline, it is completely surrounded by Senegal, and English is its official language.",
    economy: "The economy rests on farming and services. Many families grow groundnuts (peanuts), rice, millet and cashews, and fishing is important along the coast and the river. Tourism, trade with neighbouring countries through the port of Banjul, and money sent home by Gambians working abroad are major sources of income, and these remittances are large compared with the size of the economy.",
    business: "Main exports include cashew nuts, groundnuts and fish, while much trade consists of goods re-exported to neighbouring countries. Key trading partners include Senegal, other West African states, China and India. The currency is the dalasi. The Gambia belongs to ECOWAS, the Economic Community of West African States, and a bridge over the Gambia River that opened in 2019 has made road trade with Senegal easier.",
    tourism: "Most visitors come for winter sun on the beaches near Banjul and for birdwatching, with hundreds of species recorded in this small country. River trips lead to Kunta Kinteh Island, a UNESCO World Heritage site linked to the slave trade and to the book Roots, and to the ancient Senegambian stone circles at Wassu. Many also visit the Kachikally crocodile pool and try domoda, a peanut stew.",
    history: "The area was part of the Mali Empire, then of smaller Mandinka and Wolof kingdoms. Britain colonised the river valley in the 19th century. The Gambia became independent from Britain in 1965 and a republic in 1970. It formed the Senegambia confederation with Senegal from 1982 to 1989, and one leader, who took power in a 1994 coup, ruled for over two decades until losing the 2016 election."
  },
  "Ghana": {
    known: "Ghana is known for colourful kente cloth, for Lake Volta, one of the largest artificial lakes in the world, and for a stable democracy in which power has passed peacefully between parties several times. It is also known for jollof rice and its love of football, with the national team nicknamed the Black Stars.",
    economy: "Ghana has a mixed economy in which services are the largest sector. Gold, cocoa and oil earn most export income: gold comes both from large mines and from many small-scale miners, and oil has been pumped offshore since 2010. Many people still farm, growing cocoa, cassava, yams, maize and plantains.",
    business: "Main exports are gold, cocoa beans and cocoa products, and crude oil, sold to buyers such as Switzerland, the United Arab Emirates, India and China. The currency is the cedi. Ghana belongs to ECOWAS, and Accra hosts the secretariat of the African Continental Free Trade Area. The port of Tema, near Accra, is one of the busiest in West Africa.",
    tourism: "Visitors come to see the forts and castles along the coast, such as Cape Coast and Elmina, UNESCO World Heritage sites where enslaved Africans were held before being shipped across the Atlantic, and many people of African descent come to trace their roots. Others enjoy the canopy walkway in Kakum National Park, elephants in Mole National Park, Ashanti culture in Kumasi and dishes like fufu.",
    history: "The Ashanti (Asante) Empire, rich in gold, dominated much of the region from around 1700. Europeans called the coast the Gold Coast, and Britain made it a colony in the 19th century after wars with the Ashanti. Led by Kwame Nkrumah, Ghana won independence from Britain in 1957, taking its name from the medieval Ghana Empire. After several military coups, it returned to multi-party democracy in 1992."
  },
  "Guinea": {
    known: "Guinea is known as the water tower of West Africa, because the Niger, Senegal and Gambia rivers all rise in its highlands. It is also famous for its music, including the balafon and kora traditions of the Mandinka, and for its national dance company, Les Ballets Africains.",
    economy: "Guinea's economy rests on mining and farming. Mining, above all of bauxite and gold, provides most export earnings, and the country also holds Simandou, one of the world's richest deposits of iron ore, now linked to the coast by a new railway. Yet most Guineans work in agriculture, growing rice, cassava, maize, fonio, bananas and oil palms, often on small family plots.",
    business: "Guinea is the world's largest exporter of bauxite, most of it bought by China, while much of its gold goes to the United Arab Emirates and Switzerland. Large mines are run by international companies, often with a share held by the state. The currency is the Guinean franc. Guinea is a member of ECOWAS and of the Mano River Union, with Liberia, Sierra Leone and Côte d'Ivoire.",
    tourism: "Tourism is small, but hikers love the Fouta Djallon highlands, with their waterfalls, cool plateaus and villages. Mount Nimba Strict Nature Reserve, shared with Côte d'Ivoire, is a UNESCO World Heritage site known for rare species such as a toad that gives birth to live young. From Conakry, visitors can take a boat to the Îles de Loos and enjoy live music and dishes such as rice with peanut sauce.",
    history: "Parts of Guinea belonged to the Mali Empire, and in the 18th century the Fula founded an Islamic state in the Fouta Djallon. Samori Touré led resistance to the French until his capture in 1898. In 1958 Guinea was the only French colony to vote against joining a new French-led community, and it became independent from France that year. Long authoritarian rule followed, and the army seized power in 1984, 2008 and 2021."
  },
  "Guinea-Bissau": {
    known: "Guinea-Bissau is known for cashew nuts, which it grows in huge quantities, and for its mangroves, rivers and wetlands. It is also known for gumbe, its popular music. Portuguese is the official language, but most people speak Guinea-Bissau Creole, called Kriol, in daily life.",
    economy: "The economy is mainly agricultural: most families grow cashews, rice, cassava, groundnuts and oil palms, and cashew nuts bring in most export earnings. Rice is the main food, but much of it is imported. Fishing licences sold to foreign fleets are another important source of government income, and foreign aid supports public spending.",
    business: "Raw cashew nuts are by far the main export, most of them shipped to India and Vietnam to be shelled and processed. The currency is the West African CFA franc, shared with several other countries in the West African Economic and Monetary Union. Guinea-Bissau also belongs to ECOWAS and to the Community of Portuguese Language Countries.",
    tourism: "Visitors come mainly for the Bijagós islands, a UNESCO biosphere reserve where sea turtles nest and fishing villages keep old traditions. Birdwatchers explore the mangroves along the Cacheu River, and anglers enjoy the Atlantic coast. In Bissau, people stroll the old Portuguese quarter of Bissau Velho and join the colourful carnival held before Lent.",
    history: "The area was once part of the Kaabu kingdom, linked to the Mali Empire. Portuguese traders arrived in the 15th century and used the coast for the slave trade, and it later became the colony of Portuguese Guinea. Independence fighters led by Amílcar Cabral waged a long war, and independence was declared in 1973 and recognised by Portugal in 1974. The country has since seen several coups."
  },
  "Kenya": {
    known: "Kenya is famous for its long-distance runners, who have won many Olympic and world titles, and for the Maasai people, known for their red cloth and beadwork. It is also known for its tea and coffee, its Indian Ocean beaches and the M-Pesa mobile money service, which changed how people pay for things.",
    economy: "Kenya's economy is mainly based on services and is one of the most varied in East Africa. Farming still employs many people and produces tea, coffee, cut flowers, vegetables and maize. Tourism, banking, telecommunications and a fast-growing technology scene are important, and Nairobi, nicknamed the Silicon Savannah, is a regional hub for business and international organisations. Money sent home by Kenyans abroad is also a major source of income.",
    business: "Main exports include tea, cut flowers, coffee, vegetables and clothing, sold to countries such as Uganda, the Netherlands, the United Kingdom, Pakistan and the United States. Safaricom, which runs M-Pesa, is one of the region's best-known companies. The currency is the Kenyan shilling. Kenya belongs to the East African Community and COMESA, and Mombasa is a major port serving much of East Africa.",
    tourism: "Besides safaris in parks such as the Maasai Mara, Amboseli and Tsavo, visitors climb Mount Kenya, a UNESCO World Heritage site, and relax on Indian Ocean beaches at Diani, Malindi and around Mombasa. The old Swahili town of Lamu and Fort Jesus in Mombasa are also on the UNESCO list. Many travellers try nyama choma, grilled meat, with ugali, a thick maize porridge.",
    history: "The coast was home for centuries to Swahili trading towns linked to Arabia, Persia and India. Britain took control in the late 19th century and made Kenya a colony in 1920, and settlers took much fertile land. The Mau Mau uprising of the 1950s fought colonial rule, and Kenya won independence from Britain in 1963, led by Jomo Kenyatta. Multi-party elections returned in 1992, and a new constitution was adopted in 2010."
  },
  "Lesotho": {
    known: "Lesotho is known for its Basotho blankets and cone-shaped mokorotlo hats, both national symbols, and for sturdy Basotho ponies used to cross the mountains. It is a kingdom whose king plays a mainly ceremonial role, and it has one of Africa's few ski resorts.",
    economy: "Lesotho has a mixed economy that depends heavily on South Africa. Many people farm maize and sorghum and keep livestock, and wool and mohair from sheep and Angora goats are key products. Clothing factories employ many workers, diamonds are mined in the highlands, and the Lesotho Highlands Water Project sells water to South Africa. Income from a shared customs union and wages earned by Basotho working in South Africa are also vital.",
    business: "Clothing made for international brands, diamonds, wool and mohair are the main exports, and South Africa is by far the biggest trading partner. The Letšeng mine is famous for producing very large, valuable diamonds. The currency is the loti, worth the same as the South African rand, which is also accepted. Lesotho belongs to the Southern African Customs Union and the Southern African Development Community.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for mountain landscapes, pony trekking and hiking, including the steep Sani Pass road and the Maletsunyane Falls, among the highest single-drop waterfalls in southern Africa. Sehlabathebe National Park is part of a UNESCO World Heritage site shared with South Africa, known for San rock art. Dinosaur footprints can be seen in several places, and in winter visitors can ski at Afriski.",
    history: "Lesotho was founded in the 1820s by King Moshoeshoe I, who gathered people fleeing wars in southern Africa at the mountain fortress of Thaba Bosiu. To resist Boer settlers, he sought British protection, and in 1868 the land became the British protectorate of Basutoland. It became independent from Britain in 1966 as the Kingdom of Lesotho, and later went through periods of unrest, including military rule from 1986 to 1993."
  },
  "Liberia": {
    known: "Liberia is known as Africa's oldest republic, declared independent in 1847, and as the first African country to elect a woman as president, in 2005. It is also known for its rubber plantations and rainforests, and for registering one of the world's largest fleets of merchant ships under its flag.",
    economy: "Liberia's economy is based mainly on farming, mining and forestry. Most people grow rice and cassava on small farms, while rubber, palm oil, timber, iron ore and gold are the main commercial products. The country has been rebuilding after years of civil war and the Ebola epidemic of 2014 to 2016, and many people depend on informal work and money sent by relatives abroad.",
    business: "Gold, iron ore, rubber and palm oil are major exports, and fees from its huge ship registry add further income. Firestone has run one of the world's largest rubber plantations here since 1926, and ArcelorMittal mines iron ore in Nimba County. The currency is the Liberian dollar, and the US dollar is also widely used. Liberia belongs to ECOWAS and the Mano River Union.",
    tourism: "Tourism is small. Surfers come to Robertsport, visitors walk the forests of Sapo National Park, home to pygmy hippos and chimpanzees, and others explore beaches along the Atlantic coast. In Monrovia, sights include Providence Island, where the first settlers from America landed, and the ruined Ducor Hotel. Local dishes include cassava leaf stew and jollof rice.",
    history: "Before the settlers arrived, the land was home to many peoples, including the Kpelle, Bassa, Vai, Kru and Gola. From 1822 the American Colonization Society settled freed African Americans on the coast, and Liberia declared independence from the society in 1847. Americo-Liberians, the settlers' descendants, dominated politics for over a century until a coup in 1980. Two civil wars between 1989 and 2003 cost many thousands of lives, and peace and elections followed."
  },
  "Libya": {
    known: "Libya is known for its oil reserves, the largest in Africa, and for spectacular Greek and Roman ruins along its Mediterranean coast. It is also known for the Great Man-Made River, a vast network of pipes carrying ancient underground water from the Sahara to coastal cities.",
    economy: "Libya's economy is based almost entirely on oil and gas, which provide nearly all export earnings and most government income. The state employs a large share of workers and has long subsidised fuel and food. Little farming is possible outside the coast and oases, so most food is imported, and years of conflict have damaged infrastructure and at times halted oil production.",
    business: "Crude oil and natural gas are the main exports, going mostly to European countries such as Italy, Germany, Spain and France, and also to China. The state-owned National Oil Corporation runs the sector with foreign partners, including Italy's Eni. The currency is the Libyan dinar. Libya belongs to OPEC, the Arab League and the African Union, and the Greenstream pipeline carries gas under the sea to Sicily.",
    tourism: "Libya has five UNESCO World Heritage sites: the Roman cities of Leptis Magna and Sabratha, the Greek city of Cyrene, the rock art of the Tadrart Acacus mountains and the old desert town of Ghadames. The Sahara also offers dunes, lakes and volcanic craters such as Waw an Namus. Since 2011, insecurity has kept visitor numbers very low.",
    history: "Phoenicians, Greeks, Romans, Arabs and Ottoman Turks all ruled parts of Libya. Italy seized it in 1911 and ruled harshly, and after the Second World War Britain and France ran it until it became independent in 1951, as a kingdom under King Idris. Muammar Gaddafi took power in a 1969 coup and ruled for 42 years until an uprising in 2011, after which rival governments emerged in the west and east."
  },
  "Madagascar": {
    known: "Madagascar is the world's fourth-largest island and is famous for vanilla, producing most of the world's natural vanilla. It is also known for its baobab trees and for a culture that blends African and Southeast Asian roots: the Malagasy language is related to languages spoken in Borneo.",
    economy: "Madagascar's economy depends heavily on farming: most people grow rice, the main food, and cassava, as well as cash crops such as vanilla, cloves, coffee and lychees. Mines produce nickel, cobalt, ilmenite and graphite, and clothing factories and tourism add jobs. Despite these riches, it remains one of the world's poorest countries, and cyclones and droughts often hit farmers.",
    business: "Vanilla, nickel, cobalt, cloves, clothing and seafood are major exports, sold mostly to France and other European countries, the United States and China. The currency is the ariary, one of very few currencies not divided into hundredths: one ariary equals five iraimbilanja. Madagascar belongs to the Southern African Development Community, COMESA and the Indian Ocean Commission.",
    tourism: "Visitors come to see lemurs and chameleons in parks such as Ranomafana and Masoala, part of the UNESCO-listed Rainforests of the Atsinanana, and Andasibe, home of the singing indri lemur. The Tsingy de Bemaraha, a forest of razor-sharp limestone pinnacles, and the Royal Hill of Ambohimanga are also World Heritage sites. Others dive off Nosy Be, watch whales at Île Sainte-Marie or photograph the Avenue of the Baobabs.",
    history: "Madagascar was settled by seafarers from Southeast Asia, probably from Borneo, and by people from East Africa. The Merina kingdom united most of the island in the 19th century, until France invaded in 1895 and made it a colony the following year. A major uprising against French rule in 1947 was brutally crushed, and Madagascar became independent from France in 1960. It has since seen several political crises and changes of power."
  },
  "Malawi": {
    known: "Malawi is known for Lake Malawi, which covers about a fifth of the country, and for its tobacco and tea. It is a densely populated, landlocked country where most people live in villages, and it has a reputation for being peaceful, having avoided civil war since independence.",
    economy: "Malawi's economy depends heavily on farming: most people are small farmers growing maize, the staple food, along with cassava, groundnuts and beans. Tobacco is the biggest cash crop and export earner, and tea, sugar and macadamia nuts are also sold abroad. There is little industry and the country relies heavily on foreign aid, while droughts, floods and cyclones can quickly cause food shortages.",
    business: "Tobacco, tea, sugar, groundnuts and macadamia nuts are the main exports, sold to Belgium and other European countries and to African countries such as South Africa. Tobacco is sold at large auction floors in Lilongwe and other cities. The currency is the Malawian kwacha. Malawi belongs to the Southern African Development Community and COMESA, and moves much of its trade through ports in Mozambique and Tanzania.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Lake Malawi National Park, a UNESCO World Heritage site where they snorkel and kayak in clear water, and for beaches at Cape Maclear and Nkhata Bay. Others hike Mount Mulanje and the Zomba Plateau or see elephants and rhinos in Liwonde National Park. The ancient rock art of Chongoni is another UNESCO site, and chambo fish from the lake is a favourite meal.",
    history: "Bantu-speaking peoples, including the Chewa, settled the region centuries ago and formed the Maravi kingdoms, which gave the country its name. The Scottish missionary David Livingstone reached the lake in 1859, and Britain later made the area the protectorate of Nyasaland. It became independent from Britain in 1964 as Malawi, under Hastings Banda, whose one-party rule lasted until multi-party elections in 1994."
  },
  "Mali": {
    known: "Mali is known for Mansa Musa, the 14th-century emperor often called one of the richest people in history, and for the Great Mosque of Djenné, one of the largest mud-brick buildings in the world. It is also famous for its music, including the kora and the desert blues of the north, and for its centuries-old manuscripts.",
    economy: "Mali's economy relies on farming, herding and gold mining. Most people grow millet, sorghum, rice and maize or raise cattle, sheep and goats, and cotton is the main cash crop, grown mostly in the south. Gold is by far the biggest export, and large mines are run with foreign companies. Much of the country is desert, and droughts and insecurity make life hard for many farmers and herders.",
    business: "Gold, cotton and livestock are the main exports, with much of the gold going to Switzerland and the United Arab Emirates. The currency is the West African CFA franc. Landlocked Mali moves goods by road through ports such as Dakar and Abidjan. It formed the Alliance of Sahel States with Burkina Faso and Niger in 2023, and all three left ECOWAS in 2025.",
    tourism: "Mali has four UNESCO World Heritage sites: the old towns of Djenné and Timbuktu, the Tomb of Askia in Gao, and the Cliff of Bandiagara, where Dogon villages cling to a huge sandstone escarpment. Travellers once also came for the Festival in the Desert and boat trips on the Niger. Since 2012, conflict has kept most tourists away, and several of these sites are on UNESCO's list of World Heritage in Danger.",
    history: "Mali was home to the Ghana, Mali and Songhai empires, rich from trans-Saharan trade in gold, salt and slaves. France conquered the area in the late 19th century and ruled it as French Sudan. Mali became independent from France in 1960, briefly in a federation with Senegal. A rebellion and a coup in 2012 led to years of conflict in the north and centre, and further military coups followed in 2020 and 2021."
  },
  "Mauritania": {
    known: "Mauritania is known for its iron ore, carried across the Sahara by one of the world's longest and heaviest trains, and for the rich fishing grounds off its Atlantic coast. It is an Islamic republic, and its people include Arabic-speaking groups and other African peoples such as the Fula, Soninke and Wolof.",
    economy: "Mauritania's economy is based on mining, fishing and livestock. Iron ore, gold and copper are mined for export, and fishing provides many jobs and a large share of exports. Most crops are grown along the Senegal River in the south, and many people raise camels, goats and sheep. An offshore gas field shared with Senegal has begun producing liquefied natural gas for export.",
    business: "Iron ore, gold, fish and copper are the main exports, sold mainly to China and European countries. The mostly state-owned company SNIM mines the iron ore and ships it from Nouadhibou. The currency is the ouguiya, which, like Madagascar's ariary, is divided into five parts rather than a hundred. Mauritania belongs to the Arab League and the Arab Maghreb Union, and it left ECOWAS in 2000.",
    tourism: "Few tourists visit, but those who do come for the Sahara: huge dunes in the Adrar region, and the ancient caravan towns of Chinguetti, Ouadane, Tichitt and Oualata, a UNESCO World Heritage site famous for old libraries of manuscripts. The Banc d'Arguin National Park, another UNESCO site, is where millions of migrating birds gather. Visitors also drink sweet mint tea, served in three rounds.",
    history: "Berber groups and later Arab tribes crossed the region on trans-Saharan trade routes, and in the 11th century the Almoravid movement began here before spreading to Morocco and Spain. France made Mauritania a colony in the early 20th century, and it became independent from France in 1960. It formally abolished slavery in 1981, the last country in the world to do so, and has seen several military coups since the 1970s."
  },
  "Mauritius": {
    known: "Mauritius is best known for its white-sand beaches, turquoise lagoons protected by coral reefs, and luxury resorts. It is also admired as one of Africa's most stable democracies and most prosperous economies, with a population whose ancestors came mainly from India, Africa, France and China.",
    economy: "The economy is based mainly on services. Tourism, financial services and information technology bring in much of the country's income, while clothing factories have grown since the 1970s in a special export zone. Sugar cane, once the backbone of the island, still covers much of the farmland but matters far less than it used to.",
    business: "Mauritius exports clothing, canned tuna, sugar and other goods, mainly to Europe, the United States and nearby African countries. Its currency is the Mauritian rupee. It belongs to the Southern African Development Community (SADC) and the Common Market for Eastern and Southern Africa (COMESA), and it has built up an offshore financial centre that international companies use to invest in Africa and India.",
    tourism: "Besides the beaches, visitors go diving and kitesurfing, hike in Black River Gorges National Park and see the Seven Coloured Earths of Chamarel. Two places are UNESCO World Heritage Sites: Aapravasi Ghat in Port Louis, where hundreds of thousands of indentured workers, mostly from India, arrived in the 1800s, and Le Morne, a mountain where escaped slaves once hid. The food mixes Indian, Creole, Chinese and French flavours.",
    history: "The island was uninhabited when Dutch sailors landed in 1598 and named it after Prince Maurice of Nassau. The French took over in 1715 and brought enslaved Africans to work on sugar plantations; Britain captured the island in 1810, ended slavery and brought indentured workers from India. Mauritius became independent from Britain in 1968 and a republic in 1992."
  },
  "Morocco": {
    known: "Morocco is known for its crafts, such as leather goods, carpets and colourful mosaic tiles, and for mint tea served with great ceremony. It also stands out for the snow-capped Atlas Mountains and for lying just across the narrow Strait of Gibraltar from Spain, a meeting point of Africa and Europe.",
    economy: "Morocco has a mixed economy that is mainly based on services, including tourism. Farming still employs many people, growing wheat, olives, citrus fruit and tomatoes, and the country holds most of the world's known reserves of phosphate rock, used to make fertiliser. Car making and aircraft parts have grown quickly in recent decades.",
    business: "Cars, phosphates and fertilisers, electrical parts, clothing and fresh produce are the main exports, and the European Union, led by Spain and France, is by far the largest trading partner. The state-owned OCP Group is one of the world's biggest fertiliser makers, and Tanger Med is one of Africa's busiest ports. The currency is the Moroccan dirham, and Casablanca is a leading financial hub for Africa.",
    tourism: "Visitors explore the old walled city of Fez, the Roman ruins of Volubilis and the fortified clay village of Ait Ben Haddou, all UNESCO World Heritage Sites. Many trek up Toubkal, the highest peak in North Africa, surf on the Atlantic coast at Taghazout or Essaouira, or ride camels among the dunes near Merzouga. Tagines, couscous and pastilla are favourite dishes to try.",
    history: "Amazigh (Berber) peoples have lived here for thousands of years, and after Arab armies brought Islam in the late 600s, dynasties such as the Almoravids and Almohads ruled empires reaching into Spain. The Alaouite dynasty, which still reigns, took power in the 1600s; in 1912 France and Spain divided Morocco into protectorates, and it regained independence from both in 1956. Morocco administers most of Western Sahara, whose status is disputed."
  },
  "Mozambique": {
    known: "Mozambique is known for the Zambezi, one of Africa's great rivers, which flows across the country to the Indian Ocean, and for marrabenta dance music and the intricate wood carvings of the Makonde people. Its national flag is one of very few in the world to show a firearm, an AK-47 rifle, as a symbol of defence and vigilance.",
    economy: "Most Mozambicans live from small-scale farming, growing cassava, maize and cashew nuts, so farming provides most jobs. Much of the export income, though, comes from mining and energy: coal from Tete province, titanium-rich sands, rubies, and natural gas from huge fields discovered off the northern coast in the 2010s. The Cahora Bassa dam on the Zambezi sells hydroelectric power to neighbouring countries.",
    business: "Coal, natural gas, electricity, heavy mineral sands and farm goods such as tobacco, sugar and cashew nuts are major exports, and South Africa, India and China are among its biggest trading partners. Its ports at Maputo, Beira and Nacala handle goods for landlocked neighbours such as Zimbabwe, Zambia and Malawi. The currency is the metical, and Mozambique belongs to the Southern African Development Community (SADC).",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the islands of the Bazaruto Archipelago and the reefs at Tofo, where divers swim with whale sharks and manta rays. The Island of Mozambique, the old capital of Portuguese East Africa with its fort and churches, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and Gorongosa National Park is known for bringing its wildlife back after the civil war. Seafood grilled with spicy peri-peri sauce is a favourite.",
    history: "Bantu-speaking farmers settled here about 2,000 years ago, and Swahili and Arab traders later built ports along the coast. The Portuguese arrived with Vasco da Gama in 1498 and slowly extended their rule; Mozambique won independence from Portugal in 1975 after a ten-year liberation war led by FRELIMO. A civil war followed until a peace deal in 1992, and in 1995 the country joined the Commonwealth, although it had never been a British colony."
  },
  "Namibia": {
    known: "Namibia is known for its huge, open landscapes and some of the darkest night skies on Earth, as one of the most thinly populated countries in the world. It is also famous for the shipwreck-strewn Skeleton Coast, desert-adapted elephants and lions, and the Himba people, whose women coat their skin and hair with a red ochre paste.",
    economy: "Namibia's economy rests mainly on mining and services. Diamonds, many of them recovered from the seabed off the coast, uranium and gold earn much of its export income, while fishing, cattle ranching and tourism are also important. Although farming supports many families, the dry climate means much of the country's food is imported, largely from South Africa.",
    business: "Diamonds, uranium, gold, copper, fish and beef are leading exports, and South Africa is the largest trading partner, with China and the European Union also important. The Namibian dollar is worth exactly one South African rand, and rand notes are accepted too. Namibia belongs to the Southern African Customs Union, the world's oldest customs union, and to SADC, and Walvis Bay is its main port.",
    tourism: "Visitors track black rhinos, elephants and lions around the waterholes of Etosha National Park and gaze into Fish River Canyon, one of the largest canyons in Africa. The ancient rock engravings of Twyfelfontein and the Namib Sand Sea, with the dunes of Sossusvlei, are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. Swakopmund offers sandboarding and German-style cafés, and grilled game meat is a local favourite.",
    history: "San peoples have lived here for thousands of years, later joined by Nama, Herero, Ovambo and other groups. Germany colonised the territory in 1884 as German South West Africa, and from 1904 to 1908 its troops killed tens of thousands of Herero and Nama people, an atrocity widely called the first genocide of the 20th century. South Africa took control in 1915, and Namibia won independence from South Africa in 1990."
  },
  "Niger": {
    known: "Niger is the largest country in West Africa, and the Sahara covers most of its land. It is known for the camel caravans and silver jewellery of the Tuareg people and for the Cure Salée, a gathering of nomadic herders at the end of the rainy season, where young Wodaabe men in elaborate make-up dance to win admirers.",
    economy: "Niger's economy is mainly agricultural: most people grow millet, sorghum, cowpeas and onions or herd cattle, goats and camels, though droughts often damage harvests. Uranium, mined near Arlit in the north, has long been a major export, and in 2024 the country began exporting crude oil through a long pipeline to the coast of Benin. Gold is also mined.",
    business: "Uranium, crude oil, gold, onions and livestock are among the main exports, and much trade crosses the long border with Nigeria. French companies long mined the uranium, while Chinese firms developed the oil fields. Niger uses the West African CFA franc, and in January 2025 it left the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS), together with Mali and Burkina Faso, its partners in the Alliance of Sahel States.",
    tourism: "Visitors come to see the old town of Agadez, with its towering mud-brick minaret, and the Aïr and Ténéré Natural Reserves, both UNESCO World Heritage Sites. Near Kouré, not far from Niamey, they can walk among the last wild West African giraffes, and W National Park, shared with Benin and Burkina Faso, shelters elephants and buffalo. Local food includes tuwo, a thick millet or sorghum paste, and kilishi, a spicy dried meat.",
    history: "The region was crossed by trans-Saharan trade routes and fell partly within great empires such as Songhai and Kanem-Bornu, while the Hausa city states and the Tuareg sultanate of Agadez flourished. France conquered it around 1900 and made it part of French West Africa; Niger became independent from France in 1960. Since then it has seen several military takeovers, including one in 2023."
  },
  "Nigeria": {
    known: "Nigeria is known around the world for Afrobeats music and for jollof rice, the subject of a friendly rivalry with Ghana. It is also famous for writers such as Chinua Achebe and Wole Soyinka, the first African from south of the Sahara to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, and for Lagos, one of Africa's largest and liveliest cities.",
    economy: "Nigeria has one of the largest economies in Africa, based mainly on services, farming and oil. Oil and gas from the Niger Delta provide most export earnings and much of the government's income, while farming employs a large share of workers, producing cassava, yams, maize, sorghum and cocoa. Banking, telecommunications, trade and film have grown fast.",
    business: "Crude oil and liquefied natural gas make up most exports, alongside cocoa, sesame seeds and fertiliser, and India, Europe and China are major trading partners. Well-known businesses include the Dangote Group, which makes cement and runs a giant oil refinery near Lagos, and tech start-ups such as Flutterwave. The currency is the naira, and Nigeria belongs to the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS), whose headquarters are in Abuja.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Lagos's beaches, art galleries and nightlife, and for festivals such as the Durbar horse parades held in northern cities at the end of Ramadan. The Osun-Osogbo Sacred Grove and the Sukur Cultural Landscape in the mountains near Cameroon are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. Other highlights include Yankari National Park, the Obudu Plateau and suya, spicy grilled meat on skewers.",
    history: "The Nok culture, famous for its terracotta sculptures, flourished here over 2,000 years ago, followed by states such as Ife, Oyo, the Kingdom of Benin, Kanem-Bornu and the Hausa city states. Britain took control step by step in the 1800s and united the region as one colony, Nigeria, in 1914. Independence from Britain came in 1960, and a civil war over the breakaway state of Biafra followed from 1967 to 1970."
  },
  "Rwanda": {
    known: "Rwanda is known for its rebuilding after the 1994 genocide against the Tutsi, and for Umuganda, a monthly day when citizens join in community work. It is also known for having one of the highest shares of women in parliament in the world, for coffee and tea grown on steep green hillsides, and for Intore dancers in traditional dress.",
    economy: "Rwanda's economy is based mainly on farming and services. Most people farm small hillside plots, growing bananas, beans, sweet potatoes and cassava, while coffee and tea are the main cash crops. Tourism, especially gorilla trekking, earns much foreign currency, and mining of tin, tantalum and tungsten ores adds to exports.",
    business: "Rwanda's main exports are coffee, tea, minerals and farm produce, and because it is landlocked most of its trade passes through ports in Kenya and Tanzania. Kigali promotes itself as a business and conference hub, with a large convention centre and a quick process for registering companies. The currency is the Rwandan franc, and Rwanda belongs to the East African Community and the Commonwealth.",
    tourism: "Visitors trek to see mountain gorillas and golden monkeys in Volcanoes National Park, track chimpanzees in Nyungwe Forest, and go on safari in Akagera, where lions and rhinos have been brought back. Nyungwe and four genocide memorial sites, including the Kigali Genocide Memorial, are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. Lake Kivu is popular for beaches, kayaking and boat trips.",
    history: "The Kingdom of Rwanda, ruled by kings called mwami, grew powerful over several centuries before Germany colonised the area in the 1890s; Belgium took over after the First World War and hardened the divisions between Hutu and Tutsi. Rwanda became independent from Belgium in 1962. In 1994, Hutu extremists killed hundreds of thousands of people, mostly Tutsi, in about 100 days, until the Rwandan Patriotic Front ended the genocide."
  },
  "Sao Tome and Principe": {
    known: "São Tomé and Príncipe is known for its rainforests full of birds, orchids and other species found nowhere else, and for beaches where sea turtles come ashore to nest. The island of Príncipe is a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve, and the islands' relaxed pace of life is summed up by the local saying leve-leve, meaning slowly, slowly.",
    economy: "The economy is small and relies mainly on farming, services, tourism and foreign aid. Cocoa is still the main export, alongside coffee, coconut products and palm oil, and many families live from fishing or small farms. Much of the food and nearly all fuel must be imported, so the country depends heavily on help from donors.",
    business: "Cocoa beans make up most exports, sold mainly to Europe, and a few local producers make prized chocolate on the islands. Portugal is the main source of imports. The currency is the dobra, which is tied to the euro, and the country belongs to the Economic Community of Central African States and the Community of Portuguese Language Countries.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for quiet beaches, rainforest walks in Obô Natural Park and the climb up Pico de São Tomé, the highest peak. Old cocoa estates called roças, some now guesthouses, show the colonial past, and at Roça Sundy on Príncipe visitors can see where Arthur Eddington watched the 1919 solar eclipse that helped confirm Einstein's theory of relativity. Calulu, a stew of fish or meat with greens, is the national dish.",
    history: "The islands were uninhabited until Portuguese sailors arrived around 1470, and Portugal settled them with colonists and enslaved Africans to grow sugar. Later, cocoa plantations relied on forced contract labour, which led British chocolate makers to boycott São Tomé cocoa in 1909, and a massacre by colonial forces at Batepá in 1953 is still remembered. Independence from Portugal came in 1975."
  },
  "Senegal": {
    known: "Senegal is known for its lively music, especially mbalax with its fast sabar drums, and for traditional wrestling, one of the country's most popular sports. It is also known for its long record of peaceful elections and handovers of power, and for thieboudienne, a dish of fish, rice and vegetables that UNESCO lists as intangible cultural heritage.",
    economy: "Senegal's economy is mainly based on services, such as trade, transport and telecommunications, while farming employs many people. Groundnuts (peanuts), millet, rice and vegetables are key crops, and fishing is important along the long Atlantic coast. Phosphates and gold are mined, and oil and gas production began offshore in 2024, adding a new source of income.",
    business: "Gold, fish, phosphoric acid for fertiliser, groundnuts and, more recently, crude oil are major exports, and neighbouring Mali, which ships much of its trade through the port of Dakar, is a key partner. Senegal uses the West African CFA franc and belongs to the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) and the West African Economic and Monetary Union. Dakar is a regional hub for banking, shipping and international organisations.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Gorée Island, a UNESCO World Heritage Site and a symbol of the Atlantic slave trade, and for the colonial town of Saint-Louis, also on the UNESCO list. Lake Retba, whose water can turn pink, the birdlife of Djoudj National Bird Sanctuary and the mangroves of the Saloum Delta are popular, as are the beaches of Casamance. Chicken yassa, cooked with lemon and onions, is a favourite dish.",
    history: "Parts of the region belonged to great West African empires such as Mali and the Jolof Empire, and Islam spread here from the 11th century. Portuguese, Dutch, British and French traders arrived from the 1400s, and France made Dakar the capital of French West Africa in 1902. Senegal became independent from France in 1960, briefly as part of the Mali Federation, and its first president was the poet Léopold Sédar Senghor."
  },
  "Seychelles": {
    known: "Seychelles is known for dazzling beaches framed by huge granite boulders, as its inner islands are rare granite islands in the middle of an ocean, pieces of an ancient continent. It is also one of Africa's wealthiest countries per person, and it protects about half of its land and nearly a third of its sea area for nature.",
    economy: "The economy is based mainly on tourism and fishing. Tourism brings in most foreign earnings and a large share of jobs, while tuna fishing and canning form the other main industry, since Seychelles controls a huge area of the Indian Ocean. Farming is limited by the small, rocky islands, so most food has to be imported.",
    business: "Canned tuna and frozen fish are the main goods exports, sold mostly to Europe, and the islands also offer offshore company and financial services. The currency is the Seychelles rupee. Seychelles belongs to regional groups such as the Southern African Development Community (SADC) and the Indian Ocean Commission, and Port Victoria is one of the busiest tuna ports in the Indian Ocean.",
    tourism: "Visitors swim and snorkel at beaches such as Anse Source d'Argent on La Digue and Anse Lazio on Praslin. The Vallée de Mai on Praslin, home of the coco de mer palm, and Aldabra Atoll, one of the world's largest coral atolls with about 100,000 giant tortoises, are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. Creole food mixes fish, coconut, curry and chilli.",
    history: "The islands were uninhabited until French settlers arrived in 1770, bringing enslaved Africans to grow crops such as cotton and spices. Britain took control during the Napoleonic Wars and ruled the islands from Mauritius until they became a separate colony in 1903. Seychelles became independent from Britain in 1976; after a coup in 1977 it became a one-party state, and multiparty democracy returned in 1993."
  },
  "Sierra Leone": {
    known: "Sierra Leone is known for Krio, an English-based creole language spoken across the country, and for Freetown's vast natural harbour, one of the largest in the world. The phrase blood diamonds became linked with it after rebel groups used diamond money to fund the civil war, and it is also remembered for the Ebola epidemic of 2014 to 2016.",
    economy: "Sierra Leone's economy is largely agricultural: most people farm, growing rice, the main food, along with cassava, cocoa, coffee and palm oil. Mining earns most export income, from iron ore, diamonds, rutile (a titanium mineral), bauxite and gold, and fishing is also important. The country is still recovering from war and the Ebola epidemic, and many people live in poverty.",
    business: "Iron ore, diamonds, rutile, bauxite, cocoa and fish are key exports; China is a leading buyer of its minerals, while Antwerp in Belgium is a traditional hub for its diamonds. The currency is the leone, which dropped three zeros in 2022. Sierra Leone belongs to the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) and to the Mano River Union with Guinea, Liberia and Côte d'Ivoire.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the white-sand beaches of the Freetown Peninsula, such as River Number Two and Tokeh, and for the Banana Islands and Turtle Islands. Tiwai Island is known for its many monkey species and pygmy hippos, and near Freetown the Tacugama sanctuary cares for rescued chimpanzees. Bunce Island, a former slave-trading fort, draws visitors, especially African Americans tracing their roots.",
    history: "The Temne, Mende and other peoples lived here long before Portuguese sailors named the coast in 1462. Freetown became a British crown colony in 1808, and the interior was made a British protectorate in 1896. Sierra Leone became independent from Britain in 1961; a brutal civil war from 1991 to 2002 ended with help from United Nations peacekeepers and British troops."
  },
  "Somalia": {
    known: "Somalia is known for its nomadic camel herders, as it has one of the largest camel populations in the world, and for a rich tradition of oral poetry that has earned it the name nation of poets. Many Somalis also live abroad, with large communities in cities such as Nairobi, London and Minneapolis.",
    economy: "Somalia's economy rests mainly on livestock herding, farming and services, and money sent home by Somalis abroad, called remittances, is a vital lifeline. Camels, goats, sheep and cattle are the main exports, mostly shipped to Gulf countries, while bananas, sorghum and maize grow along the Shabelle and Jubba rivers. Telecommunications and mobile money grew quickly despite years of conflict.",
    business: "Livestock, sesame seeds, frankincense and bananas are among the exports, sold mainly to Gulf states such as Saudi Arabia and the United Arab Emirates, and Somalia is one of the world's main sources of frankincense. The official currency is the Somali shilling, although US dollars are widely used, often through mobile phones. Somalia joined the East African Community in 2024 and also belongs to the Arab League.",
    tourism: "Few tourists visit, but those who do can see Mogadishu's Lido Beach and the old coral-stone quarter of Hamar Weyne, and the Laas Geel caves in the north-west, whose painted cattle are thousands of years old. Somali food is a highlight: rice with goat or camel meat, canjeero flatbread, and pasta, a legacy of Italian rule.",
    history: "The region is often linked with the ancient Land of Punt, and in the Middle Ages sultanates such as Adal and Ajuran grew rich from trade. Britain later colonised the north and Italy the south, and in 1960 both parts won independence from them and united as Somalia. After the government collapsed in 1991, years of civil war followed, and Somaliland in the north-west declared independence, which most countries do not recognise."
  },
  "South Africa": {
    known: "South Africa is known for Nelson Mandela and the peaceful end of apartheid, and for being called the Rainbow Nation because of its many cultures and twelve official languages. It is also famous for Big Five safaris, its wines, and its passion for sport, especially rugby, cricket and football.",
    economy: "South Africa has the most industrialised economy in Africa, based mainly on services such as finance, retail and transport, along with mining and manufacturing. It is a leading producer of platinum, chromium and manganese, and also mines gold, coal and iron ore. Farms grow maize, citrus fruit, grapes and sugar cane, and raise cattle and sheep.",
    business: "Leading exports include platinum and other metals, gold, coal, iron ore, cars and citrus fruit, and China, the United States, Germany and neighbouring African countries are major partners. Johannesburg has Africa's largest stock exchange, well-known South African companies include MTN, Sasol and Shoprite, and carmakers such as BMW and Toyota build vehicles there. The currency is the rand, and South Africa belongs to SADC and the BRICS group.",
    tourism: "Visitors go on safari in Kruger National Park, ride the cable car up Table Mountain, and tour the Cape Winelands and the coastal Garden Route. UNESCO World Heritage Sites include Robben Island, where Nelson Mandela was imprisoned, the fossil caves of the Cradle of Humankind and the Drakensberg mountains. Braai barbecues, bobotie and biltong are local favourites.",
    history: "San and Khoikhoi peoples, and later Bantu-speaking peoples such as the Zulu and Xhosa, lived here before Dutch settlers arrived at the Cape in 1652, followed by the British. South Africa became a self-governing union under the British Crown in 1910 and a republic in 1961. Apartheid, the system of racial segregation enforced from 1948, ended with the first all-race elections in 1994, won by Nelson Mandela's African National Congress."
  },
  "South Sudan": {
    known: "South Sudan is known for the Sudd, one of the largest wetlands in the world, formed by the White Nile, and for its cattle-herding peoples, such as the Dinka and Nuer, for whom cattle are a sign of wealth and status. The Dinka are also known as one of the tallest peoples in the world.",
    economy: "South Sudan's economy depends almost entirely on oil, which provides nearly all export earnings and most government income, but it must be piped north through Sudan to reach the sea. Most people live by herding cattle, goats and sheep and by small-scale farming of sorghum, maize and groundnuts, and many rely on food aid after years of conflict and flooding.",
    business: "Crude oil makes up the vast majority of exports, sold mainly to buyers in Asia such as China, while most food, fuel and manufactured goods are imported, largely through Uganda and Kenya. The currency is the South Sudanese pound. South Sudan joined the East African Community in 2016 and also belongs to IGAD, a regional group of countries in the Horn of Africa and East Africa.",
    tourism: "Visitors are few, but adventurous travellers come to see the great antelope migration in Boma and Badingilo National Parks, hike in the Imatong Mountains around Mount Kinyeti, the highest peak, and visit the cattle camps of the Mundari people, where herders coat themselves and their long-horned cattle in ash to keep insects away. Local food includes kisra, a thin sorghum flatbread.",
    history: "Peoples such as the Dinka, Nuer and Shilluk have lived here for centuries, and the Shilluk kingdom was once powerful along the Nile. In the 1800s Egypt conquered the region, which later became part of Anglo-Egyptian Sudan and joined an independent Sudan in 1956. After two civil wars with the north, a 2005 peace deal led to a 2011 vote for independence from Sudan, but a new civil war broke out in 2013."
  },
  "Sudan": {
    known: "Sudan, one of Africa's largest countries, has long been the world's biggest producer of gum arabic, a natural tree resin used in soft drinks, sweets and medicines. It is also known for long wars, including the conflict in Darfur that began in 2003 and the war that broke out in 2023, which forced millions of people from their homes.",
    economy: "Sudan's economy rests mainly on farming and herding, which employ a large share of its people. Sorghum and millet are the main food crops; sesame, groundnuts and cotton are grown for sale, and sheep and camels are exported live. Oil was once the biggest earner, but most oil fields went to South Sudan in 2011, and gold, much of it dug by small-scale miners, has become the leading export.",
    business: "Sudan's main exports are gold, livestock, sesame, groundnuts and gum arabic, and its biggest trading partners include the United Arab Emirates, Saudi Arabia, Egypt and China. Most foreign trade passes through Port Sudan on the Red Sea, and South Sudan pays fees to send its oil to the coast through Sudanese pipelines. The currency is the Sudanese pound, and Sudan belongs to the Arab League and the COMESA trade bloc.",
    tourism: "Sudan sees few tourists. Besides Meroe, the sacred mountain of Jebel Barkal and its temples are a UNESCO World Heritage site, as are the coral reefs of Sanganeb and Dungonab Bay on the Red Sea, prized by divers. In Omdurman, across the Nile from Khartoum, Sufi dervishes have long gathered on Fridays to chant and whirl, and a favourite dish is ful, made from slow-cooked broad beans.",
    history: "In ancient times the kingdom of Kush grew so powerful that its kings ruled Egypt as pharaohs, and later came Christian Nubian kingdoms and Muslim sultanates. Egypt conquered Sudan in the 1820s but was driven out by a religious uprising led by the Mahdi, and after 1898 Britain and Egypt ruled it jointly until independence in 1956. Long civil wars followed, and in 2011 the south broke away as South Sudan."
  },
  "Tanzania": {
    known: "Tanzania is known for safaris in its national parks, where vast herds of wildebeest and zebra migrate each year, and for the islands of Zanzibar, long famous for their spices. Swahili, spoken across the country, unites its many peoples, and Tanzania is the only place in the world where the violet-blue gemstone tanzanite is mined.",
    economy: "Tanzania is largely agricultural: farming employs most people, who grow maize, cassava and rice to eat and coffee, cashew nuts, tobacco, tea and cotton to sell. Gold is the biggest export earner, and the country also mines diamonds and gemstones and has large natural gas reserves off its coast. Tourism is one of its largest sources of foreign currency.",
    business: "Tanzania's main exports are gold, cashew nuts, tobacco, coffee and gemstones, and its biggest trading partners include India, the United Arab Emirates, China and neighbouring Kenya. The port of Dar es Salaam handles goods for landlocked neighbours such as Zambia, Rwanda and Burundi. The currency is the Tanzanian shilling, and Tanzania belongs to both the East African Community, which has its headquarters in Arusha, and the Southern African Development Community.",
    tourism: "Besides Kilimanjaro and the Serengeti, visitors come to see the Ngorongoro Crater, a huge collapsed volcano teeming with wildlife, and to relax on Zanzibar's white-sand beaches. Zanzibar's Stone Town, with its carved wooden doors and narrow lanes, is a UNESCO World Heritage site, as are the ruins of the medieval trading port of Kilwa Kisiwani. Popular dishes include pilau, a spiced rice dish, and ugali, a stiff maize porridge.",
    history: "Fossils and stone tools at Olduvai Gorge show that human ancestors lived here nearly two million years ago. Swahili trading towns later grew up along the coast, and Zanzibar came under sultans from Oman, growing rich on cloves and on the slave trade. The mainland became German East Africa and then British-run Tanganyika, which won independence in 1961 and in 1964 united with Zanzibar to form Tanzania under its first president, Julius Nyerere."
  },
  "Togo": {
    known: "Togo is one of the homelands of Vodun, the traditional religion often called voodoo, whose followers honour the spirits of nature and of their ancestors; Lomé's Akodessewa market is famous for selling charms and other items used in Vodun rituals. The country is also known for its phosphate mines and for the port of Lomé, one of the busiest in West Africa.",
    economy: "Togo's economy rests on farming and trade. Many people grow maize, cassava, yams and sorghum for food, and cotton, coffee and cocoa for export, while phosphate mining has long been an important source of export earnings. Services are the largest part of the economy, with the port of Lomé making transport, trade and re-exports to neighbouring countries a major source of income.",
    business: "Togo exports cotton, coffee, cocoa, phosphates and cement, trading mostly with its West African neighbours, China and India, and it re-exports fuel, cars and other goods to landlocked countries such as Burkina Faso and Niger. Lomé is home to the headquarters of Ecobank, a bank with branches across Africa. The currency is the West African CFA franc, and Togo belongs to the regional blocs ECOWAS and the West African Economic and Monetary Union.",
    tourism: "In the north, visitors come to see Koutammakou, the land of the Batammariba people, whose fortress-like clay tower houses are a UNESCO World Heritage site. The green hills around Kpalimé offer waterfalls, butterflies and coffee and cocoa farms, while Lake Togo and the palm-lined beaches near Lomé are popular places to relax. A favourite dish is fufu, pounded yam, served with spicy sauces.",
    history: "The coast of present-day Togo was part of what Europeans called the Slave Coast, and in 1884 the area became the German colony of Togoland, later divided between Britain and France. French Togo won independence in 1960, and in 1963 its first president, Sylvanus Olympio, was killed in one of Africa's first military coups. In 2022 Togo joined the Commonwealth, the association of mostly former British territories."
  },
  "Tunisia": {
    known: "Tunisia is known for its olive groves, which make it one of the world's leading producers of olive oil, and for harissa, a fiery red chilli paste. It is home to the northernmost point of Africa, and its culture blends Arab, Amazigh (Berber), Mediterranean and French influences.",
    economy: "Tunisia has a mixed economy in which services, including tourism, are the biggest sector. Factories make clothing, car parts and electrical cables, largely for European companies, and farmers grow olives, dates, citrus fruit and wheat. The country also mines phosphates around Gafsa, which are made into fertiliser, and produces some oil and gas.",
    business: "Most of Tunisia's trade is with the European Union, above all France, Italy and Germany, which buy its electrical cables, car parts, clothing, olive oil and dates; phosphates and fertilisers are important exports too. The currency is the Tunisian dinar. Tunisia has a free trade agreement with the EU for industrial goods and belongs to the Arab League and the COMESA trade bloc.",
    tourism: "Many visitors come for beach holidays on Djerba or in resorts such as Hammamet and Sousse. Tunisia has several UNESCO World Heritage sites, including the huge Roman amphitheatre of El Jem, the old medina of Tunis and the holy city of Kairouan. Others come for the blue and white village of Sidi Bou Said, camel treks in the Sahara, and dishes such as couscous and brik, a crisp fried pastry filled with egg.",
    history: "After Rome destroyed Carthage in 146 BC, the region became a Roman province that supplied Rome with grain. Arab armies brought Islam in the 7th century, and from the 16th century Tunisia belonged to the Ottoman Empire, though local rulers called beys governed it in practice. France made Tunisia a protectorate in 1881, and after independence in 1956 its first president, Habib Bourguiba, expanded schooling and women's rights, including a ban on polygamy."
  },
  "Uganda": {
    known: "Uganda is famous for its mountain gorillas: nearly half of all those left in the world live in its misty forests of Bwindi and Mgahinga. It is also known for coffee, for the Rwenzori Mountains, often called the Mountains of the Moon, and for lying right on the equator.",
    economy: "Most Ugandans make a living from farming. Cooking bananas called matooke, cassava, maize and sweet potatoes feed the country, while coffee, tea, tobacco and fish from Lake Victoria are sold abroad. Services such as trade, transport and telecoms make up the largest part of the economy, and large oil reserves were discovered near Lake Albert in 2006.",
    business: "Uganda's main exports are coffee and gold, along with fish, tea, tobacco and sugar, and its big trading partners include Kenya, the United Arab Emirates, South Sudan and the Democratic Republic of the Congo. As a landlocked country, it sends much of its trade through the Kenyan port of Mombasa. The currency is the Ugandan shilling, and Uganda belongs to the East African Community and COMESA.",
    tourism: "Besides gorilla trekking, visitors track chimpanzees in Kibale Forest, watch the Nile force its way through a narrow gorge at Murchison Falls and look for tree-climbing lions in Queen Elizabeth National Park. Bwindi and the Rwenzori Mountains are UNESCO World Heritage sites. Adventurous travellers go white-water rafting on the Nile near Jinja, and a favourite street snack is the rolex, an omelette rolled in a chapati.",
    history: "Before colonial rule the region was home to powerful kingdoms such as Bunyoro and Buganda, which gave the country its name and whose king still has a ceremonial role. Britain made Uganda a protectorate in 1894, and it became independent in 1962. In the 1970s the military ruler Idi Amin governed through terror and expelled the country's Asian community, until Tanzanian troops and Ugandan exiles overthrew him in 1979."
  },
  "Zambia": {
    known: "Zambia is known above all for copper: its Copperbelt region holds some of the richest copper deposits in the world. It is also known for the Zambezi River and Lake Kariba, one of the largest man-made lakes on Earth, and for walking safaris, which were pioneered in its Luangwa Valley.",
    economy: "Zambia's economy depends heavily on mining: copper and cobalt earn most of its export income, and it is also a major source of the world's emeralds. Yet most rural families live by farming, growing maize, the main food, as well as cassava, groundnuts, soya beans, tobacco and sugar cane. Most of the country's electricity comes from hydropower dams on the Zambezi and Kafue rivers.",
    business: "Copper dominates Zambia's exports, much of it sold through commodity traders in Switzerland or shipped to China, while South Africa supplies many of its imports. Being landlocked, Zambia sends goods by road and rail to foreign ports, including along the TAZARA railway to Dar es Salaam. The currency is the Zambian kwacha, Lusaka hosts the headquarters of the COMESA trade bloc, and Zambia also belongs to the Southern African Development Community.",
    tourism: "Victoria Falls, also called Mosi-oa-Tunya, the Smoke that Thunders, is a UNESCO World Heritage site and the top sight, but visitors also go on safari in Kafue National Park and canoe past hippos on the Lower Zambezi. Up to ten million fruit bats gather in Kasanka National Park each year, and at the Kuomboka ceremony the Lozi king moves by royal barge to escape floods. The staple food is nshima, a stiff maize porridge.",
    history: "Fossils such as the Kabwe skull, found in 1921, show that early humans lived here hundreds of thousands of years ago. In the 1890s the British South Africa Company of Cecil Rhodes took control, and the land became the British protectorate of Northern Rhodesia. It won independence in 1964 as Zambia under Kenneth Kaunda, who led it until 1991, when he lost a multiparty election and handed over power peacefully."
  },
  "Zimbabwe": {
    known: "Zimbabwe is known for its wildlife, for Shona stone sculpture, carved from local rock and admired around the world, and for having one of the highest literacy rates in Africa. Many people also remember the extreme inflation of the late 2000s, when prices rose so fast that a 100 trillion dollar banknote was printed.",
    economy: "Zimbabwe's economy rests on mining and farming. It mines gold, platinum, diamonds, chrome and lithium, a metal used in batteries, while farmers grow maize to eat and tobacco, cotton and sugar cane to sell. Many people work in small informal businesses, and money sent home by Zimbabweans living abroad, especially in South Africa and Britain, supports many families.",
    business: "Zimbabwe's main exports are gold, platinum, tobacco, diamonds and chrome, sold mainly to South Africa, the United Arab Emirates and China, and the country belongs to the Southern African Development Community and COMESA. Well-known local firms include the mobile phone company Econet and the drinks maker Delta. US dollars came into everyday use after the Zimbabwe dollar collapsed in 2009, and they still circulate beside Zimbabwe Gold (ZiG), a local currency launched in 2024.",
    tourism: "Most visitors head for Victoria Falls, shared with Zambia, where they can raft, bungee jump or fly over the spray. Hwange National Park has huge herds of elephants, and Mana Pools, a UNESCO World Heritage site, is known for canoeing and walking safaris. The Matobo Hills, with their balancing granite boulders and ancient rock paintings, are also on UNESCO's list, and the staple food is sadza, a thick maize porridge.",
    history: "After Great Zimbabwe declined, the Mutapa state grew rich trading gold with Swahili and Portuguese merchants. In 1890 the British South Africa Company of Cecil Rhodes began to colonise the land, which became Southern Rhodesia. In 1965 its white minority government declared independence from Britain to avoid majority rule, leading to a long guerrilla war, and in 1980 the country became independent as Zimbabwe, led by Robert Mugabe until 2017."
  },
  "Antigua and Barbuda": {
    known: "Antigua and Barbuda is known for sailing, above all the yachts that gather each spring for Antigua Sailing Week, and for its love of cricket, which produced the famous West Indies batsman Viv Richards. Barbuda, the quieter of the two islands, is known for its pink-tinged sand beaches and one of the largest colonies of frigatebirds in the world.",
    economy: "Tourism is by far the biggest part of the economy, bringing in most foreign income and many jobs through beach holidays, yachting and cruise ship visits. Banking and other financial services, along with construction, also contribute. Farming is small because the islands are dry and have almost no rivers, but farmers grow the Antigua black pineapple, a small and very sweet variety, as well as vegetables, and there is some fishing.",
    business: "Antigua and Barbuda imports most of what it uses, especially from the United States, and exports little apart from re-exported fuel and a few manufactured goods; its earnings come mainly from visitors, yacht services and offshore finance. The currency is the Eastern Caribbean dollar, shared with several neighbouring islands and pegged to the US dollar. The country belongs to the Caribbean Community and the Organisation of Eastern Caribbean States.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the beaches, snorkelling and sailing, and for Nelson's Dockyard at English Harbour, a restored 18th-century British naval base that is a UNESCO World Heritage site. On Sunday evenings crowds gather at the Shirley Heights lookout for steel band music and sunset views. Carnival, held around the start of August, celebrates the end of slavery, and the national dish is fungee, cornmeal cooked with okra, served with pepperpot stew.",
    history: "Before Europeans came, Arawak and later Kalinago peoples lived here. Christopher Columbus named Antigua in 1493 after a revered image of the Virgin Mary in Seville, and English settlers who arrived in 1632 built sugar plantations worked by enslaved Africans, until slavery ended in 1834. The islands became independent from Britain in 1981, and in 2017 Hurricane Irma damaged or destroyed most buildings on Barbuda, forcing everyone there to evacuate to Antigua."
  },
  "Bahamas": {
    known: "The Bahamas is known for its shallow turquoise seas, luxury resorts and cruise ship visits, and as a centre for international banking. It is also known for Junkanoo, a lively street parade of costumes, drums and cowbells held at Christmas and New Year, and as the place where Christopher Columbus is believed to have first landed in the Americas, in 1492.",
    economy: "Tourism is the heart of the Bahamian economy, providing directly and indirectly about half of all income and a large share of jobs. Banking and other financial services come second, helped by the absence of personal income tax. Fishing for spiny lobster, known locally as crawfish, is important, as is salt production on Great Inagua, while farming is small.",
    business: "The United States is by far the biggest trading partner of the Bahamas, supplying most imports and tourists and buying exports such as crawfish, salt and fuel products. Nassau is a major offshore finance centre, and Freeport on Grand Bahama has a low-tax business zone and a large container port. The currency is the Bahamian dollar, pegged to the US dollar, and the country is a member of the Caribbean Community.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for beaches, diving and fishing, from the pink sand of Harbour Island to Dean's Blue Hole on Long Island, one of the deepest underwater sinkholes in the world. Great Inagua is home to tens of thousands of pink flamingos, the national bird. Popular foods include conch, a large sea snail served in fritters or fresh salads, and guava duff, a sweet rolled pudding.",
    history: "The first inhabitants, the Lucayan people, were enslaved by the Spanish and had disappeared within a few decades, and English settlers arrived from Bermuda in 1648. Later, settlers loyal to Britain fled the American Revolution and brought enslaved Africans to work cotton plantations, until slavery ended in 1834. The Bahamas became independent from Britain in 1973, and in 2019 Hurricane Dorian devastated the islands of Abaco and Grand Bahama."
  },
  "Barbados": {
    known: "Barbados is known for its beaches, its passion for cricket and the flying fish, a national symbol. Its long British rule earned it the nickname Little England, and in 2021 it became a republic, replacing the British monarch with a Barbadian president as head of state while remaining in the Commonwealth.",
    economy: "Barbados has a services economy built on tourism, which brings in the largest share of its foreign earnings, and on international business and finance, which attract foreign companies to set up there. Sugar cane once covered much of the island, but today it is grown on a much smaller scale, mainly for rum and sugar, and light industry makes food, drinks and building materials.",
    business: "Rum is one of Barbados's main exports, and Mount Gay, whose history goes back to 1703, is one of the oldest rum brands in the world. Other exports include drinks, food and cement, sold mainly to the United States and nearby Caribbean countries. The currency is the Barbados dollar, pegged to the US dollar, and Barbados, a founding member of the Caribbean Community, is home to the Caribbean Development Bank.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the calm turquoise sea of the west coast, the surfing waves of Bathsheba on the Atlantic side and Harrison's Cave, full of stalactites. The summer Crop Over festival, which once marked the end of the sugar cane harvest, ends with a huge costumed parade. On Friday nights many head to Oistins for its fish fry, and the national dish is cou-cou, a cornmeal and okra dish, with flying fish.",
    history: "English settlers arrived in 1627 on an island that was by then uninhabited. By the 1640s Barbados had become a sugar colony worked by enslaved Africans, and its plantation system spread to other colonies in the Caribbean and North America. In 1816 an enslaved man named Bussa led a major uprising, slavery ended in the 1830s, and in 1966 Barbados, which had never changed hands between European powers, became independent from Britain."
  },
  "Belize": {
    known: "Belize is known for its Maya ruins, rainforests and small islands called cayes, and for Cockscomb Basin, the world's first reserve created to protect jaguars. It is the only country in Central America where English is the official language, and its people include Kriol, Mestizo, Maya, Garifuna and Mennonite communities.",
    economy: "Belize's economy rests on tourism, its largest source of foreign income, and on farming and fishing. The country grows sugar cane, bananas and citrus fruit, turning oranges into juice concentrate for export, and its fishermen catch lobster and conch. A little oil has been produced since it was found in 2005, but in 2017 Belize banned offshore oil drilling to protect its reef.",
    business: "Belize exports sugar, bananas, orange juice concentrate, seafood and molasses, mainly to the United States, the United Kingdom and the European Union. One of its best-known brands is Marie Sharp's hot sauce, made from local habanero peppers. The currency is the Belize dollar, pegged to the US dollar, and Belize belongs both to the Caribbean Community and to the Central American Integration System.",
    tourism: "Divers and snorkellers head for cayes such as Ambergris Caye and for the barrier reef, a UNESCO World Heritage site. Inland, visitors explore Maya cities such as Caracol and Xunantunich and caves such as Actun Tunichil Muknal, where ancient Maya skeletons lie. Garifuna Settlement Day in November, which marks the arrival of the Garifuna people, brings drumming and dance, and a favourite meal is rice and beans with stewed chicken.",
    history: "The Maya built great cities here long before Europeans arrived. From the 1600s British woodcutters settled the coast to harvest logwood and mahogany, and in 1798 they fought off a Spanish fleet at St George's Caye. The territory became the colony of British Honduras, was renamed Belize in 1973 and won independence from Britain in 1981, and a long-standing territorial dispute with Guatemala has been taken to the International Court of Justice."
  },
  "Canada": {
    known: "Canada is known for its vast wilderness of forests, mountains and Arctic tundra, for ice hockey and for maple syrup, of which the province of Quebec alone makes most of the world's supply. It has two official languages, English and French, and the longest coastline of any country, touching the Atlantic, Pacific and Arctic oceans.",
    economy: "Most Canadians work in services such as finance, health, education and retail, but natural resources earn much of the country's export income. Canada has some of the world's largest oil reserves, mostly in the oil sands of Alberta, and it mines potash, uranium, nickel and gold. Its prairies grow wheat and canola, a crop developed by Canadian scientists, while Ontario builds cars and Quebec builds aircraft.",
    business: "Most of Canada's exports, such as oil, gas, cars, timber and metals, go to the United States, and it has signed free trade deals with the United States, Mexico and the European Union. Well-known Canadian companies include Shopify, Bombardier, Lululemon and the coffee chain Tim Hortons, and Toronto is its financial capital. The currency is the Canadian dollar, nicknamed the loonie after the bird on its one-dollar coin.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Niagara Falls and for UNESCO World Heritage sites such as the Rocky Mountain parks of Banff and Jasper, with their turquoise lakes, and the walled old town of Quebec City. Others watch polar bears near Churchill in Manitoba, see the northern lights in the Yukon or go to the Calgary Stampede rodeo. A classic snack is poutine, chips topped with cheese curds and gravy.",
    history: "Indigenous peoples, including the First Nations and the Inuit, have lived here for thousands of years, and Vikings briefly settled in Newfoundland around the year 1000. France founded Quebec City in 1608, but Britain took over New France in 1763 after the Seven Years' War. Canada became a self-governing country in 1867, gained full control of its constitution in 1982 and in 1999 created Nunavut, a mainly Inuit territory in the Arctic."
  },
  "Costa Rica": {
    known: "Costa Rica is known for its rainforests, cloud forests and wildlife, from sloths and toucans to brightly coloured frogs, which give it one of the richest varieties of plants and animals on Earth for its size. Costa Ricans call themselves Ticos and often use the phrase pura vida, meaning pure life, as a greeting and a way of saying all is well.",
    economy: "Costa Rica's economy is based mainly on services, including tourism and business services, together with an important manufacturing sector in special free trade zones. Medical devices, such as catheters and surgical instruments made there by foreign companies, have become its biggest export. Farming remains important: Costa Rica is one of the world's leading exporters of bananas and pineapples, and it grows high-quality coffee.",
    business: "The United States is Costa Rica's main trading partner, followed by Europe and Central America, and many multinational companies run factories and service centres there, drawn by its educated workforce. It has a free trade agreement with the United States and its neighbours, and in 2021 it joined the OECD, a group of mostly wealthy countries. The currency is the colón, named after Christopher Columbus, Cristóbal Colón in Spanish.",
    tourism: "Visitors come to hike in the misty cloud forest of Monteverde, see Arenal Volcano, fly through the treetops on zip wires and surf on the Pacific coast. Turtles nest on the beaches of Tortuguero, and Cocos Island, famous for its hammerhead sharks, is one of several UNESCO World Heritage sites, along with the stone spheres made by ancient peoples in the south. Breakfast is often gallo pinto, rice and beans fried together.",
    history: "Indigenous peoples lived here long before Christopher Columbus reached the Caribbean coast in 1502. As a poor, remote Spanish colony it grew into a land of small farms, and it became independent from Spain with the rest of Central America in 1821. Coffee brought wealth in the 1800s, and in 1987 a Costa Rican president won the Nobel Peace Prize for a plan to end the wars in Central America."
  },
  "Cuba": {
    known: "Cuba is known for the 1959 revolution led by Fidel Castro and Che Guevara, after which it became a communist state with a single ruling party. It is also known for its free public health care and its many doctors, thousands of whom work abroad, and for baseball, the national sport.",
    economy: "Cuba has a mostly state-run economy dominated by services, and small private businesses have been allowed to grow since the 2010s. Its biggest sources of foreign money are doctors and other professionals working abroad, tourism, and money sent home by Cubans overseas, especially in the United States. Sugar was once the backbone of the economy but has declined sharply, and Cuba also mines nickel and grows tobacco.",
    business: "Cuba's famous cigars are sold under brands such as Cohiba and Montecristo, and its best-known rum is Havana Club. It also exports nickel, sugar, seafood and medicines made by its biotechnology industry, and its main trading partners include China, Spain, Canada and Mexico. A trade embargo imposed by the United States in the early 1960s has long limited trade with its nearest big neighbour, and the currency is the Cuban peso.",
    tourism: "Many visitors stay at beach resorts such as Varadero and Cayo Coco. Cuba has several UNESCO World Heritage sites, including the Viñales Valley, where tobacco grows among steep limestone hills, and the colourful colonial town of Trinidad. Travellers also come to dance salsa, see the carnival of Santiago de Cuba and try dishes such as ropa vieja, shredded beef stewed with tomatoes and peppers.",
    history: "Christopher Columbus reached Cuba in 1492, and Spain ruled it for nearly four centuries, growing sugar with the labour of enslaved Africans. Cubans fought long wars for independence, inspired by the poet José Martí, and won it in 1902 after the United States intervened against Spain. After the 1959 revolution Cuba allied with the Soviet Union, and in 1962 Soviet missiles on the island brought the world close to nuclear war."
  },
  "Dominica": {
    known: "Dominica is known for its sperm whales, which live off the west coast all year round; in 2023 the country created a marine reserve to protect them. It is also home to the Sisserou parrot, found nowhere else and shown on the national flag, and to the Kalinago people, descendants of the island's Indigenous inhabitants, who have their own territory on the east coast.",
    economy: "Dominica has a small economy based mostly on services. Tourism built around nature rather than big beach resorts is important, and so is the sale of citizenship to foreign investors, which brings in a large share of government income. Farming still employs many people, who grow bananas, citrus fruits, plantains and root crops such as dasheen.",
    business: "Dominica's exports include bananas and other fruit, root vegetables and bay oil, which is made from the leaves of the West Indian bay tree and used in perfumes, and much of its trade is with the United States and other Caribbean countries. It uses the Eastern Caribbean dollar, which it shares with several nearby islands, and belongs to CARICOM, the Caribbean trading community, and to the Organisation of Eastern Caribbean States.",
    tourism: "Visitors come to hike, especially along the Waitukubuli National Trail, which runs the whole length of the island, and to see waterfalls such as Trafalgar Falls. Morne Trois Pitons National Park is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Divers enjoy Champagne Reef, where volcanic gas bubbles up through the water, and the World Creole Music Festival fills Roseau with music each October.",
    history: "Columbus sighted the island on a Sunday in 1493 and named it after that day, from the Latin for Sunday. Kalinago resistance slowed European settlement, and France and Britain then fought over the island until Britain secured it in the late 1700s. Dominica became independent from Britain in 1978, and in 2017 Hurricane Maria, a category 5 storm, damaged most of its buildings."
  },
  "Dominican Republic": {
    known: "The Dominican Republic is known for its all-inclusive beach resorts, such as those around Punta Cana, and for baseball: more Major League Baseball players have come from here than from any other country apart from the United States. It is also the home of bachata, a romantic style of guitar music, and one of the world's biggest makers of hand-rolled cigars.",
    economy: "The economy is based mainly on services, especially tourism, and money sent home by Dominicans living abroad, mostly in the United States, adds a large sum every year. Factories in free trade zones make medical devices, clothing and cigars for export, and the Pueblo Viejo mine is one of the biggest gold mines in the world. Farmers grow sugar cane, cocoa, bananas, tobacco and rice.",
    business: "The United States is by far its biggest trading partner, and the country belongs to CAFTA-DR, a free trade agreement with the United States and Central America. Main exports include gold, medical devices, cigars, cocoa and bananas. Well-known local brands include Brugal rum and Presidente beer, and the currency is the Dominican peso.",
    tourism: "Most visitors come for the white sand beaches and resorts of the east and north coasts. The Colonial City of Santo Domingo, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, holds the first cathedral built in the Americas. Humpback whales gather in Samaná Bay in winter, hikers climb Pico Duarte, the highest mountain in the Caribbean, and a typical lunch is la bandera, rice with beans and stewed meat.",
    history: "The Taíno lived on the island when Columbus arrived in 1492, and it became the site of Spain's first colony in the Americas. Neighbouring Haiti ruled the eastern part from 1822 until 1844, when the Dominican Republic declared independence on 27 February. United States forces occupied the country from 1916 to 1924, and the dictator Rafael Trujillo ruled harshly from 1930 until he was assassinated in 1961."
  },
  "El Salvador": {
    known: "El Salvador is known for pupusas, thick corn tortillas stuffed with cheese, beans or pork, which are the national dish and even have their own national day. It is also known for its Pacific surf beaches, which draw surfers from around the world, and for coffee grown on the slopes of its volcanoes.",
    economy: "El Salvador's economy is mostly services, and money sent home by the many Salvadorans living in the United States is worth close to a quarter of the whole economy. Factories sew clothing for export, and farmers grow coffee, which was once the country's main source of wealth, as well as sugar cane, maize and beans.",
    business: "Most exports go to the United States and to Central American neighbours such as Guatemala and Honduras, with clothing, coffee and sugar among the main products. El Salvador has used the US dollar as its currency since 2001, and it belongs to the CAFTA-DR free trade agreement and to the Central American Integration System, which links the region's countries.",
    tourism: "Joya de Cerén, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is a Maya farming village buried by volcanic ash around 1,400 years ago and so well preserved that it is called the Pompeii of the Americas. Visitors also climb Santa Ana volcano to see its crater lake, swim in Lake Coatepeque, surf at beaches such as El Tunco and tour the coffee towns of the Ruta de las Flores.",
    history: "The Pipil people called their land Cuzcatlán before Spanish conquerors arrived in the 1520s. El Salvador gained independence from Spain in 1821 along with the rest of Central America and became a separate republic after the Central American federation broke up around 1840. A civil war from 1980 to 1992 killed about 75,000 people and ended with peace agreements signed in 1992."
  },
  "Grenada": {
    known: "Grenada is known for its beaches, above all the long white sand of Grand Anse, and for the Molinere Underwater Sculpture Park, where statues placed on the seabed have slowly become an artificial reef. The island also makes fine chocolate from its own cocoa, and its smaller sister island of Carriacou is known for traditional wooden boatbuilding.",
    economy: "Grenada's economy is based mainly on services. Tourism is the biggest earner, and St George's University, a large medical school with thousands of students from abroad, also brings in a lot of money. Farming is smaller but still important, producing nutmeg and mace, a spice made from the red covering of the nutmeg seed, as well as cocoa and fruit. The country also sells citizenship to foreign investors.",
    business: "Grenada's exports include nutmeg and mace, fresh fish such as tuna, and cocoa, sold to buyers in North America, Europe and nearby Caribbean islands. Its nutmeg has long been sold through a cooperative owned by the farmers, the Grenada Cooperative Nutmeg Association. The country uses the Eastern Caribbean dollar and belongs to CARICOM and the Organisation of Eastern Caribbean States.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for beaches, diving and sailing, including trips to Carriacou and Petite Martinique. Inland, the rainforest of Grand Etang holds a crater lake and waterfalls, and visitors can tour nutmeg processing stations, cocoa estates and a rum distillery still powered by a water wheel. The Spicemas carnival takes place in August, and the national dish, oil down, is a stew of breadfruit, salted meat and dumplings cooked in coconut milk.",
    history: "Kalinago resistance held off settlers until the French took the island in the mid-1600s; tradition says the last Kalinago leapt from a cliff at Sauteurs rather than surrender. Britain gained Grenada in 1763; under both powers, enslaved Africans were forced to work its plantations. Independence from Britain came in 1974. In 1983 Maurice Bishop, who had led a revolution in 1979, was killed in a power struggle, and a US-led force invaded days later."
  },
  "Guatemala": {
    known: "Guatemala is known for its living Maya culture: many people speak one of more than 20 Maya languages, and women in highland villages weave brightly patterned clothing whose designs can show which town the wearer comes from. It is also famous for its coffee and for Lake Atitlán, a deep blue lake overlooked by three volcanoes.",
    economy: "Guatemala's economy is based mostly on services, and money sent home by Guatemalans living in the United States is worth about a fifth of the whole economy. Farming still employs many people and produces coffee, bananas, sugar cane and cardamom, a spice of which Guatemala is one of the world's biggest exporters. Factories also make clothing for export.",
    business: "The United States is Guatemala's biggest trading partner, its Central American neighbours and Mexico are also important, and the country belongs to the CAFTA-DR free trade agreement. Main exports include clothing, bananas, coffee, sugar and palm oil. Famous Guatemalan brands include the fried chicken chain Pollo Campero and Zacapa rum, and the currency is the quetzal, named after the national bird.",
    tourism: "Antigua Guatemala, a former colonial capital of cobbled streets and ruined churches, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, as are Tikal and Quiriguá, a Maya site known for its giant carved stone pillars. Visitors hike Acatenango volcano to watch nearby Fuego erupt, shop at the market of Chichicastenango and see the carpets of coloured sawdust and flowers laid in Antigua's streets for Holy Week.",
    history: "Long after the great lowland Maya cities declined, Spanish forces conquered the highland Maya kingdoms in the 1520s, and Guatemala became the centre of Spanish rule over most of Central America. It gained independence from Spain in 1821. In 1954 a coup backed by the United States removed the elected president, Jacobo Árbenz, and a civil war from 1960 to 1996 left about 200,000 people dead or missing, most of them Maya."
  },
  "Haiti": {
    known: "Haiti is known for Haitian Creole, which almost everyone speaks and which is an official language together with French, and for Vodou, a religion that blends West African beliefs with Catholic traditions. Its art is famous too, from brightly painted tap-taps, the shared taxis that crowd its streets, to paintings and sculptures cut from old oil drums.",
    economy: "Haiti is the poorest country in the Americas. Its economy relies heavily on money sent home by Haitians living abroad, which is worth about a fifth of the whole economy, and on foreign aid. Many people make a living from small farms growing rice, maize, beans, mangoes and coffee, and factories sew clothing for export.",
    business: "Clothing made in assembly factories brings in most of Haiti's export earnings, and most of it goes to the United States, its main trading partner. Other exports include mangoes, cocoa and vetiver oil, which comes from the roots of a grass and is used in perfumes; Haiti is a leading producer. The currency is the gourde, and Haiti is a member of CARICOM.",
    tourism: "The Citadelle Laferrière, a huge mountaintop fortress built after independence, forms a UNESCO World Heritage Site with the ruined Sans-Souci Palace. Visitors also enjoy the beaches and turquoise waterfall pools near Jacmel, whose carnival is famous for papier-mâché masks. On 1 January Haitians eat soup joumou, a pumpkin soup that celebrates freedom. Tourism has long been held back by unrest and violence.",
    history: "The Taíno called the island Ayiti. Under French rule the colony of Saint-Domingue grew sugar and coffee on plantations worked by hundreds of thousands of enslaved Africans, until a revolution led by Toussaint Louverture and Jean-Jacques Dessalines won independence from France. In 1825 France demanded a huge payment for recognising Haiti, a debt that burdened it for over a century, and in 2010 a powerful earthquake destroyed much of Port-au-Prince."
  },
  "Honduras": {
    known: "Honduras is known for coffee, being one of the world's leading coffee exporters, and for La Mosquitia, a vast area of rainforest, rivers and lagoons in the east with very few roads. The phrase banana republic was coined by the American writer O. Henry after he stayed in Honduras in the 1890s.",
    economy: "Honduras's economy is based mostly on services, with farming and factories also important, and money sent home by Hondurans abroad, mostly in the United States, is worth about a quarter of it, one of the highest shares in the world. Farmers grow coffee, bananas, oil palms and melons, shrimp are farmed on the Pacific coast, and factories around San Pedro Sula sew large amounts of clothing for export.",
    business: "The United States is Honduras's main trading partner, buying much of the clothing and car wiring made in its export factories, while its coffee goes to buyers in Europe and North America. The country belongs to the CAFTA-DR free trade agreement, and San Pedro Sula is its business centre. The currency, the lempira, is named after a Lenca chief who fought the Spanish in the 1530s.",
    tourism: "The Maya ruins of Copán are a UNESCO World Heritage Site, as is the Río Plátano Biosphere Reserve, which protects rainforest, rivers and rare wildlife. Visitors can swim with whale sharks off Utila, raft rivers near La Ceiba and visit Garífuna villages on the Caribbean coast. A favourite snack is the baleada, a flour tortilla folded around beans, cream and cheese.",
    history: "The Maya city of Copán flourished from about the 5th to the 9th century. Columbus reached the coast in 1502, and Spain conquered the region despite fierce resistance. Honduras gained independence from Spain in 1821 and became a separate republic in 1838; American fruit companies later came to control much of its land and railways. In 1998 Hurricane Mitch killed thousands of people and caused huge damage."
  },
  "Jamaica": {
    known: "Jamaica is known for jerk, a spicy way of grilling chicken and pork, for Blue Mountain coffee, one of the world's most expensive, and for the Rastafari movement, which began on the island in the 1930s. Jamaican Patois, an English-based creole, has spread around the world through the island's music.",
    economy: "Jamaica's economy is mostly services. Tourism is the biggest earner of foreign money, and money sent home by Jamaicans abroad is another major source of income. Mining of bauxite, the rock used to make aluminium, has long been important, farmers grow sugar cane, bananas, yams and coffee, and many people work in call centres serving companies in North America.",
    business: "Jamaica exports alumina, a white powder made from bauxite, as well as rum, coffee and foods such as sauces and spices, and the United States is its main trading partner. Famous brands include Appleton Estate rum and Red Stripe beer. The currency is the Jamaican dollar, and Jamaica was a founding member of CARICOM, the Caribbean trading community.",
    tourism: "Most visitors head for the beaches of Montego Bay and Negril, and near Ocho Rios they climb the terraced Dunn's River Falls. The Blue and John Crow Mountains, where the famous coffee grows, are a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and the Reggae Sumfest festival draws big crowds to Montego Bay each summer. The national dish is ackee and saltfish; cooked ackee fruit looks like scrambled egg.",
    history: "The Taíno called the island Xaymaca, land of wood and water. Spain claimed it after Columbus arrived in 1494, and England seized it in 1655, turning it into a rich sugar colony worked by enslaved Africans. In 1692 an earthquake sank much of Port Royal, a notorious pirate town. Escaped slaves known as Maroons fought the British and won treaties granting them land, and Jamaica became independent from Britain in 1962."
  },
  "Mexico": {
    known: "Mexico is known for tacos, for tequila made from the blue agave plant, for mariachi bands and for the Day of the Dead, when families build colourful altars to remember loved ones who have died. Its traditional cooking, the Day of the Dead and mariachi music are all on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage, and it has more Spanish speakers than any other country.",
    economy: "Mexico has one of the two largest economies in Latin America, based mainly on services and manufacturing. Factories, many near the border with the United States, build cars, car parts, televisions and computers, and the country also produces oil and more silver than almost anywhere else. Farmers grow maize, berries and limes, and Mexico supplies most of the avocados eaten in the United States.",
    business: "Mexico's main exports include cars and car parts, computers, oil, fresh produce and beer, and about four fifths of all its exports go to the United States. Trade with the United States and Canada has run under a free trade agreement since 1994, called NAFTA until 2020 and the USMCA since then. Famous Mexican names include the baker Grupo Bimbo, the cement maker Cemex and Corona beer, and the currency is the peso.",
    tourism: "Mexico has dozens of UNESCO World Heritage Sites, including the pyramids of Teotihuacan and the Maya city of Chichén Itzá. Visitors crowd the beaches of Cancún and the Riviera Maya, swim in cenotes, which are natural sinkholes filled with fresh water, and wander colourful colonial towns such as Guanajuato. In winter, huge numbers of monarch butterflies gather in the mountain forests of central Mexico.",
    history: "The Olmecs created one of the region's earliest civilisations over 3,000 years ago, and around AD 500 Teotihuacan was one of the world's largest cities. Spanish forces under Hernán Cortés conquered the Aztec Empire in 1521, and Mexico, then part of the colony of New Spain, won independence from Spain in 1821. By 1848, after a war with the United States, Mexico had lost about half its territory, and a revolution began in 1910."
  },
  "Nicaragua": {
    known: "Nicaragua is called the land of lakes and volcanoes, and one of its best-known sights is Ometepe, an island formed by two volcanoes rising out of Lake Nicaragua. It is also known for Flor de Caña rum and for the poet Rubén Darío, who led a movement that changed poetry in the Spanish language around 1900.",
    economy: "Nicaragua is one of the poorest countries in the Americas. Its economy rests on farming, mining and money sent home by Nicaraguans abroad, which is worth about a quarter of the economy. Farmers raise cattle and grow coffee, sugar cane, peanuts and beans, gold mining has grown into a major industry, and factories in free trade zones make clothing and car wiring.",
    business: "Nicaragua's main exports include gold, beef, coffee, clothing and hand-rolled cigars; most of the cigars are made around the northern city of Estelí. The United States is its biggest customer, and Mexico and its Central American neighbours are other important markets. The currency is the córdoba, named after the Spanish founder of the cities of Granada and León, and the country belongs to the Central American Integration System.",
    tourism: "Visitors explore the colonial cities of Granada and León; León's cathedral and the ruins of León Viejo, an early Spanish town abandoned after earthquakes in 1610, are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. Travellers peer into the smoking crater of Masaya volcano, slide down the black ash slopes of Cerro Negro on boards and surf near San Juan del Sur. Gallo pinto, rice and beans, is eaten at almost every meal.",
    history: "Spanish conquerors arrived in the 1520s, and Nicaragua gained independence from Spain in 1821. United States Marines occupied the country for most of the time from 1912 to 1933, and the Somoza family then ruled as dictators until the Sandinista revolution overthrew them in 1979. In the 1980s the Sandinista government fought the Contras, rebels backed by the United States, until a peace process led to elections in 1990."
  },
  "Panama": {
    known: "Panama is known for Geisha coffee, one of the most expensive coffees in the world, and for the Darién, a jungle without roads on the border with Colombia that forms the only gap in the Pan-American Highway, which otherwise runs from Alaska to Argentina. The Panama hat is a surprise: it is made in Ecuador and took its name from being sold and worn in Panama.",
    economy: "Panama's economy is built mainly on services. The canal earns large fees, and banking, shipping, ports and the Colón Free Trade Zone, one of the biggest duty-free trading zones in the world, employ many people. Panama uses the US dollar alongside its own balboa coins, and farming, with bananas, pineapples and coffee, is only a small part of the economy.",
    business: "Many international companies run their Latin American offices from Panama City, a major banking centre, and Copa Airlines links the Americas through its hub at Tocumen airport. The United States and China, the canal's biggest users, are among Panama's main trading partners, and goods are shipped on across the region from Colón. Panama also has one of the world's largest registers of merchant ships.",
    tourism: "Visitors watch giant ships pass through the canal's locks, stroll the old quarter of Casco Viejo and see the ruins of Panamá Viejo, destroyed by fire when the pirate Henry Morgan attacked in 1671; together they form a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Others sail to the San Blas Islands, home of the Guna people and their colourful sewn molas, or head for the beaches of Bocas del Toro and the coffee farms of Boquete.",
    history: "In 1513 Vasco Núñez de Balboa crossed the narrow strip of land and became the first European to see the Pacific from the Americas; later, Spanish treasure from Peru was carried across it. Panama left Spanish rule in 1821 to join Colombia and became independent from Colombia in 1903 with United States support. The United States built the canal, which opened in 1914, and handed it to Panama at the end of 1999."
  },
  "Saint Kitts and Nevis": {
    known: "Saint Kitts and Nevis is known for running the world's oldest citizenship by investment programme, begun in 1984, which lets foreigners gain a passport by investing in the country. Nevis is famous as the birthplace of Alexander Hamilton, one of the founding fathers of the United States, and both islands are home to green vervet monkeys, brought over from Africa centuries ago.",
    economy: "For over 300 years Saint Kitts depended on sugar, until the state-run sugar industry closed in 2005. Today the economy is based on services: tourism, including many cruise ship visits, the sale of citizenship, which brings in a large share of government income, and offshore finance on Nevis. A few factories assemble electronic parts for export.",
    business: "The Eastern Caribbean Central Bank, which issues the Eastern Caribbean dollar used by the country and its neighbours, has its headquarters in Basseterre. Nevis is known around the world as a place to register offshore companies, and the country trades mostly with the United States and other Caribbean islands. It belongs to CARICOM and the Organisation of Eastern Caribbean States.",
    tourism: "Visitors ride the St Kitts Scenic Railway, a small railway first built to carry sugar cane, and climb Mount Liamuiga to the rim of its crater. Brimstone Hill Fortress is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. On quieter Nevis, travellers stay in old plantation inns and bathe in hot springs, while on Saint Kitts the Sugar Mas carnival brightens the Christmas and New Year season.",
    history: "In 1624 Saint Kitts became the first lasting English colony in the Caribbean; French settlers followed, and in 1626 the two groups massacred many Kalinago at a place still called Bloody Point. Britain gained the whole island in 1713 and ran sugar plantations worked by enslaved Africans. Independence from Britain came in 1983, and in 1998 a vote for Nevis to leave fell short of the two-thirds majority needed."
  },
  "Saint Lucia": {
    known: "Saint Lucia is a favourite honeymoon destination, known for luxury resorts, rainforest and the Sulphur Springs near Soufrière, often called the world's only drive-in volcano. It is nicknamed the Helen of the West Indies because, like Helen of Troy in Greek legend, it was fought over again and again.",
    economy: "Saint Lucia's economy depends mainly on tourism, which brings in most of the country's foreign earnings and provides many of its jobs. Bananas were once the main export, but sales fell after Europe ended special trade deals for Caribbean growers, and farmers now also grow cocoa, coconuts and mangoes. The government also earns money by selling citizenship to foreign investors.",
    business: "Saint Lucia sells rum, beer and bananas abroad, mostly to other Caribbean islands and the United States, and its best-known brands are Chairman's Reserve rum and Piton beer. It uses the Eastern Caribbean dollar and belongs to CARICOM, and the headquarters of the Organisation of Eastern Caribbean States is in Castries.",
    tourism: "The Pitons and the surrounding reefs form a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and many visitors climb Gros Piton or snorkel on the reefs below. Others explore the old forts of Pigeon Island, the sheltered anchorage of Marigot Bay and the waterfall of the Diamond Botanical Gardens. On Friday nights the village of Gros Islet holds a lively street party, and the national dish is green fig and saltfish, made with green bananas.",
    history: "Kalinago resistance drove off early English settlers in the 1600s before the French founded a lasting colony. Britain and France then fought over Saint Lucia for about 150 years, and it changed hands 14 times until Britain kept it for good in 1814. French rule left a lasting mark: many people speak Kwéyòl, a French-based creole. Saint Lucia became independent from Britain in 1979."
  },
  "Saint Vincent and the Grenadines": {
    known: "Saint Vincent and the Grenadines is known as a sailing paradise, with yachts cruising between Bequia, the Tobago Cays and Mustique, a private island famous for its wealthy and royal visitors. The north of the main island is dominated by La Soufrière, an active volcano whose explosive eruption in 2021 forced thousands of people to leave their homes.",
    economy: "The economy is based mainly on services, especially tourism, but farming matters more here than on many neighbouring islands. Bananas were once the main export, and farmers also grow plantains and root crops such as dasheen and arrowroot, a plant whose starch the island has long exported. Argyle International Airport, opened in 2017, was built to bring in more visitors.",
    business: "Saint Vincent and the Grenadines sells root crops such as dasheen and flour milled on the island, mostly to its Caribbean neighbours, and imports much of its food and fuel. It uses the Eastern Caribbean dollar and belongs to CARICOM and the Organisation of Eastern Caribbean States. Chartering yachts and running marinas in the Grenadines is also an important business.",
    tourism: "Visitors sail and snorkel among the turtles of the Tobago Cays Marine Park and relax on the beaches of Bequia. On the main island, hikers climb La Soufrière or visit Dark View Falls, and the Botanical Gardens in Kingstown, founded in 1765, are among the oldest in the Americas. The Vincy Mas carnival fills the early summer, and the national dish is roasted breadfruit with fried jackfish.",
    history: "The Kalinago and the Garifuna, descendants of Kalinago and Africans, held off European settlers longer than the people of most Caribbean islands and kept fighting after Britain took control in 1763. In 1793 Captain William Bligh brought breadfruit here from Tahiti. After the Garifuna chief Joseph Chatoyer was killed fighting the British in 1795, thousands of Garifuna were deported to Roatán, off Honduras. The country became independent from Britain in 1979."
  },
  "Trinidad and Tobago": {
    known: "Beyond its music and carnival, Trinidad and Tobago is known as one of the richest countries in the Caribbean, thanks to its oil and natural gas. It is also home to the Pitch Lake, the world's largest natural deposit of asphalt, and has a lively mix of cultures, with African and Indian traditions at its heart.",
    economy: "The economy rests on energy. Oil and, above all, natural gas are pumped mostly from under the sea, and the gas is turned into liquefied natural gas, ammonia and methanol, making the country one of the most industrialised in the Caribbean and one of the world's big exporters of ammonia and methanol. Services such as banking and trade also matter. Tobago relies largely on tourism, and farming now plays only a small part.",
    business: "Its main exports are liquefied natural gas, oil, ammonia and methanol, and the United States is its main trading partner. The best-known local brand is Angostura, whose aromatic bitters, a strong herbal flavouring, are used in cocktails around the world. The currency is the Trinidad and Tobago dollar, and the country is a member of CARICOM, the Caribbean Community.",
    tourism: "Tobago draws visitors with quiet beaches, coral reefs and the Main Ridge rainforest, protected since 1776. On Trinidad, people watch flocks of scarlet ibis fly into the Caroni Swamp at dusk, see giant leatherback turtles nesting on northern and eastern beaches and go birdwatching in the hills. Street food is a highlight too, especially doubles, curried chickpeas in soft fried bread, and roti.",
    history: "Amerindian peoples such as the Arawaks and Caribs lived on the islands before Christopher Columbus reached Trinidad in 1498. Spain held Trinidad until Britain took it in 1797, while Tobago changed hands many times before the two were joined as one British colony in 1889. Enslaved Africans, and later workers from India, were brought to the plantations. Independence from Britain came in 1962, and the country became a republic in 1976."
  },
  "United States": {
    known: "The United States is known around the world for Hollywood films, popular music, sport and fast food, and for technology companies based in places such as Silicon Valley in California. It is also famous for landing the first astronauts on the Moon, in 1969, and for landmarks like the Statue of Liberty in New York.",
    economy: "The American economy is mainly based on services, such as finance, health care, technology, retail and entertainment, which make up more than three-quarters of it. It is also a major industrial power, making aircraft, cars, chemicals and medicines, and one of the biggest producers of oil and natural gas. The country's farms employ few people but make it a leading exporter of soybeans, maize and meat.",
    business: "Famous American companies include Apple, Microsoft, Google, Amazon, Coca-Cola and McDonald's. Its biggest trading partners include Canada, Mexico, the European Union and China. Its currency, the US dollar, is the money most used around the world for trade and savings, and New York, home of Wall Street, is one of the world's leading financial centres.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for cities such as New York, Los Angeles, Las Vegas and San Francisco, the theme parks of Florida and California, and national parks like Yellowstone, the world's first national park, and Yosemite. UNESCO World Heritage sites range from the Everglades wetlands to the ancient cliff dwellings of Mesa Verde. Festivals such as Mardi Gras in New Orleans and regional food like Texan barbecue are big draws.",
    history: "Native American peoples lived across the land for thousands of years before Europeans arrived. Thirteen colonies on the Atlantic coast declared independence from Britain in 1776 and won it in a war that ended in 1783. The nation expanded west to the Pacific and fought a civil war from 1861 to 1865 that ended slavery. After the Second World War it became a superpower, locked in a Cold War with the Soviet Union until 1991."
  },
  "Argentina": {
    known: "Argentina is famous for its beef, grilled over hot embers at a family barbecue called an asado, for its Malbec red wine and for its passion for football, with World Cup wins and legendary players such as Diego Maradona. The gaucho, the horse-riding cowboy of the grassy Pampas, is a national symbol.",
    economy: "Argentina has one of the largest economies in Latin America. Services make up the biggest share, but farming and food processing earn most of its money abroad: the fertile Pampas grow soybeans, maize and wheat and raise cattle, and the country is a leading exporter of soybean meal and oil. It also has huge shale oil and gas reserves at Vaca Muerta and large lithium deposits in the north-west.",
    business: "Main exports include soybean meal and oil, maize, wheat, beef, cars and trucks, and crude oil. Brazil and China are its biggest trading partners, along with the United States and the European Union. The currency is the Argentine peso, and Argentina is a founding member of Mercosur, a South American trade bloc. Well-known firms include the oil company YPF and the online marketplace Mercado Libre, founded in Buenos Aires.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Iguazú Falls on the border with Brazil, the Perito Moreno Glacier in the far south, the whales of Península Valdés and the vineyards of Mendoza. The national parks of Iguazú and Los Glaciares, home to the glacier, and Península Valdés are all UNESCO World Heritage sites. Many also travel to Ushuaia, one of the world's southernmost cities, and eat steak at a traditional grill house, a parrilla.",
    history: "Part of the north-west once belonged to the Inca Empire. Argentina declared independence from Spain in 1816, and José de San Martín became the hero of the wars that followed. Millions of Europeans, mostly Italians and Spaniards, settled from about 1870 to 1930. Later came Juan and Eva Perón, military rule from 1976 to 1983 and the 1982 war with Britain over the Falkland Islands, which Argentina calls the Malvinas and claims."
  },
  "Bolivia": {
    known: "Bolivia is known for its strong Indigenous cultures, especially those of the Quechua and Aymara peoples, whose languages are official alongside Spanish. Aymara women in bowler hats and wide layered skirts are a famous sight around La Paz. The country shares Lake Titicaca with Peru and, although it has no coast, keeps a navy that patrols the lake and its rivers.",
    economy: "Bolivia's economy is based mainly on mining, natural gas and farming. It produces zinc, silver, tin, lead and gold, and for years natural gas sold to Brazil and Argentina was its biggest earner, though output has since fallen. Soybeans grow in the eastern lowlands, and potatoes and quinoa in the highlands. Beneath its salt flats lie some of the world's largest lithium resources, still little used.",
    business: "Its main exports are gold, zinc, silver, natural gas and soybean products, and neighbours such as Brazil and Argentina have long been major trading partners. The currency is the boliviano. Bolivia belongs to both the Andean Community and Mercosur, and Santa Cruz de la Sierra, its largest city, is the main centre of business and large-scale farming.",
    tourism: "Beyond the salt flat, visitors come for Lake Titicaca and its Island of the Sun, sacred to the Incas, and for three of the country's UNESCO World Heritage sites: the ancient ruins of Tiwanaku and the colonial cities of Potosí and Sucre. The Oruro Carnival, with its devil dances, is on UNESCO's list of living heritage, and in La Paz cable cars serve as everyday public transport.",
    history: "The Tiwanaku civilisation flourished near Lake Titicaca long before the Incas arrived. Under Spain, silver made Potosí one of the world's richest cities. Bolivia won independence from Spain in 1825 and took its name from Simón Bolívar. It lost its coastline to Chile in the War of the Pacific and much of the Chaco to Paraguay in the 1930s. A 2009 constitution renamed it the Plurinational State of Bolivia, recognising its many Indigenous peoples."
  },
  "Brazil": {
    known: "Brazil is famous for football and legendary players such as Pelé, for samba music and the giant carnival of Rio de Janeiro, and for coffee, of which it is the world's largest producer. It is the only country in the Americas where Portuguese is the main language.",
    economy: "Brazil has the largest economy in Latin America. Services make up most of it, but it is also a farming superpower and a leading exporter of soybeans, sugar, coffee, orange juice, beef and chicken. It is a big producer of iron ore and of oil, much of it from deep-sea fields off the coast, and a large industrial base makes cars, steel, machinery and aircraft.",
    business: "Its main exports are soybeans, crude oil, iron ore, sugar, coffee and meat. China is its largest trading partner, with the United States and the European Union also important. Well-known companies include the oil giant Petrobras, the mining firm Vale and the aircraft maker Embraer. The currency is the real, and Brazil is a founding member of Mercosur and of the BRICS group, while São Paulo is South America's biggest business centre.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Rio de Janeiro's beaches, Sugarloaf Mountain and the Christ the Redeemer statue, and for the Iguaçu Falls on the border with Argentina. Other highlights are the Pantanal wetlands, one of the best places to see jaguars, and the colourful old town of Salvador, a UNESCO World Heritage site. Favourite foods include churrasco barbecue and feijoada, a stew of black beans and pork.",
    history: "Indigenous peoples lived in Brazil for thousands of years before Portuguese explorers arrived in 1500. As a colony it produced sugar and later gold using millions of enslaved Africans, and it was the last country in the Americas to abolish slavery, in 1888. Brazil became independent from Portugal in 1822 as an empire and turned into a republic in 1889. From 1964 to 1985 it was ruled by the military."
  },
  "Chile": {
    known: "Chile is the world's largest producer of copper and is known for its wines, its long chain of volcanoes and its frequent earthquakes, as well as for the giant stone moai statues of Easter Island in the Pacific. It has produced two Nobel Prize-winning poets, Gabriela Mistral and Pablo Neruda.",
    economy: "Chile's economy is built on mining, above all copper, which brings in around half of its export earnings, and it is one of the world's top producers of lithium. Services such as finance and retail make up most of the economy overall. Farming, forestry and fishing matter too: Chile exports cherries, grapes and other fruit, as well as wine, wood pulp and farmed salmon.",
    business: "Its main exports are copper, lithium, fruit, salmon, wine and wood pulp. China is by far its largest trading partner, followed by the United States, and Chile has free-trade agreements with dozens of countries. The currency is the Chilean peso. The state-owned Codelco is one of the world's biggest copper miners, and in 2010 Chile became the first South American country to join the OECD, a group of mostly wealthy countries.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Torres del Paine National Park, with its granite towers and glaciers, the lakes and volcanoes of the south and the wooden churches of Chiloé, a UNESCO World Heritage site. In the north, the Atacama's clear skies make it one of the best places on Earth for stargazing. Many also tour the wine valleys near Santiago and the colourful hillside port of Valparaíso, also on the UNESCO list.",
    history: "The Mapuche and other peoples lived here before the Spanish, and the Inca Empire reached into the north. Spain conquered much of Chile from 1541, when Santiago was founded, but the Mapuche held out in the south for over three centuries. Led by Bernardo O'Higgins, Chile declared independence from Spain in 1818. In 1960 it suffered the strongest earthquake ever recorded, and in 1973 a military coup began a dictatorship that lasted until 1990."
  },
  "Colombia": {
    known: "Colombia is named after Christopher Columbus and is the only South American country with coastlines on both the Pacific Ocean and the Caribbean Sea. It is known for the writer Gabriel García Márquez, whose novels blend everyday life with magic, for the painter Fernando Botero and his plump figures, and for lively music such as cumbia and vallenato.",
    economy: "Colombia's economy is mainly based on services, but oil and coal are its biggest exports, and its mines also produce gold and nickel. Farming remains important: besides coffee, the country grows bananas, sugar cane, avocados and flowers, and it is one of the world's largest exporters of cut flowers. Factories in cities such as Medellín and Cali make food products, chemicals, textiles and clothing.",
    business: "Its main exports are crude oil, coal, coffee, gold, flowers and bananas. The United States is its largest trading partner, followed by China. The currency is the Colombian peso. Colombia belongs to the Pacific Alliance and the Andean Community and joined the OECD, a group of mostly wealthy countries, in 2020. Well-known names include the Juan Valdez coffee brand and Avianca, founded in 1919 and one of the world's oldest airlines.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the walled old city of Cartagena on the Caribbean coast and for the Coffee Cultural Landscape, both UNESCO World Heritage sites. In the coffee region they tour farms and see the towering wax palms of the Cocora Valley. Others trek to the ancient Lost City in the Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta, relax on the beaches of Tayrona National Park or join the carnival of Barranquilla.",
    history: "Before the Spanish came, peoples such as the Muisca lived in the highlands; their gold offerings inspired the legend of El Dorado. Colombia declared independence from Spain in 1810 and secured it in 1819 under Simón Bolívar; Panama broke away in 1903. From the 1960s, decades of conflict involved guerrilla groups, paramilitaries and drug cartels, and in 2016 a peace deal was signed with the FARC, the largest guerrilla group."
  },
  "Ecuador": {
    known: "Ecuador is the world's biggest exporter of bananas and is famous for its roses and for the so-called Panama hat, which is actually woven in Ecuador from toquilla straw. Its volcano Chimborazo has the point on Earth's surface farthest from the planet's centre, because the Earth bulges at the equator.",
    economy: "Ecuador's economy relies heavily on oil and on exports from its farms and fisheries. Oil from the Amazon region is a major export and an important source of government income, and the country is also one of the world's largest exporters of shrimp, as well as selling bananas, cocoa, cut flowers and tuna. Services make up the largest share of the economy, and money sent home by emigrants helps many families.",
    business: "Its main exports are crude oil, shrimp, bananas, cocoa, flowers and canned tuna, and its biggest trading partners include the United States, China and the European Union. Since 2000 Ecuador has used the US dollar as its own currency. It belongs to the Andean Community, and the port city of Guayaquil is its business and trading capital.",
    tourism: "Beyond the Galápagos, visitors explore snow-capped Andean volcanoes such as Cotopaxi, the Amazon rainforest in the east and the colonial city of Cuenca, a UNESCO World Heritage site. The Otavalo market is famous for Indigenous crafts and textiles, and near Quito the Middle of the World monument was built to mark the equator.",
    history: "Many peoples lived in the region before the Incas conquered it, only decades before the Spanish arrived in the 1530s. In 1809 Quito made one of the first calls for independence in Spanish America, and Spanish rule ended with the Battle of Pichincha in 1822. Ecuador then joined Gran Colombia and became a separate country in 1830. It fought several border wars with Peru before a peace agreement in 1998."
  },
  "Guyana": {
    known: "Guyana is known for Kaieteur Falls, one of the most powerful single-drop waterfalls in the world, and for demerara sugar, named after its Demerara region. It has also become known for huge offshore oil fields, in production since 2019, that have rapidly transformed its economy. Culturally it belongs to the Caribbean, and its cricketers play for the West Indies.",
    economy: "For most of its history Guyana lived on sugar, rice, bauxite and gold, but its economy is now dominated by oil, pumped from fields off the coast by foreign companies led by ExxonMobil. Oil makes up most of its exports and has multiplied the size of the economy several times over. Rice farming, sugar and gold mining still employ many people.",
    business: "Crude oil is by far the main export and is sold to refineries in Europe, the Americas and Asia; gold, rice, bauxite and timber come next. The currency is the Guyanese dollar. Guyana is a founding member of CARICOM, the Caribbean Community, which has its headquarters in Georgetown, and the United States and its Caribbean neighbours have long been among its main trading partners.",
    tourism: "Most visitors come for wildlife and wild places: the Iwokrama rainforest and the Rupununi savannahs, home to giant anteaters, giant river otters and jaguars, and rivers holding the huge arapaima fish. Many stay in lodges run by Indigenous communities, and small planes fly over the forest to Kaieteur Falls. In Georgetown people browse the Stabroek Market and try pepperpot, the national dish, a meat stew flavoured with a sauce made from cassava.",
    history: "Indigenous peoples lived here before the Dutch set up plantations in the 1600s, worked by enslaved Africans. Britain took over in the early 1800s and, after slavery ended, brought workers from India, whose descendants are now the largest group. Guyana won independence from Britain in 1966 and became a republic in 1970. Venezuela claims the Essequibo region, most of Guyana's territory, and Guyana has taken the dispute to the International Court of Justice."
  },
  "Paraguay": {
    known: "Paraguay is known for the giant Itaipú Dam, which it shares with Brazil and which is one of the largest hydroelectric plants in the world. People there drink tereré, an ice-cold version of the herbal tea yerba mate, and the country is also famous for its delicate ñandutí lace, woven like a spider's web, and for the Paraguayan harp.",
    economy: "Paraguay's economy relies mainly on farming and on hydroelectric power. It is one of the world's leading exporters of soybeans and also sells beef, maize and rice, much of it grown in the east or raised on ranches in the dry Chaco. Dams shared with Brazil and Argentina supply almost all its electricity, and much of Paraguay's share of their power is sold to those neighbours.",
    business: "Its main exports are soybeans and soy products, beef, electricity, maize and rice. It trades mainly with its neighbours Brazil and Argentina and with China, which supplies many of its imports. The currency is the guaraní, named after the Guaraní people. Paraguay is a founding member of Mercosur, created by a treaty signed in Asunción in 1991, and the border city of Ciudad del Este is a busy trading hub.",
    tourism: "Visitors come to see the ruined Jesuit missions of Trinidad and Jesús, a UNESCO World Heritage site, and to explore the vast Chaco, with its wildlife and Mennonite farming towns. The Pantanal wetlands reach into the far north. Local food includes chipa, a cheesy bread, and sopa paraguaya, a firm cornbread despite a name that means Paraguayan soup.",
    history: "Guaraní peoples lived here before the Spanish founded Asunción in 1537, and Jesuit missionaries later gathered many Guaraní into mission towns. Paraguay declared independence from Spain in 1811. The War of the Triple Alliance against Brazil, Argentina and Uruguay, from 1864 to 1870, killed a large part of its population, but Paraguay won the Chaco War against Bolivia in the 1930s. A military dictatorship ruled from 1954 to 1989."
  },
  "Peru": {
    known: "Peru is known for the Nazca Lines, giant drawings scratched into the desert floor that are best seen from the air, and for the alpacas of the Andes, whose soft wool is prized worldwide. It is also the homeland of the potato, with thousands of local varieties, and much of its land is covered by the Amazon rainforest.",
    economy: "Peru's economy is driven by mining: it is one of the world's biggest producers of copper, silver and zinc and a major gold producer, and minerals make up most of its exports. Farming has boomed on the irrigated desert coast, which grows blueberries, avocados, grapes and asparagus for export. Fishing is big too, especially anchovies turned into fishmeal, though services are the largest part of the economy.",
    business: "Its main exports are copper, gold, zinc, fishmeal and fresh fruit. China is its largest trading partner, followed by the United States. The currency is the sol. Peru belongs to the Pacific Alliance and the Andean Community, and a deep-water port at Chancay, built with Chinese investment, opened in 2024 to speed up trade with Asia. Its most famous home-grown brand is Inca Kola, a bright yellow soft drink.",
    tourism: "Beyond Machu Picchu, visitors explore Cusco, the old Inca capital and a UNESCO World Heritage site, and the Sacred Valley nearby, and many hike the Inca Trail. Other highlights include the floating reed islands of the Uros people on Lake Titicaca, the deep Colca Canyon with its condors, the Amazon rainforest around Iquitos and Inti Raymi, Cusco's Inca sun festival held every June.",
    history: "Peru was home to some of the Americas' oldest civilisations, such as Caral, and cultures like the Nazca and Moche, long before the Incas built their empire. Spaniards led by Francisco Pizarro conquered the Incas in the 1530s, and Lima became the centre of Spanish rule in South America. Peru declared independence from Spain in 1821, secured in 1824. In the 1980s and 1990s it suffered a violent conflict with the Shining Path guerrillas."
  },
  "Suriname": {
    known: "Suriname is one of the most forested countries on Earth, with rainforest covering more than nine-tenths of its land. It is also known for its very mixed population, with roots in India, Africa, Indonesia, China and Europe as well as among Indigenous peoples, and in Paramaribo a mosque and a synagogue stand side by side.",
    economy: "Suriname's economy relies mainly on mining and oil. Gold is by far its biggest export, much of it dug by small-scale miners in the interior; oil is pumped on land, and big oil discoveries have been made off the coast since 2020. Bauxite mining, once the backbone of the economy, ended in the 2010s. Rice, bananas and fish are the main farm and sea products.",
    business: "Gold is the main export, followed by oil, timber, rice and fish. Important trading partners include the United States, China and the Netherlands, home to a large Surinamese community. The currency is the Surinamese dollar, which replaced the guilder in 2004. Suriname belongs to CARICOM, the Caribbean Community, and its best-known company is the state oil firm Staatsolie.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the Central Suriname Nature Reserve, a UNESCO World Heritage site of untouched rainforest, waterfalls and granite domes such as the Voltzberg, and for boat trips upriver to Maroon and Indigenous villages. Sea turtles nest on the beaches of Galibi, and the food mixes Indian, Javanese, Creole and Chinese cooking, with favourites such as roti and bami noodles.",
    history: "Indigenous peoples lived here first. The English set up plantations, and in 1667 the Dutch took over, while England kept New Amsterdam, now New York. Enslaved Africans worked the plantations; many escaped into the forest and formed Maroon communities that still exist. After slavery ended in 1863, workers came from India and Java. Suriname became independent from the Netherlands in 1975, and a 1980 military coup led to years of army rule."
  },
  "Uruguay": {
    known: "Uruguay is known for its cattle, which outnumber people by about three to one, and for its love of mate, a herbal tea sipped from a gourd through a metal straw. It is also famous for candombe, the drum music of its Afro-Uruguayan community, and for producing the great majority of its electricity from renewable sources such as wind and water.",
    economy: "Uruguay has a mainly services-based economy, with banking, trade, tourism and software important, but most of its exports come from farming and forestry. Cattle ranching, soybeans, dairy and rice are key, and large mills turn wood from its tree plantations into pulp for making paper. It has one of the highest incomes per person in Latin America and a large middle class.",
    business: "Its main exports are wood pulp, beef, soybeans, dairy products and rice. China and Brazil are its main trading partners, along with Argentina and the United States. The currency is the Uruguayan peso. Uruguay is a founding member of Mercosur, which has its headquarters in Montevideo, and its free-trade zones and banks make it a regional business hub.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for the beaches of Punta del Este, a fashionable seaside resort, and for the cobbled old town of Colonia del Sacramento, a UNESCO World Heritage site across the river from Buenos Aires. Others stroll along Montevideo's long seafront promenade, the Rambla, enjoy its carnival, which lasts more than a month, or taste wines made from Tannat, the country's signature red grape.",
    history: "The Charrúa and other peoples lived here before Europeans arrived, and Spain and Portugal fought over the land for about a century. José Gervasio Artigas led the early struggle for independence and is the national hero. After years of Portuguese and then Brazilian rule, Uruguay became independent in 1828, following a war between Brazil and Argentina. It built a pioneering welfare state in the early 1900s, and military rule lasted from 1973 to 1985."
  },
  "Venezuela": {
    known: "Venezuela has the largest proven oil reserves in the world and is the birthplace of Simón Bolívar, the hero who led much of South America to independence from Spain. It is also known for baseball, its favourite sport, which has sent many players to the North American major leagues, and for arepas, round cornmeal breads that are a daily staple.",
    economy: "Venezuela's economy is based on oil, which has long provided almost all of its export earnings, and the state company PDVSA dominates the industry. Production fell steeply from the 2010s after years of low investment, a crash in oil prices and later US sanctions, and the economy shrank dramatically. It also has iron ore, bauxite and gold, but farming and manufacturing are small, and it has long imported much of its food.",
    business: "Crude oil and oil products make up the great majority of exports. The United States was for decades its main customer, while China and India later became major buyers. The currency is the bolívar, named after the independence hero, and in 1960 Venezuela helped found OPEC, the group of oil-exporting countries. Its best-known consumer brand is Harina P.A.N., a pre-cooked maize flour sold across the Americas.",
    tourism: "Visitors come for Canaima National Park, a UNESCO World Heritage site of flat-topped mountains called tepuis, and for Mount Roraima, a tepui on the borders with Brazil and Guyana. Others explore the coral islands of Los Roques in the Caribbean, the wildlife-rich Llanos plains and the Orinoco Delta, or the Andean city of Mérida, while the colonial town of Coro is also on the UNESCO list.",
    history: "Columbus reached the coast in 1498, and explorers named it Venezuela, little Venice, after seeing Indigenous houses on stilts over the water. Venezuela declared independence from Spain in 1811, won it under Bolívar in 1821 and left Gran Colombia in 1830. Oil found in the early 1900s transformed the country. Hugo Chávez led a socialist government from 1999 until his death in 2013, and a deep crisis from the mid-2010s drove millions to emigrate."
  },
  "Australia": {
    known: "Australia is known around the world for its sunny beaches and surf culture, its love of sport, especially cricket, rugby and Australian rules football, and landmarks such as the Sydney Opera House and the Great Barrier Reef, the largest coral reef system on Earth. It is also home to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, whose cultures are among the oldest living cultures in the world.",
    economy: "Australia has a mainly services-based economy, with most people working in areas such as health, education, finance and retail. Its exports, however, are dominated by mining: it is the world's largest exporter of iron ore and a leading supplier of coal, natural gas and gold. Farms produce wheat, beef, wool and wine, and teaching international students is also a big export earner.",
    business: "Australia's main exports are iron ore, coal, natural gas, gold, beef and wheat, and its biggest trading partner by far is China; Japan, the United States, South Korea and India are also important. Well-known Australian companies include the mining giant BHP, the airline Qantas and the online design tool Canva. The currency is the Australian dollar, Sydney is the main financial centre, and Australia belongs to APEC and the G20.",
    tourism: "Visitors snorkel and dive on the Great Barrier Reef, watch the sun set over Uluru, the giant red rock of the outback, and drive the Great Ocean Road past the sea stacks of the Twelve Apostles. UNESCO World Heritage sites include Kakadu National Park, the Tasmanian Wilderness and the Greater Blue Mountains. Sydney's New Year's Eve fireworks and the Australian Open tennis in Melbourne draw big crowds, and Barossa Valley wines are popular.",
    history: "Aboriginal peoples have lived in Australia for at least 50,000 years. In 1788 Britain founded a convict colony at Sydney, and British settlement spread across the continent, bringing disease, violence and loss of land to Aboriginal communities. Gold rushes in the 1850s drew many migrants, and in 1901 the six colonies united as the Commonwealth of Australia. A 1999 referendum on replacing the monarch with a president as head of state was rejected."
  },
  "Fiji": {
    known: "Fiji is known around the world as a holiday destination of palm-fringed beaches and island resorts, where visitors are welcomed with a cheerful bula, the Fijian hello. It is also a rugby powerhouse: its men's sevens team won Fiji's first ever Olympic medal, a gold, at the 2016 Rio Games, and won gold again in Tokyo. Kava, a drink made from the root of a pepper plant, is shared at ceremonies and gatherings.",
    economy: "Fiji has one of the more developed economies among the Pacific islands, and it is mainly services-based, with tourism the biggest source of income and jobs. Sugar cane was long the main crop and is still grown on thousands of small farms, alongside coconuts, kava and root crops such as taro and cassava. Fishing, forestry, gold mining and money sent home by Fijians working abroad also matter.",
    business: "Fiji's main exports include bottled water, sold worldwide under the Fiji Water brand, along with sugar, fish, timber, gold and kava. Key trading partners are Australia, New Zealand, the United States and China. The currency is the Fijian dollar, Fiji Airways links the islands to the world through Nadi, and Suva is a regional hub, home to the Pacific Islands Forum secretariat and the main campus of the University of the South Pacific.",
    tourism: "Most visitors come for sun, sea and diving: resorts line the Coral Coast of Viti Levu, the Mamanuca and Yasawa island groups offer lagoons and tiny islands, and colourful soft corals draw divers. The old port town of Levuka, Fiji's first colonial capital, is a UNESCO World Heritage site. Visitors can also watch the firewalkers of Beqa island stride across hot stones and taste lovo, a feast cooked in an earth oven.",
    history: "People first settled Fiji about 3,000 years ago, and the islands became a meeting point of Melanesian and Polynesian cultures. Fijian chiefs ceded the islands to Britain in 1874, and the British brought tens of thousands of contract workers from India to the sugar plantations; their descendants remain a large part of the population. Fiji became independent from Britain in 1970 and later faced coups in 1987, 2000 and 2006."
  },
  "Kiribati": {
    known: "Kiribati is known as a country that is almost all sea: its 32 coral atolls and one raised island, Banaba, add up to only a small area of land. Its Line Islands, including Kiritimati, or Christmas Island, the largest coral atoll by land area in the world, are among the first places on Earth to welcome each new year. The country's name is the local way of saying Gilberts, pronounced roughly Kiri-bass.",
    economy: "Kiribati's small economy depends mainly on the ocean and on outside support. The government's biggest income comes from selling licences to foreign fleets that catch tuna in its waters, while copra, or dried coconut meat, and fish are the main exports. Money sent home by sailors working on foreign ships supports many families, while foreign aid and a national savings fund built from past phosphate mining help pay for public services.",
    business: "Kiribati exports fish, copra and coconut oil, but imports most of its food, fuel and manufactured goods, much of it from Australia, Fiji and Asia. It uses the Australian dollar. With its neighbours in the Parties to the Nauru Agreement, it controls tuna fishing by selling foreign boats a limited number of fishing days, and Betio, at the western tip of Tarawa, is the main port.",
    tourism: "Kiribati is one of the least visited countries in the world, which appeals to travellers seeking quiet lagoons and untouched reefs. Anglers come to Kiritimati for its famous fly-fishing for bonefish, birdwatchers for its huge seabird colonies, and history lovers to see Second World War guns and bunkers on Tarawa. The remote Phoenix Islands Protected Area, a vast stretch of ocean and coral atolls, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.",
    history: "The islands were first settled by Micronesian seafarers. Britain made them a protectorate in 1892 and later ruled them with today's Tuvalu as the Gilbert and Ellice Islands Colony, while phosphate was mined on Banaba, whose people were resettled in Fiji. In 1943 Tarawa saw one of the bloodiest battles of the Pacific War, and Britain and the United States later tested nuclear weapons near Kiritimati. Kiribati became independent from Britain in 1979."
  },
  "Marshall Islands": {
    known: "The Marshall Islands are famous for the stick charts of their master navigators, frameworks of sticks and shells that showed how ocean swells bend around islands. Bikini Atoll gave its name to the bikini swimsuit in 1946, and Kwajalein, one of the largest coral atolls in the world, hosts a United States missile-testing base.",
    economy: "The Marshall Islands have a small economy that depends heavily on aid and payments from the United States under a treaty called the Compact of Free Association, including rent for the Kwajalein base. Fees from foreign tuna fleets bring in money, and copra, or dried coconut meat, is the main farm product. Many families also rely on fishing, small gardens and money from relatives living in the United States.",
    business: "The Marshall Islands have one of the world's biggest ship registries: thousands of foreign-owned ships sail under the Marshallese flag and pay registration fees. Exports are small, mainly fish, copra and coconut oil, and most food, fuel and goods are imported from the United States and Asia. The country uses the United States dollar and belongs to the Pacific Islands Forum and the Parties to the Nauru Agreement, which manage tuna fishing.",
    tourism: "Visitors are few, but divers and anglers come for clear lagoons, coral reefs and plenty of fish. Bikini Atoll is a UNESCO World Heritage site, where warships sunk during the nuclear tests lie on the lagoon floor, and outer atolls still hold wrecks and bunkers left by Japanese forces in the Second World War. In Majuro, visitors relax on Laura Beach and see stick charts and crafts at the Alele Museum.",
    history: "Micronesian navigators settled the atolls at least 2,000 years ago. The islands were named after John Marshall, a British sea captain who sailed past in 1788, and were later ruled by Germany and then Japan until American forces captured them in 1944. After decades as a United Nations trust territory run by the United States, the country became self-governing in 1979 and independent in 1986, in free association with the United States."
  },
  "Micronesia": {
    known: "Micronesia, formally the Federated States of Micronesia, is known for the giant stone money of Yap: huge stone discs, some taller than a person, quarried in Palau and carried across the sea, and still used in traditional exchanges. Divers know it for Chuuk Lagoon, where dozens of Japanese ships sunk in the Second World War rest on the seabed, one of the world's great wreck-diving sites.",
    economy: "Micronesia's economy rests largely on aid from the United States under the Compact of Free Association and on fees paid by foreign fleets to catch tuna in its huge ocean area. The government is the biggest employer, while many families live from fishing and from farming breadfruit, taro, bananas and coconuts. Tourism is small, and many Micronesians work in the United States and send money home.",
    business: "Fish, mostly tuna, is by far the leading export, with small amounts of copra, kava and pepper; most goods are imported, many of them from the United States and Asia. The currency is the United States dollar. Micronesia belongs to the Pacific Islands Forum and the Parties to the Nauru Agreement, which manage tuna fishing, and Kolonia, the former capital near Palikir, is the busiest town on Pohnpei.",
    tourism: "Besides Chuuk's wrecks, divers come to Yap to swim with manta rays that live in its reef channels all year. Pohnpei offers the UNESCO-listed ruins of Nan Madol, jungle waterfalls and a famous surf break, while Kosrae has quiet reefs and the old stone city of Lelu. Yap Day, held around the start of March, celebrates traditional dance and crafts, and on Pohnpei visitors can try sakau, the local kava drink.",
    history: "People reached these islands more than 2,000 years ago, and each of the country's four states, Yap, Chuuk, Pohnpei and Kosrae, has its own languages. Spain ruled the islands until selling them to Germany in 1899; Japan took them in 1914 and fortified Chuuk, which American forces bombed heavily in 1944. After decades as a United States-run trust territory, Micronesia became self-governing in 1979 and independent in 1986."
  },
  "Nauru": {
    known: "Nauru is a single oval island, so small that one main road runs all the way round it. Its interior, called Topside, is a strange landscape of jagged limestone pinnacles left by nearly a century of phosphate mining. Since 2001 the island has also, at times, hosted an Australian-funded processing centre for asylum seekers who tried to reach Australia by boat.",
    economy: "After most of its phosphate was dug up, Nauru came to depend on outside income: fees from foreign tuna fleets, payments from Australia linked to the processing centre, foreign aid and a national trust fund meant to replace the lost mining earnings. Some leftover phosphate is still mined, but there is very little farming, so nearly all food is imported and much drinking water comes from desalination.",
    business: "Nauru's exports are small, mainly leftover phosphate, and almost everything else is imported, much of it from Australia. The currency is the Australian dollar. The Parties to the Nauru Agreement, a group of Pacific countries that manage tuna fishing together, is named after a deal signed on the island in 1982. In richer times Nauru's phosphate trust even built a Melbourne skyscraper, Nauru House, later sold to pay debts.",
    tourism: "Nauru sees very few tourists, but visitors can walk round the whole island in a day, swim at Anibare Bay and go deep-sea fishing for tuna and marlin. Inland they can visit Buada Lagoon, a quiet lake ringed by palms, the underground lake of the Moqua Caves, and Command Ridge, the highest point, where Japanese guns from the Second World War still stand.",
    history: "Nauru's twelve clans, remembered in the twelve-pointed star on its flag, had long lived there when a British whaler named it Pleasant Island in 1798. Germany took control in 1888, and after the First World War Australia ran the island; in the Second World War Japan occupied it and deported many Nauruans to Chuuk, where hundreds died. Nauru won independence from Australian rule in 1968 and took over the mines in 1970."
  },
  "New Zealand": {
    known: "New Zealand is known for the All Blacks rugby team and the haka, a Māori challenge dance they perform before matches, and for the kiwi, a flightless bird that gave New Zealanders their nickname. It is also famous for adventure sports, since commercial bungee jumping began near Queenstown, and for having several times more sheep than people.",
    economy: "New Zealand's economy is mainly services-based, with most people working in areas such as tourism, retail, health and finance, but farming earns much of its export income. Dairy products like milk powder, butter and cheese are the biggest goods export, with meat, timber, kiwifruit, wine and seafood also important. International students bring in money too, and most electricity comes from renewable sources, mainly hydro and geothermal power.",
    business: "New Zealand sells dairy products, meat, timber, fruit and wine, above all to China, Australia, the United States and Japan. Well-known companies include the dairy giant Fonterra, the kiwifruit marketer Zespri, Air New Zealand and the film-effects studio Weta Workshop. The currency is the New Zealand dollar, nicknamed the kiwi, and Auckland is the business hub. The country has a close free-trade deal with Australia and belongs to APEC.",
    tourism: "Visitors cruise Milford Sound in the fiords of the South Island, part of the Te Wāhipounamu World Heritage area, hike across the volcanoes of Tongariro National Park, another UNESCO site, and try skiing, rafting or jet-boating around Queenstown. Rotorua has bubbling geysers and Māori cultural evenings with hāngī feasts cooked in the ground, and the caves of Waitomo glow with thousands of tiny glowworms.",
    history: "Polynesian ancestors of the Māori arrived by canoe roughly 700 years ago, and in 1642 the Dutch explorer Abel Tasman became the first European to reach the islands. In 1840 Britain and many Māori chiefs signed the Treaty of Waitangi, whose English and Māori versions differ, and wars over land followed. In 1893 New Zealand became the first self-governing country to give women the vote, and it gradually gained full independence from Britain."
  },
  "Palau": {
    known: "Palau is known as a pioneer of ocean protection: in 2009 it created the world's first shark sanctuary, and in 2015 it passed a law setting aside most of its waters as a national marine sanctuary. Every visitor must sign the Palau Pledge, stamped into their passport, promising the children of Palau to protect the islands' nature and culture.",
    economy: "Palau's economy is built mainly on tourism, especially diving and snorkelling holidays, together with aid from the United States under the Compact of Free Association. The government is a large employer, and many families still grow taro and cassava and fish for food. Because nearly everything is imported and most visitors come from East Asia, the economy is easily hit by changes in travel.",
    business: "Palau exports very little, mostly fish, and imports most of its food, fuel and goods, mainly from the United States and Asia; carved wooden storyboards that tell local legends are a well-known craft sold to visitors. The currency is the United States dollar. Koror, the former capital and largest town, is the business centre, and Palau belongs to the Pacific Islands Forum and the Parties to the Nauru Agreement, which manage tuna fishing.",
    tourism: "Palau is one of the world's most famous diving destinations, with sharks and manta rays at sites such as Blue Corner and German Channel, and its Rock Islands Southern Lagoon is a UNESCO World Heritage site. Visitors also kayak to hidden beaches, coat themselves in white limestone mud at the Milky Way lagoon, see ancient stone monoliths at Badrulchau and explore Second World War relics on Peleliu.",
    history: "Palau has been inhabited for at least 3,000 years, with villages built around carved meeting houses called bai. Spain, Germany and Japan ruled in turn, and in 1944 American forces took Peleliu in one of the bloodiest battles of the Pacific War. After decades as a United States-run trust territory and a long debate over its nuclear-free constitution, Palau became independent in 1994 and moved its capital to Ngerulmud in 2006."
  },
  "Papua New Guinea": {
    known: "Papua New Guinea is known for its birds of paradise, one of which appears on the national flag, and for the colourful sing-sing gatherings of the Highlands, where groups dance in face paint and feathered headdresses. It shares the island of New Guinea, the second largest island in the world after Greenland, with Indonesia.",
    economy: "Papua New Guinea has two economies side by side. Mining and energy, above all gold, copper and liquefied natural gas, earn most of its export income, while most people live in villages and grow their own food, such as sweet potatoes, taro and bananas. Farmers also grow cash crops like coffee, cocoa, oil palm and coconuts, and the forests and seas supply timber and tuna.",
    business: "Papua New Guinea's main exports are liquefied natural gas, gold, copper, oil, palm oil, timber, coffee and cocoa, sold mostly to Australia and Asian countries such as China, Japan and Singapore. The currency is the kina, named after a pearl shell once used as money. The country belongs to APEC, whose leaders' meeting it hosted in Port Moresby in 2018, and to the Melanesian Spearhead Group and the Pacific Islands Forum.",
    tourism: "Visitors trek the Kokoda Track across the mountains, travel up the Sepik River to see spirit houses and wood carvings, and dive on colourful coral reefs. Highland festivals such as the Goroka Show bring together dancers from many communities, and a mumu, a feast of pork and vegetables cooked on hot stones in a pit, is a highlight. The Kuk Early Agricultural Site is on the UNESCO World Heritage list.",
    history: "People reached New Guinea at least 40,000 years ago, and its highlanders were among the few peoples to invent farming independently. In the 1880s Germany claimed the north and Britain the south, and both later came under Australian rule; in 1942 Australian troops fought Japanese forces along the Kokoda Track. Papua New Guinea became independent from Australia in 1975, and a long conflict on Bougainville ended with a peace agreement in 2001."
  },
  "Samoa": {
    known: "Samoa is known for fa'a Samoa, the Samoan way of life built around extended families, village chiefs called matai and the church. It is famous for its traditional tattooing, or tatau, a Polynesian word that gave English the word tattoo; the pe'a, a man's tattoo, stretches from the waist to the knees. Rugby is a passion, and the national team is called Manu Samoa.",
    economy: "Samoa's economy relies heavily on money sent home by the many Samoans living in New Zealand, Australia and the United States, and on tourism, with foreign aid also important. Most families grow part of their own food, such as taro, bananas, breadfruit and coconuts, and farming and fishing still employ many people.",
    business: "Samoa exports fish, coconut products, nonu juice made from a local fruit, taro and kava, and a well-known local brand is Vailima beer, brewed near Apia. New Zealand, Australia, American Samoa, China and the United States are important trading partners. The currency is the tala, Samoa joined the World Trade Organization in 2012, and in 2024 it became the first Pacific island nation to host the Commonwealth Heads of Government Meeting.",
    tourism: "Visitors swim in the To Sua Ocean Trench, a deep natural pool reached by a ladder, sleep in open-sided beach huts called fale, and explore the blowholes and lava fields of Savai'i. Evening fiafia shows feature the fire knife dance, and a Sunday umu feast brings taro, pork and palusami, taro leaves baked in coconut cream. The Teuila Festival in Apia celebrates Samoan culture each September.",
    history: "Samoa was settled about 3,000 years ago. In 1899 the islands were split: the east became American Samoa, while the west was ruled by Germany and, from 1914, by New Zealand, whose rule was challenged by the peaceful Mau independence movement. Western Samoa became independent from New Zealand in 1962, the first Pacific island country to do so in the twentieth century, and it changed its name to Samoa in 1997."
  },
  "Solomon Islands": {
    known: "The Solomon Islands are known for shell money, strings of tiny polished shell discs still made in Langa Langa Lagoon on Malaita and used in wedding exchanges and ceremonies, and for the artificial islands that families built from coral rocks in Malaita's lagoons. Some Solomon Islanders are born with naturally blond hair, caused by a gene variant different from the one found in Europeans.",
    economy: "The economy is mainly based on farming and fishing: most Solomon Islanders live in villages and grow much of their own food, such as sweet potatoes, taro, cassava and bananas, and fish on the reefs. For export income the country relies heavily on logging and on fishing, especially for tuna. Oil palm, cocoa and coconuts, sold as copra, are other cash crops, and foreign aid helps fund public services.",
    business: "Logs, mostly shipped to China, have long been the main export, along with fish, palm oil, cocoa and copra, and tuna is canned at Noro in the Western Province. Imports come largely from Australia, China and Southeast Asia. The currency is the Solomon Islands dollar, and the country belongs to the Pacific Islands Forum, the Melanesian Spearhead Group and the Parties to the Nauru Agreement, which manage tuna fishing.",
    tourism: "Beyond the wartime wrecks, visitors come for Marovo Lagoon, one of the largest saltwater lagoons in the world, with village guesthouses, reefs and fine wood carving. Divers head to Gizo, where nearby Kennedy Island recalls how the young John F. Kennedy survived after his boat was sunk in 1943. East Rennell, part of the world's largest raised coral atoll and home to a vast lake, is a UNESCO World Heritage site.",
    history: "The islands were settled in waves over thousands of years, and in 1568 the Spanish explorer Álvaro de Mendaña named them after the biblical King Solomon, imagining they held great riches. Britain made them a protectorate in the 1890s, and they became independent from Britain in 1978. From 1998 to 2003 violent unrest between groups from Guadalcanal and Malaita led to an Australian-led regional peacekeeping mission, which stayed until 2017."
  },
  "Tonga": {
    known: "Tonga is known as the Friendly Islands, a name given by Captain James Cook after the welcome he received in the 1770s. It is one of the few places where visitors can swim with humpback whales, which come to its warm waters to mate and give birth, and it made world headlines in 2022, when the undersea Hunga Tonga-Hunga Ha'apai volcano erupted in one of the most powerful blasts ever recorded.",
    economy: "Tonga's economy leans heavily on money sent home by the many Tongans living in New Zealand, Australia and the United States, which makes up a very large share of national income. At home, farming is the main activity: families grow yams, taro and cassava, while kava, vanilla and squash are grown for export. Tourism, fishing and foreign aid add to the mix.",
    business: "Tonga's exports are small: kava, vanilla, fish, root crops and squash, sold mainly to New Zealand, the United States and Australia; Tongan squash has also long been sold to Japan. Most food, fuel and manufactured goods are imported. The currency is the pa'anga, and Tonga belongs to the Pacific Islands Forum and the World Trade Organization; Nuku'alofa is its commercial centre and main port.",
    tourism: "Visitors sail among the sheltered islands of Vava'u, popular with yachts, and join whale-watching trips from July to October. On Tongatapu they visit the Ha'amonga 'a Maui, a giant stone gateway about 800 years old, the ancient royal tombs at Lapaha and the Houma blowholes. Sunday church singing and an umu feast, with dishes such as lu pulu, corned beef in taro leaves with coconut cream, are highlights.",
    history: "Tonga was settled about 3,000 years ago and was among the first islands in Polynesia to be inhabited. Later the Tu'i Tonga kings built a seafaring realm whose influence reached nearby islands such as Samoa and Fiji. King George Tupou I united the islands in 1845 and granted a constitution in 1875; Tonga was a British protected state from 1900 to 1970, and reforms in 2010 let voters elect most of parliament."
  },
  "Tuvalu": {
    known: "Tuvalu is known worldwide as a symbol of the danger of rising seas: its highest point is less than 5 metres above sea level. Its leaders have long campaigned for strong action on climate change, and in 2022 the country announced plans to become a digital nation, recreating its islands, culture and records online so they survive even if the land is lost.",
    economy: "Tuvalu has one of the world's smallest economies, resting mainly on the sea and outside help. The government lives largely on fees paid by foreign tuna fleets, a national trust fund set up in 1987 with Britain, Australia and New Zealand, and foreign aid, while seafarers on foreign ships send money home. Families grow coconuts, bananas and pulaka, a giant taro raised in dug-out pits, and fish in the lagoons.",
    business: "Tuvalu exports very little, mainly fish, and imports nearly all its food, fuel and goods; its closest trade and transport links are with Fiji, Australia and New Zealand. It uses the Australian dollar alongside its own coins, and Tuvaluan stamps are sold to collectors abroad. Tuvalu belongs to the Pacific Islands Forum and the Parties to the Nauru Agreement, which manage tuna fishing.",
    tourism: "Tuvalu is one of the least visited countries on Earth, which is part of its charm. Visitors snorkel and spot turtles and seabirds in the Funafuti Conservation Area, a protected stretch of lagoon and tiny islets, see relics of the Second World War, when American forces built the airfield, and watch the fatele, a lively traditional song and dance. Meals feature fresh fish, coconut crab and pulaka.",
    history: "Polynesian seafarers settled the islands long ago; the name Tuvalu means eight standing together, for the country's eight traditionally inhabited islands. As the Ellice Islands they came under British protection in 1892 and were ruled together with today's Kiribati until Tuvaluans voted to separate in 1974. Tuvalu became independent from Britain in 1978, and in 2023 it amended its constitution to declare that it will remain a state even if its land is lost."
  },
  "Vanuatu": {
    known: "Vanuatu is known for the land diving of Pentecost Island, where men leap from tall wooden towers with only vines tied to their ankles, a ritual that helped inspire modern bungee jumping. It is also famous for its strong kava, drunk at evening gathering places called nakamals, and for sand drawings, traced in one continuous line, which UNESCO recognises as an important living tradition.",
    economy: "Vanuatu's economy rests on farming and tourism. Most people live in rural villages, growing food for their families and cash crops such as coconuts for copra, kava, cocoa and coffee, and some raise cattle for beef. Tourism, from cruise ships and holidaymakers flying in from Australia and New Zealand, is a major source of foreign earnings, and foreign aid also matters. Cyclones and earthquakes often cause heavy damage.",
    business: "Kava has become one of the leading exports, alongside copra, coconut oil, beef, cocoa and timber, and the main trading partners include Australia, New Zealand, New Caledonia, Fiji and Asian countries. The currency is the vatu. Vanuatu is an offshore financial centre, belongs to both the Commonwealth and La Francophonie, and hosts the headquarters of the Melanesian Spearhead Group in Port Vila.",
    tourism: "Visitors dive the wreck of the SS President Coolidge, a huge wartime troopship off Espiritu Santo, swim in Santo's clear blue holes and relax on Champagne Beach. Chief Roi Mata's Domain, the home and burial site of a powerful chief who lived about 400 years ago, is a UNESCO World Heritage site. The national dish, lap lap, is a pudding of grated yam, taro or banana baked with coconut milk in leaves.",
    history: "Lapita seafarers settled the islands about 3,000 years ago, and James Cook named them the New Hebrides in 1774. In the 1800s many islanders were taken, often by force or trickery, to work on plantations in Australia and Fiji. From 1906 Britain and France ruled jointly, with separate British and French police, schools and courts, until independence from both in 1980, which came with a brief rebellion on Espiritu Santo."
  }
};
